{"meta": {"limit": 500, "next": null, "offset": 0, "previous": null, "total_count": 380}, "objects": [{"abstract": "   This document provides the problem statement and gap analysis of\n   agent-enabled mobile core network.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-08T13:34:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146885, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-agent-gap-network", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-agent-gap-network/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153640/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-09T07:09:47Z", "title": "Problem Statement and Gap Analysis for Agent-enabled Mobile Core Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agent communication requires intelligent task level coordination\n   to manage dynamic workloads across large-scale, heterogeneous\n   networking environments.  This draft proposes general requirements\n   for an agent protocol to enable autonomous task coordination at\n   scale, including dynamic task discovery, negotiation, and context-\n   aware scheduling with real-time adaptability.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-04T12:58:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146950, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-ai-agent-task", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-ai-agent-task/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153905/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165507/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-03T12:58:14Z", "title": "Task-oriented Coordination Requirements for AI Agent Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This specification defines a standard message and protocol for\n   communicating Agent Context information between AI agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-25T10:06:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 148563, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-agent-context-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-agent-context-protocol/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/154288/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-26T07:09:06Z", "title": "Agent Context Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines an \"agent_session\" extension for Virtualized\n   Conversations (vCon) that profiles the use of vCon parties, dialog,\n   analysis, and attachments to carry the record of an autonomous AI\n   agent's internal session - its prompts, tool invocations, tool\n   results, reasoning, and file or artifact provenance - alongside the\n   human-facing conversation that the agent participated in or acted\n   upon.\n\n   The extension is a Compatible vCon extension.  It introduces no new\n   top-level fields and does not alter the semantics of existing ones.\n   Instead, it specifies (a) how an autonomous agent is represented as a\n   vCon party, (b) how agent message turns are placed in the dialog\n   array, (c) how the internal agent trace (tool calls, results,\n   reasoning, system events) is carried as a structured analysis entry\n   whose body conforms to the Verifiable Agent Conversations (VAC) CDDL\n   schema [I-D.draft-birkholz-verifiable-agent-conversations], and (d)\n   how files and artifacts modified by the agent are carried as\n   attachments.\n\n   By projecting agent-session data into the vCon model, implementations\n   inherit vCon's party/identity model, the lawful basis framework\n   [I-D.draft-howe-vcon-lawful-basis], the lifecycle and redaction\n   machinery [I-D.draft-howe-vcon-lifecycle], and JWS-based signing,\n   without re-specifying any of those concerns.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-21T20:14:16Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156757, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-howe-vcon-agent-session", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-howe-vcon-agent-session/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163354/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-20T20:14:16Z", "title": "vCon Agent Session", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "This memo defines a standardized framework for extensible SNMP agents.  It defines processing entities called master agents and subagents, a protocol (AgentX) used to communicate between them, and the elements of procedure by which the extensible agent processes SNMP protocol messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": null, "expires": "1997-10-25T07:00:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1282/", "id": 18517, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-agentx-ext-pro", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 80, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-agentx-ext-pro/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2013-03-02T16:56:13Z", "title": "Agent Extensibility (AgentX) Protocol Version 1", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 18085}, {"abstract": "Newer high-speed public Internet access technologies call for a high- speed modem to have a local area network (LAN) attachment to one or more customer premise hosts.  It is advantageous to use the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) as defined in RFC 2131 to assign customer premise host IP addresses in this environment.  However, a number of security and scaling problems arise with such \"public\" DHCP use.  This document describes a new DHCP option to address these issues.  This option extends the set of DHCP options as defined in RFC 2132. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": null, "expires": "2001-01-14T08:00:11Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/988/", "id": 18871, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-agent-options", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-agent-options/", "rev": "11", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2013-03-02T16:57:12Z", "title": "DHCP Relay Agent Information Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4186}, {"abstract": "This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects managing SNMP agents that use the Agent Extensibility (AgentX) Protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": null, "expires": "2000-04-06T07:00:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1282/", "id": 19285, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ds/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-agentx-mib", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 20, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-agentx-mib/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ds/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2013-03-02T16:56:48Z", "title": "Definitions of Managed Objects for Extensible SNMP Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4224}, {"abstract": "This memo defines a standardized framework for extensible SNMP agents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": null, "expires": "2000-04-21T07:00:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1282/", "id": 21391, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ds/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-agentx-rfc-update", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 91, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-agentx-rfc-update/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ds/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2013-03-02T16:56:48Z", "title": "Agent Extensibility (AgentX) Protocol Version 1", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 20733}, {"abstract": "In some environments, a relay agent resides in a network element\r\nwhich also has access to one or more VPNs.  If one DHCP server wishes\r\nto offer service to DHCP clients on those different VPNs the DHCP\r\nserver needs to know the VPN on which each client resides.  The vpn-\r\nid sub-option of the relay-agent-information option is used by the\r\nrelay agent to tell the DHCP server the VPN for every DHCP request it\r\npasses on to the DHCP server, and is also used to properly forward\r\nany DHCP reply that the DHCP server sends back to the relay agent.", "ad": null, "expires": "2001-09-09T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 24032, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kinnear-agent-vpn-id", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kinnear-agent-vpn-id/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2001-03-08T08:00:00Z", "title": "VPN Identifier sub-option for Relay Agent Information Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1553}, {"abstract": "Since the inclusion of the Network Access Identifier (NAIs) into\r\nthe mobile ip fabric, home agents have had a way to identify mobile\r\nnodes which do not have home IP addresses.  After authenticating the\r\nregistration request from such a mobile node, the home agent is then\r\nexpected to assign a home addresses to the mobile node in the\r\nregistration reply to be used on a semi-permanent basis.\r\nUnfortunately, no specific mechanism has yet been proposed.  Ideally,\r\nas DHCP centralizes address management, a home agent should contact a\r\nDHCP server to allocate an address for the mobile node, thereby\r\npreserving DHCP as the central address maintainer.  The technology\r\ndoes exist for a Home Agent to use DHCP controlled addresses, namely\r\nfor the Home Agent to behave as a DHCP proxy agent.", "ad": null, "expires": "2001-09-03T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 22565, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-glass-mobileip-agent-dhcp-proxy", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-glass-mobileip-agent-dhcp-proxy/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2001-03-02T08:00:00Z", "title": "Mobile IP Agents as DHCP Proxies", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 9334}, {"abstract": "In RFC2131, the giaddr specifies both the subnet on which a DHCP\r\nclient resides as well as an IP address which can be used to\r\ncommunicate with the relay agent.  The subnet selection option allows\r\nthese functions of the giaddr to be split so that when one entity is\r\nperforming as a DHCP proxy, it can specify the subnet from which to\r\nallocate an IP address which is different from the IP address with\r\nwhich it desires to communicate with the DHCP server.", "ad": null, "expires": "2001-09-09T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 24033, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kinnear-agent-subnet-selection", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kinnear-agent-subnet-selection/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2001-03-08T08:00:00Z", "title": "Subnet Selection sub-option for Relay Agent Information Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1712}, {"abstract": "This document proposes a new sub-option to the DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) Relay Agent Information Option. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": null, "expires": "2002-05-16T07:00:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/988/", "id": 23406, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-agentoptions-device-class", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-agentoptions-device-class/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2013-03-02T16:57:23Z", "title": "The DOCSIS (Data-Over-Cable Service Interface Specifications) Device Class DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) Relay Agent Information Sub-option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1067}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a new DHCPv6 option and number of sub-options\r\n   for DHCP Relay Agent to facilitate Mobile IPv6 bootstrapping along\r\n   with a AAA infrastructure.", "ad": null, "expires": "2005-04-21T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 29962, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chowdhury-dhc-mip6-agentop", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chowdhury-dhc-mip6-agentop/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2004-10-18T07:00:00Z", "title": "DHCP Relay Agent Option to Support Mobile IPv6 bootstrapping", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2697}, {"abstract": "This document describes the link selection sub-option of the relay- agent-information option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4).  The giaddr specifies an IP address which determines both a subnet, and thereby a link on which a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) client resides as well as an IP address that can be used to communicate with the relay agent.  The subnet-selection option allows the functions of the giaddr to be split so that when one entity is performing as a DHCP proxy, it can specify the subnet/link from which to allocate an IP address, which is different from the IP address with which it desires to communicate with the DHCP server.  Analogous situations exist where the relay agent needs to specify the subnet/link on which a DHCP client resides, which is different from an IP address that can be used to communicate with the relay agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/7262/", "expires": "2003-05-01T07:00:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/988/", "id": 24598, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-agent-subnet-selection", "note": "2003-04-24: Published at RFC 3527", "notify": "<rdroms@cisco.com>", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-agent-subnet-selection/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2013-03-02T16:57:47Z", "title": "Link Selection sub-option for the Relay Agent Information Option for DHCPv4", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2203}, {"abstract": "This draft is part of a gladiator contest within the MIDCOM WG to \r\ndetermine what network topology information is needed at the Midcom agent. \r\nBy taking out application awareness from Middle Boxes in the \r\nnetworks, and keeping this application knowledge in the application \r\ndevices (the Midcom Agents); sufficient information needs to be put \r\nin the Midcom Agent to allow them to fulfill their responsibility. \r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2002-03-04T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 24807, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-aoun-midcom-agent-information", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-aoun-midcom-agent-information/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2001-08-31T07:00:00Z", "title": "Required Information in Midcom Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2225}, {"abstract": "   InfiniBand Architecture (IBA) specifies a high speed, channel based, \r\n   switched fabric architecture that delivers scalable performance in \r\n   data centers.\r\n        \r\n   This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) \r\n   for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.\r\n   In particular, it defines objects for managing InfiniBand Subnet \r\n   Management Agents (SMA).", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-03-17T08:00:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1539/", "id": 24985, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-ipoib-subnet-mgmt-agent-mib", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 70, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-ipoib-subnet-mgmt-agent-mib/", "rev": "08", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-09-13T07:00:00Z", "title": "Definitions of Managed Objects for the InfiniBand Subnet Management Agent (SMA)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 13053}, {"abstract": "This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) \r\nfor use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  \r\nIn particular, it defines objects for managing an InfiniBand Device \r\nManagement Agent (http://www.infinibandta.org)", "ad": null, "expires": "2002-05-11T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 25047, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-swortwood-mib-ib-device-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-swortwood-mib-ib-device-agent/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2001-11-07T08:00:00Z", "title": "InfiniBand Device Management Agent Management Information Base", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4476}, {"abstract": "The IEEE 802.1X protocol provides authenticated layer 2 network\r\naccess.  As part of the authentication for 802.1X, a device such as a\r\nswitch or a wireless LAN access point can receive credentials from\r\nthe authentication authority identifying the user of a device\r\nrequesting access.  These credentials can then be used by a DHCP\r\nserver in the selection of an IP address for assignment to the device\r\nthrough its DHCP client.  The 802.1X Credentials sub-option allows an\r\naccess device that implements 802.1X and that can create DHCP Relay\r\nAgent options to pass along credentials for the user of a device\r\nreceived during 802.1X authentication to a DHCP server.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2002-05-20T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 25206, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-droms-agentopt-8021x", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-droms-agentopt-8021x/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2001-11-16T08:00:00Z", "title": "802.1X Credentials Sub-option for the DHCP Relay Agent Information Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1559}, {"abstract": "The RADIUS Attributes suboption enables a network element to pass identification and authorization attributes received during RADIUS authentication to a DHCP server.  When the DHCP server receives a message from a relay agent containing a RADIUS Attributes suboption, it extracts the contents of the suboption and uses that information in selecting configuration parameters for the client. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/119947/", "expires": "2005-03-12T21:42:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/988/", "id": 25607, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-agentopt-radius", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-agentopt-radius/", "rev": "08", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2013-03-02T16:58:15Z", "title": "Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) Attributes Suboption for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Relay Agent Information Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1954}, {"abstract": "In many protocols, such as IP Paging [1] and PANA [2], there is the\r\nneed for the user to discover an agent in the network: In IP Paging,\r\nthe user first needs to discover the address of the Paging agent in\r\norder to perform Paging registration, Paging Area Update and other\r\npaging procedures. In the same way, in PANA, the user first needs to\r\ndiscover the PANA Authentication Agent to know where to send its\r\ncredential.  This document describes an efficient method for a user\r\nto discover one, or many agents in the network.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2002-08-26T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 25615, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-le-pana-agent-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-le-pana-agent-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2002-02-22T08:00:00Z", "title": "An efficient method to dicover an agent in the network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2616}, {"abstract": "This document describes the Secure Shell authentication agent protocol\r\n(i.e., the protocol used between a client requesting authentication and\r\nthe authentication agent).  This protocol usually runs in a machine-spe-\r\ncific local channel or over a forwarded authentication channel.  It is\r\nassumed that the channel is trusted, so no protection for the communica-\r\ntions channel is provided by this protocol.", "ad": null, "expires": "2004-08-05T07:00:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1259/", "id": 25743, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-secsh-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-secsh-agent/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2004-02-02T08:00:00Z", "title": "Secure Shell Authentication Agent Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3713}, {"abstract": "   InfiniBand Architecture (IBA) specifies a high speed, channel based, \r\n   switched fabric architecture that delivers scalable performance in \r\n   data centers.\r\n        \r\n   This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) \r\n   for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.\r\n   In particular, it defines objects for managing InfiniBand Baseboard \r\n   Management Agents (BMA).", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-03-17T08:00:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1539/", "id": 26737, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-ipoib-baseboard-mgmt-agent-mib", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 42, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-ipoib-baseboard-mgmt-agent-mib/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-09-13T07:00:00Z", "title": "Definitions of Managed Objects for the InfiniBand Baseboard Management Agent (BMA)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 7707}, {"abstract": "   InfiniBand Architecture (IBA) specifies a high speed, channel based, \r\n   switched fabric architecture that delivers scalable performance in \r\n   data centers.\r\n        \r\n   This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) \r\n   for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  \r\n   In particular, it defines objects for managing InfiniBand Performance \r\n   Management Agents (PMA).", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-03-17T08:00:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1539/", "id": 26800, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-ipoib-perf-mgmt-agent-mib", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 53, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-ipoib-perf-mgmt-agent-mib/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-09-13T07:00:00Z", "title": "Definitions of Managed Objects for the InfiniBand Performance Management Agent (PMA)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 10577}, {"abstract": "'DHCP Relay Agent Information Option' (RFC 3046) assumes that DHCP\r\nmessages exchanged between relay agents and servers are not subject\r\nto attack.  This document describes how IPsec can be used to protect\r\nmessages exchanged between relay agents and servers.", "ad": null, "expires": "2003-04-28T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 26805, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-droms-dhcp-relay-agent-ipsec", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 4, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-droms-dhcp-relay-agent-ipsec/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2002-10-25T07:00:00Z", "title": "Use of IPsec for Securing DHCPv4 Messages Exchanged Between Relay Agents and Servers", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 985}, {"abstract": "This document describes a new entity 'Fast Handover Agent (FHA)', \r\nwhich can be used for fast router discovery and configuration in \r\nFMIPv6. The FHA is deployed to provide the fast RA for Mobile Nodes\r\nentering a new IP network, without any modification of existing \r\nFMIPv6 procedures.", "ad": null, "expires": "2003-10-16T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 27568, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-park-fasthandover-agent-fmipv6", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-park-fasthandover-agent-fmipv6/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2003-04-14T07:00:00Z", "title": "Fast Handover Agent (FHA) for Fast Router Discovery in FMIPv6", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1217}, {"abstract": "The DHCP Relay Agent Information Option (RFC 3046) conveys\r\ninformation between a DHCP relay agent and a DHCP server.  This\r\nspecification defines two mechanisms for securing the messages\r\nexchanged between a relay agent and a server.  The first mechanism\r\ndefines a new authentication suboption for the Relay Agent\r\nInformation Option that supports source entity authentication and\r\ndata integrity for relayed DHCP messages.  The authentication\r\nsuboption contains a cryptographic signature in a payload derived\r\nfrom the option used in DHCP Authentication (RFC 3118).  The second\r\nmechanism uses IPsec (RFC 2041) to protect messages exchanged between\r\nrelay agents and servers.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/119947/", "expires": "2003-12-11T08:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/988/", "id": 27590, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-relay-agent-auth", "note": "This document was split into two documents that will be advanced separately.", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-relay-agent-auth/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/8/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2015-10-14T20:50:45Z", "title": "The Authentication Suboption for the DHCP Relay Agent Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 5197}, {"abstract": "   The DHCP Relay Agent Information Option (RFC 3046) conveys\r\n   information between a DHCP relay agent and a DHCP server.  This\r\n   specification defines a  mechanism for securing the messages\r\n   exchanged between a relay agent and a server using IPsec (RFC 2401)", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/119947/", "expires": "2005-11-27T22:31:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/988/", "id": 28205, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-relay-agent-ipsec", "note": "Document was withdrawn from consideration after discussion with author, WG chairs, IESG and WG.  There is already another mechanism to secure this communication defined in draft-ietf-dhc-auth-suboption.", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-relay-agent-ipsec/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/8/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2005-11-09T08:00:00Z", "title": "Authentication of DHCP Relay Agent Options Using IPsec", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2607}, {"abstract": "This document provides a survey of implementations of BGP-4 that support RFC 1657 MIB agents according to the BGP-4 v1 MIB specification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/103264/", "expires": "2005-05-15T23:35:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1041/", "id": 29020, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/inf/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-idr-bgp-mibagent-survey", "note": "", "notify": "shares@nexthop.com, yakov@juniper.net", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-idr-bgp-mibagent-survey/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/inf/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2013-03-02T16:58:29Z", "title": "BGP-4 MIB Implementation Survey", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 5279}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes a scheme to support reliability and load\r\n   balance among multiple Home Agents by extending Virtual Router\r\n   Redundancy Protocol for IPv6.  This solution could be transparent to\r\n   Mobile Node to some extent.", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-01-13T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31197, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-deng-mip6-vrrp-homeagent-reliability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 20, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-deng-mip6-vrrp-homeagent-reliability/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-07-12T07:00:00Z", "title": "Reliability and Load Balance among multiple Home Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4022}, {"abstract": "   This document is to propose a more deployable route optimization\r\n   scheme for Mobile IPv4, which is transparent to fixed IPv4 end nodes,\r\n   and does not need any preconfigured security associations.\r\n   Specifically, we propose to adapt the Mobile IPv6 Return Routability\r\n   Test for Mobile IPv4 route optimization, on the basis of an agent-\r\n   based architecture.  In order to achieve this, six new message types\r\n   and formats are defined for Mobile IPv4.", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-02-13T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31320, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-mobopts-agent-mip4rr", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-mobopts-agent-mip4rr/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-08-12T07:00:00Z", "title": "Agent-Based Return Routability Test for Mobile IPv4 Route Optimization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4953}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a new home agent option for the Dynamic Host \r\n   Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6). To assist with rapid \r\n   handovers the option allows a mobile node (DHCPv6 client) to acquire \r\n   a list of home agent addresses from a DHCPv6 server. ", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-04-17T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31533, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yuchi-dhc-homeagent-option", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yuchi-dhc-homeagent-option/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-10-14T07:00:00Z", "title": "Home Agent Configuration Option for DHCPv6", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1428}, {"abstract": "   This document modifies the Subnet selection option and defines a new \r\n   sub-option of the relay-agent-information option. Each of these \r\n   options is used by the relay agent to tell the DHCP Server to \r\n   allocation address from more than one address pool.  ", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-04-23T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31680, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zi-dhc-agent-request-multi-pool", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zi-dhc-agent-request-multi-pool/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-10-20T07:00:00Z", "title": "DHCP Relay agent Request from Multi Address Pool", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1195}, {"abstract": "   The DHCP Relay Agent Assignment Notification option is sent from a\r\n   DHCP server to a DHCP relay agent to inform the relay agent of IPv6\r\n   addresses that have been assigned or IPv6 prefixes that have been\r\n   delegated to DHCP clients.", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-05-12T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31794, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-droms-dhc-dhcpv6-agentopt-delegate", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-droms-dhc-dhcpv6-agentopt-delegate/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2005-11-08T08:00:00Z", "title": "DHCP Relay Agent Assignment Notification Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1746}, {"abstract": "This memo defines a new suboption of the DHCP relay agent information\r\noption that allows the DHCP relay to specify flags for the forwarded\r\npacket.  One flag is defined to indicate whether the DHCP relay\r\nreceived the packet via a unicast or broadcast packet.  This\r\ninformation may be used by the DHCP server to better serve clients\r\nbased on whether their request was originally broadcast or unicast.", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-12-10T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 32718, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kinnear-dhc-relay-agent-flags", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kinnear-dhc-relay-agent-flags/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2008-03-03T08:00:00Z", "title": "DHCPv4 Relay Agent Flags Suboption", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1850}, {"abstract": "This memo defines a new suboption of the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) relay agent information option that allows the DHCP relay to specify flags for the forwarded packet.  One flag is defined to indicate whether the DHCP relay received the packet via a unicast or broadcast packet.  This information may be used by the DHCP server to better serve clients based on whether their request was originally broadcast or unicast. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/21072/", "expires": "2007-11-02T21:49:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/988/", "id": 32719, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-relay-agent-flags", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-relay-agent-flags/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/108/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2015-10-14T20:51:31Z", "title": "The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol Version 4 (DHCPv4) Relay Agent Flags Suboption", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2052}, {"abstract": "This document presents a set of requirements for leaf nodes and root \r\nnode of any point-to-multipoint (P2MP) and multipoint-to-multipoint \r\n(MP2MP) Label Switched Paths (LSP) know each other dynamically \r\nwithout requiring a multicast routing protocol in connection-oriented\r\nservice provider core networks, especially in Multi-Protocol Label \r\nSwitching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks. \r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-12-21T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 32791, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zheng-mpls-p2mp-topology-agent-reqs", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zheng-mpls-p2mp-topology-agent-reqs/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2006-06-19T07:00:00Z", "title": "MPLS P2MP Topology Agent Requirements", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2202}, {"abstract": "This memo contains a mechanism by which NETCONF server and client can\r\nextended to operate in a master-agent sub-agent scheme.  It extends\r\nthe base NETCONF protocol with additional NETCONF operations,\r\ndescribes the protocol for this interaction and provides error\r\nmessages exchanged during this interaction.", "ad": null, "expires": "2007-04-05T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 33240, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kulkarni-netconf-subagent-prot", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kulkarni-netconf-subagent-prot/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2006-10-02T07:00:00Z", "title": "NETCONF Master-agent Sub-agent Communication Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2248}, {"abstract": "   Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol Proxy server is a DHCP server and\r\n   hence it supports DHCP protocol but it does not have local address\r\n   repository.  It outsources the address repository function to\r\n   external nodes or functional elements in a network.  This document\r\n   explains Proxy DHCP operation and presents some use cases.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2007-04-16T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 33304, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sarikaya-dhc-proxyagent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sarikaya-dhc-proxyagent/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2006-10-13T07:00:00Z", "title": "DHCP v4/v6 Proxy", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2356}, {"abstract": "   In Layer 2 Access Networks, Access Concentrators are present between\r\n   DHCP Clients and Relay Agent.  In this case, the Relay Agent can not\r\n   uniquely identify the end host and hence can not add unique 'Relay\r\n   Agent Information' option corresponding to the end hosts in DHCP\r\n   messages.  As the Access concentrators are closer to the end hosts,\r\n   they can uniquely identify the end hosts and add the Relay Agent\r\n   Information option in the DHCP message.  Access concentrators do not\r\n   set the 'giaddr' field.  Access Concentrators in this mode are\r\n   typically known as Layer 2 Relay agents.\r\n\r\n   This document provides insight to the behavior of the Access\r\n   Concentrators which act as DHCP Layer 2 Relay Agents in Access\r\n   Networks.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2007-12-17T08:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 33790, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-joshi-dhc-layer2-relay-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-joshi-dhc-layer2-relay-agent/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2007-06-15T07:00:00Z", "title": "Layer 2 Relay Agent", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 6121}, {"abstract": "This document describes an approach to support multicast listeners\nand senders located within a moving IPv6 network (NEMO). A NEMO\nis built up by at least one Mobile Router (MR) and a set of Mobile\nNetwork Nodes (MNNs).  The MR handles all routing related tasks to \nprovide connectivity between the MNNs and an access network\nincluding mobility management.  Correspondingly the MR also\nsubscribes to multicast groups and forwards emerging multicast\ntraffic on behalf of a MNN.\nFor optimised routing of multicast data a hierarchical multicast\nagent is introduced as a logical entity providing an anchor to the\nmulticast tree.  In the MR a corresponding functionality is defined\nwhich decides on the location of the specific agent to be used for a\ndistinct multicast traffic.\n\nConventions used in this document\nThe key words \"MUST\", \"MUST NOT\", \"REQUIRED\", \"SHALL\", \"SHALL NOT\",\n\"SHOULD\", \"SHOULD NOT\", \"RECOMMENDED\", \"MAY\", and \"OPTIONAL\" in this\ndocument are to be interpreted as described in RFC-2119 [1].", "ad": null, "expires": "2009-08-08T07:00:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 34776, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-von-hugo-multimob-agents", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-von-hugo-multimob-agents/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/2392/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/2622/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/6591/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/12187/"], "tags": [], "time": "2009-02-04T08:00:00Z", "title": "Agent-based multicast support for moving networks (NEMO)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3224}, {"abstract": "In some networks such as xDSL networks with WiFi extension, the\nperiodical transmission of Agent Advertisements (AA) by mobility \nagents is used by Mobile Nodes (MN) to detect movement. To allow fast\nmovement detection, the interval at which AAs are sent should not be\nlong. In the early deployment of Mobile IPv4 (MIPv4), mobility agents\nare deployed in the edge network. For example, in xDSL networks, Home\nAgents (HA) and Foreign Agents (FA) are located on or beside Edge\nRouters (ER) that usually serves thousands of MNs (typically between\n2000 and 5000 in xDSL networks). The periodical transmission of\nmulticast AA to MNs in such a large network consumes a significant\namount of the aggregation network bandwidth and CPU resources of ERs.\n\n\n\nThis is a practical problem in xDSL networks with WiFi extension. \nThis is also a common problem for others access networks in which a \nlarge layer-2 network is served by one router.  Hence a Mobility \nAgent Discovery proxy (MADP) can be set in access nodes to make the \nMNs detect movement fast meanwhile avoiding CPU and network bandwidth\nconsumption in the aggregation network. The MADP acts as a proxy to\nMNs as far as agent discovery is concerned allowing for AS issued by\nMNs to be locally replied according to AA issued by HA/FA on the\naccess network. MADP is a logical function that can be placed on a\nsuitable node location depending on access network technology and\narchitecture (i.e. WiMAX) to reduce network resource cost related to\nagent discovery.", "ad": null, "expires": "2009-04-30T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 36578, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yao-mip4-mobile-agent-proxy", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yao-mip4-mobile-agent-proxy/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/10008/"], "tags": [], "time": "2008-10-27T07:00:00Z", "title": "Mobile Agent Discovery Proxy (MADP) in IPv4 Mobility Management", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3122}, {"abstract": "Route Optimization is a very useful feature in Mobile IPv6. Mobile \nnode can communicate with correspondent node without the involvement \nof the home agent in Route Optimization mode. But there are some \nlimitations to this feature. One problem is that the mobile node and \nthe correspondent node must be capable for Route Optimization. This \ndocument introduces a Route Optimization Agent function used for \nRoute Optimization and this extension mechanism can enable Route \nOptimization mode to be used between mobile node and simple IP node. \nIn the extension solution, the Route Optimization Agent function may \nbe implemented in LMA or MAG and the Agent entity can reflect the  \nRO-related signal messages and accomplish the Route Optimization \nprocedure on behalf of the simple IP node. \n\n \n\n \n\nConventions used in this document \n\nThe key words \"MUST\", \"MUST NOT\", \"REQUIRED\", \"SHALL\", \"SHALL NOT\", \n\"SHOULD\", \"SHOULD NOT\", \"RECOMMENDED\", \"MAY\", and \"OPTIONAL\" in this \ndocument are to be interpreted as described in [RFC2119].", "ad": null, "expires": "2010-06-16T07:00:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 37988, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-netext-route-optimization-agent-ext", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-netext-route-optimization-agent-ext/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/18488/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/19157/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/20128/"], "tags": [], "time": "2009-12-13T08:00:00Z", "title": "Reflector Extension for Route Optimization Agent", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 6315}, {"abstract": "According to RFC 3046, there is only one DHCP Option 82 allowed in a\ncertain DHCP message.  This document describes several cases that\nneed more than one Relay Agent to add link information.  Proposed\nmethod is to have different Relay Agents add multiple DHCP Option 82.", "ad": null, "expires": "2010-04-22T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 38065, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zheng-dhc-relay-agent-stacking", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zheng-dhc-relay-agent-stacking/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/18706/"], "tags": [], "time": "2009-10-19T07:00:00Z", "title": "DHCP Relay Agent Stacking", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1566}, {"abstract": "RFC 3046 allows only the first Relay Agent to append Relay Agent\nInformation option.  In some networks, both Layer 2 Relay Agents and\nLayer 3 Relay Agents are deployed and their information is necessary\nfor DHCP packets forwarding and DHCP server's policy designing\ndescribed as [I-D.huang-dhc-relay-ps].  This document defines new\nsub-options for Relay Agent Information option (option 82) and\ndescribes how multiple relay agents add their information into option\n82.", "ad": null, "expires": "2010-09-05T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 38076, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-huang-dhc-multiple-relay-agents-option", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-huang-dhc-multiple-relay-agents-option/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/22169/"], "tags": [], "time": "2010-03-04T08:00:00Z", "title": "DHCP option including Multiple Relay Agents' Information", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1189}, {"abstract": "This document defines procedures for how a SIP User Agent should locate, retrieve, and maintain current configuration information from a Configuration Service.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/103539/", "expires": "2010-12-26T04:35:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1683/", "id": 38770, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/inf/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-lawrence-sipforum-user-agent-config", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-lawrence-sipforum-user-agent-config/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/108/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/inf/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/22821/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/23247/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/24293/"], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2020-01-21T08:30:55Z", "title": "Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) User Agent Configuration", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 8804}, {"abstract": "This document defines a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)\nRelay Agent Configuration option and associated machinery to\nconfigure the Relay Agent.", "ad": null, "expires": "2011-04-03T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 39519, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-asati-dhc-relay-agent-config", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-asati-dhc-relay-agent-config/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/26743/"], "tags": [], "time": "2010-09-30T07:00:00Z", "title": "DHCP Relay Agent Configuration Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2040}, {"abstract": "   This memo registers User-Agent and X-Mailer header fields in the\n   Permanent Message Header Field Names IANA registry.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2014-04-24T16:13:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 52279, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-melnikov-email-user-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-melnikov-email-user-agent/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/55172/"], "tags": [], "time": "2014-04-24T07:07:50Z", "title": "User-Agent and X-Mailer Header Field registration for Email", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1167}, {"abstract": "   This specification documents an extension to the Diameter Overload\n   Control (DOC) base solution.  The extension addresses the handling of\n   occurrances of overload of a Diameter agent.\n\nRequirements", "ad": null, "expires": "2015-05-14T07:04:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 52324, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-donovan-dime-agent-overload", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-donovan-dime-agent-overload/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/55357/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/58055/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/62544/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/64639/"], "tags": [], "time": "2014-11-10T08:04:08Z", "title": "Diameter Agent Overload", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 5377}, {"abstract": "   The Diameter Overload Information Conveyance (DOIC) solution\r\n   describes a mechanism for exchanging information about Diameter\r\n   Overload among Diameter nodes.  A DOIC node is a Diameter node that\r\n   acts as either a reporting node are a reacting node.  A reporting\r\n   node originates overload reports, requesting reacting nodes to reduce\r\n   the amount of traffic sent.  DOIC allows Diameter agents to act as\r\n   reporting nodes, reacting nodes, or both, but does not describe agent\r\n   behavior.  This document explores several use cases for agents to\r\n   participate in overload control, and makes recommendations for\r\n   certain agent behaviors to be added to DOIC.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2015-01-04T17:17:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 55666, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-donovan-doic-agent-cases", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 34, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-donovan-doic-agent-cases/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/61167/"], "tags": [], "time": "2015-01-04T08:09:35Z", "title": "Analysis of Agent Use Cases for Diameter Overload Information Conveyance (DOIC)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 9305}, {"abstract": "This specification documents an extension to the Diameter Overload Indication Conveyance (DOIC), a base solution for Diameter overload defined in RFC 7683.  The extension defines the Peer Overload report type.  The initial use case for the peer report is the handling of occurrences of overload of a Diameter Agent.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/118100/", "expires": "2017-09-23T20:31:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1682/", "id": 58934, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dime-agent-overload", "note": "", "notify": "\"Jouni Korhonen\" <jouni.nospam@gmail.com>", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dime-agent-overload/", "rev": "11", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/jouni.nospam@gmail.com/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/108/", "/api/v1/doc/state/115/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/", "/api/v1/doc/state/44/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/65442/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/67286/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/72839/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/71819/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/76705/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/78234/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/78887/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/83043/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/83070/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/84145/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/84776/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/85602/"], "tags": [], "time": "2019-08-15T20:08:58Z", "title": "Diameter Agent Overload and the Peer Overload Report", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 5259}, {"abstract": "   This document describes an Application Data Model (ADM) for an\n   Asynchronous Management Protocol (AMP) Agent.  The AMP Agent\n   represents a managed device in the Asynchronous Management\n   Architecture.  This document is in compliance with the template\n   provided by [I-D.birrane-dtn-adm].\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2019-09-12T20:22:48Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 63108, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-birrane-dtn-adm-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-birrane-dtn-adm-agent/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/71643/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/77013/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/79100/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/96360/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/96794/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/102469/"], "tags": [], "time": "2019-09-12T07:23:51Z", "title": "Asynchronous Management Protocol Agent Application Data Model", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 8799}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies agent identity management in the Internet of\n   Agents (IOA) system.  It defines the descriptive requirements for\n   agent identities, the agent registration process, the structure and\n   assignment of agent identifiers, and the basic and extended identity\n   management functions performed by the agent gateway based on the\n   agent's descriptive information.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-07T09:24:50Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151158, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zheng-dispatch-agent-identity-management", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zheng-dispatch-agent-identity-management/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157179/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-07T07:09:10Z", "title": "Agent Identity Managenment", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a key agent protocol for use in the Secure\n   Shell (SSH) protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-04-21T01:36:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2430/", "id": 66396, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-miller-ssh-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-miller-ssh-agent/", "rev": "17", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/36/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/78294/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/89719/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/89721/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/105821/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/108648/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/146045/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/136253/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/136243/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/136077/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/136245/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/136079/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/136099/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/136100/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/140457/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/140375/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/141963/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/146500/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/146501/"], "tags": [], "time": "2024-11-07T12:54:32Z", "title": "SSH Agent Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4241}, {"abstract": "   This document focuses on the use cases and requirements of\n   communication protocols for troubleshooting agents on network\n   devices.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-07T01:48:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151117, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-rtgwg-ai-agents-troubleshooting", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-rtgwg-ai-agents-troubleshooting/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157151/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-06T07:09:16Z", "title": "Use Cases and Requirements of Communication Protocol for Troubleshooting Agents on Network Devices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document introduces agents networking framework and defines the\n   core components of agent networking in enterprise and broadband, as\n   well as typical interactions among these key components.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T09:03:16Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150908, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zl-agents-networking-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zl-agents-networking-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157071/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-11-02T09:03:16Z", "title": "Agents Networking Framework for Enterprise and Broadband", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents increasingly act on the Internet on behalf of people\n   and organizations: they call APIs, browse websites, and delegate work\n   to other agents.  Protocols such as A2A and MCP define how agents\n   communicate, but there is no interoperable way to answer a prior\n   question: _which agent is this, and how can its claims be verified?_\n\n   This document defines the agent URI scheme as a stable, human-\n   readable identifier for an agent, a deterministic mapping from that\n   identifier to an HTTPS-hosted Agent Identity Document, anchoring of\n   the agent's keys to a web origin or a Decentralized Identifier, and\n   signed statements that bind the agent to its protocol metadata.  It\n   also defines profiles that let an agent prove its identity on HTTP\n   requests, building on HTTP Message Signatures for automated traffic,\n   and that let short-lived agent instances act under the agent's\n   identity using the WIMSE Workload Identity Token format.  It\n   deliberately does not define how agents communicate, describe their\n   capabilities, or are invoked.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-23T14:58:35Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 145784, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-narvaneni-agent-uri", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 43, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-narvaneni-agent-uri/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/151445/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/151449/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156172/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162318/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170622/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-20T14:58:35Z", "title": "The agent URI Scheme and Verifiable Agent Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes a framework for dynamic, intent-based\n   authorization for AI Agents.  The primary goal is to enable fine-\n   grained, Just-in-Time (JIT) permissions based on an agent's specific\n   intent and behavioral trustworthiness, rather than a long-lived\n   identity or role, achieve decoupling of authorization policies from\n   business operations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-08-18T07:42:07Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153664, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-agent-decoupled-authorization-model", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-agent-decoupled-authorization-model/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159687/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-18T07:09:03Z", "title": "A Decoupled Authorization Model for Agent2Agent", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic AI systems require robust, verifiable identities to operate\n   securely.  While traditional certificate enrollment protocols like\n   SCEP and EST can be extended to include remote attestation evidence,\n   performing a full validation for every agent's certificate request\n   presents significant scalability and privacy challenges.  This\n   document proposes two distinct, scalable models for integrating\n   attestation into the enrollment lifecycle.  The first model leverages\n   Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKP) to provide private, efficient, and\n   continuous attestation.  The second model uses a one-time, high-\n   assurance bootstrapping process to establish a trusted host\n   environment, which is then authorized to endorse certificate requests\n   for the agents it runs.  Both models enable high-assurance identity\n   for AI agents while addressing the performance bottlenecks of naive\n   per-enrollment verification.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-12-16T06:24:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146471, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-huang-acme-scalable-agent-enrollment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-huang-acme-scalable-agent-enrollment/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/152750/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-12-16T07:09:03Z", "title": "Extending Certificate Enrollment Protocols for Scalable Agentic AI Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes an AI agent architecture for Network Digital\n   Twin (NDT) that enables collaborative reasoning, simulation-assisted\n   decision-making, and closed-loop network operations.\n\n   The architecture integrates specialized AI agents into the core NDT\n   components and introduces mechanisms for twin validity assessment,\n   evidence-based decision-making, and policy-controlled execution.\n\n   It further describes multi-agent coordination, simulation evidence\n   exchange, and feedback-driven model evolution to improve the\n   reliability and adaptability of AI-assisted network management.\n\n   The document also identifies research challenges and potential\n   interoperability requirements for integrating AI agents with NDT-\n   based network management systems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T14:38:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153985, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhao-nmrg-ai-agent-for-ndt", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhao-nmrg-ai-agent-for-ndt/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160614/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171905/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T14:38:53Z", "title": "AI Agent Architecture for Network Digital Twin", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI Agent can do some tasks as an assistant to human beings.  During\n   the task process, the Agent may need to connect to other Agents with\n   different skills relative to the task.  The Agent to Agent\n   communication is a new kind of traffic for Internet, and some new\n   requirements for networking are proposed.  This document talks about\n   the requirements and key issues of global AI agent communication\n   towards 6G.  Some 6G related use cases from 3GPP documents are\n   introduced.  After that, the related requirements for the AI Agent\n   Communication Network (ACN) are proposed, and potential ACN\n   frameworks and standardization works for the AI Agent Communication\n   are also discussed.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T01:22:59Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146719, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-du-ai-agent-communication-6g-aspect", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-du-ai-agent-communication-6g-aspect/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153143/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171005/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T01:22:59Z", "title": "Use Cases and Requirements of AI Agent Communication from 6G Aspect", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents are rapidly and massively transitioning from cutting-edge\n   technology into real life.  The AI agent communication protocol will\n   establishing a critical means to connect agents with different users,\n   tools, and other agents.  Among all the features of AI agent\n   communication protocol, digital identity is one of the most important\n   components.  Developing a cross-industry, universal, flexible,\n   interoperable, and secure AI agent digital identity protocol is the\n   foundation for achieving communication between agents and other\n   entities in future network.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-02T09:15:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146729, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yl-agent-id-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yl-agent-id-requirements/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153166/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-03T07:09:05Z", "title": "Digital Identity Management for AI Agent Communication Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   In this draft, we address to potential adapt authorization frameworks\n   for the future AI agent.  An extension is proposed in the OAuth 2.0\n   protocol with an optional field *target_id* for granular\n   authorization.  It can be seen useful for the future virtual/physical\n   AI agents.  By explicitly identifying the target during\n   authorization, the draft aims to support precise permission\n   management and enhance traceability.  Serveral risks can be mitigated\n   through the proposed extension, such as potential unauthorized or\n   malicious behavior of AI components in the netowrk, while the\n   compatibility of existing OAuth 2.0 workflows is still maintained.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-05T09:47:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146806, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-song-oauth-ai-agent-authorization", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-song-oauth-ai-agent-authorization/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153362/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-06T07:09:06Z", "title": "OAuth2.0 Extention for AI Agent: Authorization on Target", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   In the push toward Level 4 Autonomous Networks, CSPs must to adopt\n   new processes and organizational changes, along with function-centric\n   building blocks for higher autonomy.  As intelligent agents evolve,\n   network autonomy increasingly relies on deploying these agents,\n   enabling self-management, self-healing, and adaptation with minimal\n   human intervention.  These agents facilitate the transition toward\n   fully autonomous network operations across multiple layers, including\n   services and resources.  However, achieving Level 4 autonomy, where\n   networks independently handle tasks across various domains, presents\n   significant challenges, notably secure, efficient, and accurate\n   multi-agent interactions.  This document introduces a protocol-\n   agnostic data model that enables multiple intelligent agents to\n   communicate effectively using the model, ensuring end-to-end task\n   execution and closed-loop operations in autonomous networks.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-08T12:01:57Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146877, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wang-nmrg-magent-im", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 33, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wang-nmrg-magent-im/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153613/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-09T07:09:46Z", "title": "A Protocol-agnostic Multiple Agents Interaction Model for Autonomous Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This draft introduces use cases related to AI Agents in 6G networks,\n   primarily referencing the technical report of 3GPP SA1 R20 Study on\n   6G Use Cases and Service Requirements (TR 22.870).  It also\n   elaborates on some of the requirements for introducing AI Agents into\n   6G networks from the perspective of operators.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-07-09T07:59:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146886, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yu-ai-agent-use-cases-in-6g", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yu-ai-agent-use-cases-in-6g/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153644/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/153655/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158459/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-05T07:59:02Z", "title": "AI Agent Use Cases and Requirements in 6G Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Multimodal requirements for Agent-to-\n   Agent Protocol, which enables autonomous agents to establish multi-\n   channel communication sessions, negotiate heterogeneous data\n   capabilities (e.g., text, file, real-time audio/video streams, sensor\n   streams), and exchange synchronized multimodal content with adaptive\n   QoS policies.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-08T19:58:17Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146937, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-xie-ai-agent-multimodal", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-xie-ai-agent-multimodal/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153833/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-09T07:09:53Z", "title": "Multimodal Management Requirements for AI Agent Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent application will be surely the common trend for a long time in\n   future, while agent protocols are so popular that more and more\n   protocols are being worked out.  The telecommunication industry plays\n   a pivotal role in the Agent ecosystem.  The overall technology\n   success hinges on how telecommunication industry could adopt the\n   latest AI trends in order to handle its specific usage scenarios and\n   performance requirements, e.g., in the coming 6G era.  This document\n   will provide the first attempt to analyze the agent protocol\n   requirements and relevant enabling technologies based on mobile\n   communication system specific characteristics.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-21T03:42:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 147464, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hw-ai-agent-6g", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 20, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hw-ai-agent-6g/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/154031/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-21T07:09:02Z", "title": "Requirements and Enabling Technologies of Agent Protocols for 6G Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines how Selective Disclosure for JWTs (SD-JWT)\n   integrates with Agent-to-Agent (A2A) systems to provide privacy-\n   preserving agent discovery and cryptographically verifiable identity\n   management.  It specifies the SD-Card format - an SD-JWT encoding of\n   Agent Cards that enables selective disclosure of agent capabilities,\n   contact information, and operational metadata while maintaining\n   cryptographic integrity and preventing correlation across different\n   interaction contexts.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-03T20:53:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 148867, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nandakumar-agent-sd-jwt", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 30, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nandakumar-agent-sd-jwt/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/154387/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156464/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160786/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-03T07:09:30Z", "title": "SD Agent: Selective Disclosure for Agent Discovery and Identity Management", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a set of best practices for facilitating\n   interactions between automated software agents (AI agents) using the\n   Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).  As agentic systems proliferate,\n   they frequently utilize HTTP in ad-hoc ways, leading to\n   interoperability challenges and the rise of proprietary, \"walled\n   garden\" ecosystems.  This document proposes a minimalist,\n   standardized framework based on existing HTTP semantics to promote a\n   more cohesive, efficient, and open ecosystem.  The primary\n   recommendations codify the principles of the Agentic Hypercall\n   Protocol (AHP), including tool invocation via GET requests, a\n   stateless authentication scheme, and the formalization of the 402\n   (Payment Required) status code to enable a native economic layer for\n   the machine economy.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-02-09T01:33:16Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 148981, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-campbell-agentic-http", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-campbell-agentic-http/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/154530/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-02-09T07:09:01Z", "title": "A Best Current Practice for Agentic Interactions over HTTP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a Agent-to-Agent communication framework\n   enabling structured, low-latency, and semantically rich communication\n   between autonomous agents over the Media over QUIC (MoQ) protocol.\n   It leverages MoQ's efficient media transport capabilities while\n   introducing a new application-layer framing mechanism to support\n   control signaling, session management, and large data fragmentation.\n   The design supports both intra-domain and inter-domain deployment,\n   with an emphasis on interoperability, extensibility, and minimal\n   overhead.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-04T00:03:45Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154047, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-agent-protocol-over-moq", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-agent-protocol-over-moq/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160908/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-03T07:10:00Z", "title": "Agent Protocol over MoQ", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) specifications\n   are designed to make identity management in cloud-based applications\n   and services easier.\n\n   This document provides a platform-neutral schema for representing AI\n   agents' identities in JSON format, enabling them to be transferred in\n   the SCIM protocol to the service.  This establishes an agentic\n   identity so that an agent can subsequently be authenticated and\n   authorized to interact with the service.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-02-20T01:45:51Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 149227, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wahl-scim-agent-schema", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wahl-scim-agent-schema/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/154668/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/154672/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-02-20T07:09:01Z", "title": "System for Cross-domain Identity Management: Agentic Identity Schema", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a key agent protocol for use in the Secure\n   Shell (SSH) protocol.\n\nNote\n\n   This note is to be removed before publishing as an RFC.\n\n   In the IANA considerations section, please replace \"thisRFC\" with\n   \"RFC\" and the assigned number, when known.\n", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/123703/", "expires": "2026-08-24T04:00:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2430/", "id": 141509, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-sshm-ssh-agent", "note": "", "notify": "job@sobornost.net", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-sshm-ssh-agent/", "rev": "16", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/job@sobornost.net/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/108/", "/api/v1/doc/state/154/", "/api/v1/doc/state/115/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/", "/api/v1/doc/state/44/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/154686/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/147405/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/152718/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/147773/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/150530/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/152660/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/154429/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155463/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155719/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156273/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156958/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157393/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157532/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157794/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158351/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157873/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159867/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-28T19:45:49Z", "title": "SSH Agent Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This specification extends the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework\n   [RFC6749] to enable AI agents to securely obtain access tokens for\n   acting on behalf of users.  It introduces the *requested_actor*\n   parameter in authorization requests to identify the specific agent\n   requiring delegation and the *actor_token* parameter in token\n   requests to authenticate the agent during the exchange of an\n   authorization code for an access token.  The flow can be initiated by\n   a resource server challenge, ensuring that user consent is obtained\n   dynamically when access is attempted.  This extension ensures secure\n   delegation with explicit user consent, streamlines the authorization\n   flow, and enhances auditability through access token claims that\n   document the delegation chain from the user to the agent via a client\n   application.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-02-27T03:24:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 145919, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-oauth-ai-agents-on-behalf-of-user", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-oauth-ai-agents-on-behalf-of-user/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/151737/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/151870/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/154818/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-02-27T07:09:03Z", "title": "OAuth 2.0 Extension: On-Behalf-Of User Authorization for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document presents system and Radio Access Network (RAN)\n   requirements for 3GPP Artificial Intelligence/ Machine Learning (AI/\n   ML) and AI Agents for the 6th generation mobile network, or 6G.\n   Requirements depend on the types and application areas of agents.  We\n   describe some of them defined with each type and state their\n   requirements.  RAN 6G requirements are discussed including AI related\n   requirements.  AI Agent implementation efforts, initiatives on how\n   APIs can be discovered, how inter-domain and intra domain AI Agents\n   can be discovered using DNS lookup and agent protocol design are\n   explained.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-14T15:29:33Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 149533, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sarischo-6gip-aiagent-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sarischo-6gip-aiagent-requirements/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155198/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170127/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-11T15:29:33Z", "title": "AI Agents 6G System and RAN Requirements and Implementation Approaches", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes an AI agent architecture for Digital Twin\n   Network (DTN) that integrates AI agents with digital twin technology.\n   The architecture extends the traditional DTN architecture by\n   incorporating autonomous AI agents at each component level, enabling\n   more intelligent and adaptive network management capabilities.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-23T14:09:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150294, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhao-nmrg-ai-agent-for-dtn", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhao-nmrg-ai-agent-for-dtn/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156663/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-10-20T14:09:13Z", "title": "AI Agent Architecture for DTN Digital Twin Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the framework of AI agent networks based on\n   Agent Network Protocol (ANP) protocol.  [ANP] It provides the basic\n   functions that needs to support AI agent communication in the AI\n   agent networks within the trust domain.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-23T16:12:33Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150298, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zyyhl-agent-networks-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zyyhl-agent-networks-framework/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156660/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156731/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-23T07:10:01Z", "title": "Framework for AI Agent Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the AGENTS.TXT protocol, a strict plaintext\n   policy file for automated clients, bots, and crawlers.  It defines\n   directives, top-line hash verification, optional parameters, and\n   mandatory failure behavior for malformed files.  Malformed files are\n   treated as fully restrictive to prevent unintended access.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-10T15:18:17Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 149905, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-srijal-agents-policy", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-srijal-agents-policy/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155793/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-10T07:09:02Z", "title": "AGENTS.TXT: Strict Policy File for Automated Clients", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes AgentDNS, a root domain naming and service\n   discovery system designed for Large Language Model (LLM) agents.\n   Inspired by the traditional Domain Name System (DNS), AgentDNS\n   enables agents to autonomously discover, resolve, and securely invoke\n   third-party agent and tool services across different vendors.\n   AgentDNS introduces a unified namespace, semantic service discovery,\n   protocol-aware interoperability, and unified authentication and\n   billing.  The system aims to address interoperability, service\n   discovery, and trust management challenges in large-scale agent\n   collaboration ecosystems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-12T02:15:50Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 149966, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liang-agentdns", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liang-agentdns/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155853/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-11T07:09:01Z", "title": "AgentDNS: A Root Domain Naming System for LLM Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) specification\n   [RFC7643] provides schemas that represent common identity information\n   about users and groups, as well as a protocol for communicating that\n   information between systems.\n\n   The systems that tend to implement SCIM clients and servers are\n   identity providers, and service providers.  These are the same\n   systems that are now need to manage agents and agentic applications\n   across domains.\n\n   This document describes a SCIM 2.0 extension for agents and agentic\n   applications, which includes extensions to the core User and Group\n   objects, and new resource types and schemas for agentic constructs.\n\n   This extension is intended to provide greater interoperability\n   between Identity providers, agentic applications, agents and their\n   clients while reducing the responsibilities assumed by the every\n   growing list of new protocols for agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-14T21:30:52Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150034, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-scim-agent-extension", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 27, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-scim-agent-extension/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155960/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-14T07:09:07Z", "title": "SCIM Agents and Agentic Applications Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Agentic Dispute Protocol (ADP), a\n   standardized framework for autonomous agents and AI systems to file,\n   process, and resolve disputes through structured, automated\n   processes.  The protocol defines message formats, transport\n   mechanisms, evidence submission standards, and cryptographic proof\n   requirements for internet-native dispute resolution.\n\n   ADP is designed to handle disputes arising from AI agent\n   interactions, service level agreement breaches, and automated\n   contract enforcement, providing deterministic, auditable, and legally\n   enforceable outcomes.\n\n   ADP includes chain of custody tracking, dual-format award\n   specifications (JSON and PDF), arbitrator discovery mechanisms, and\n   support for multiple resolution frameworks including expert\n   determination, binding arbitration, mediation, and hybrid approaches.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-17T05:33:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150072, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kotecha-agentic-dispute-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kotecha-agentic-dispute-protocol/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156025/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-16T07:09:09Z", "title": "Agentic Dispute Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes a standardized protocol for discovery and\n   invocation of AI agents.  It defines a common metadata format for\n   describing AI agents (including capabilities, I/O specifications,\n   supported languages, tags, authentication methods, etc.), a\n   capability-based discovery mechanism, and a unified RESTful\n   invocation interface.\n\n   This revision refines the discovery mechanism by defining fields for\n   intent-based agent selection.  This capability enables a client, host\n   agent, or orchestration system to describe a task intent and receive\n   a ranked set of candidate agents before invocation, without changing\n   existing discovery or invocation semantics.\n\n   The goal is to enable cross-platform interoperability among AI agents\n   by providing a discover-and-match mechanism and a unified invocation\n   entry point.  Security considerations, including authentication and\n   trust measures, are also discussed.  This specification aims to\n   facilitate the formation of multi-agent systems by making it easy to\n   find the right agent for a task and invoke it in a consistent manner\n   across different vendors and platforms.  Intent-based selection is an\n   application-layer capability and does not define network routing,\n   packet forwarding, path computation, reachability advertisement, or\n   address resolution.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T09:30:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150123, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-ai-agent-discovery-invocation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-ai-agent-discovery-invocation/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156082/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159616/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165840/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T09:30:21Z", "title": "AI Agent Discovery and Invocation Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document focuses on the ubiquitous access collaboration\n   scenarios, explores the requirements for AI agent communication\n   protocols.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-20T06:05:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150186, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-access-collaboration-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-access-collaboration-agent/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156275/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-19T07:09:15Z", "title": "Ubiquitous Access Collaboration Requirements for AI Agent Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) specification\n   [RFC7643] provides schemas that represent common identity information\n   about users and groups, as well as a protocol for communicating that\n   information between systems.\n\n   The systems that tend to implement SCIM clients and servers are\n   identity providers, and service providers.  These are the same\n   systems that are now need to manage agents and agentic applications\n   across domains.\n\n   This document describes a SCIM 2.0 extension for agents and agentic\n   applications, which includes extensions to the core User and Group\n   objects, and new resource types and schemas for agentic constructs.\n\n   This extension is intended to provide greater interoperability\n   between Identity providers, agentic applications, agents and their\n   clients while reducing the responsibilities assumed by the every\n   growing list of new protocols for agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-19T15:37:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150174, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-abbey-scim-agent-extension", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 27, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-abbey-scim-agent-extension/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156240/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-19T07:09:14Z", "title": "SCIM Agents and Agentic Applications Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The evolution from Level 3 (assisted automation) to Level 4 (closed-\n   loop autonomy) in Autonomous Networks (AN) introduces requirements\n   for agentic capabilities, including intent-based reasoning,\n   autonomous planning, and context-aware decision-making, and execution\n   coordination, which transcend the static, rule-based logic of\n   traditional network controllers.  This document defines the concept\n   of the Network Management Agent (NMA), a network management entity\n   with autonomous task processing capabilities designed to bridge the\n   gap between service intent and network operations.\n\n   This document describes the role of NMA in network management and\n   control architectures, and specifies how the NMA collaborates with\n   existing network controllers to achieve Autonomous L4 without\n   replacing or duplicating their functions.  It further defines the\n   reference architecture, deployment modes, and logical interactions of\n   the NMA, including the Northbound Task Interface (NTI), Agent-to-\n   Agent (A2A), Agent-to-Controller (A2C), and Agent-to-Network (A2N)\n   interactions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T12:42:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 139837, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhao-nmop-network-management-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhao-nmop-network-management-agent/", "rev": "06", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/146649/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/149677/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/153692/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156291/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160165/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165761/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171873/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T12:42:31Z", "title": "AI based Network Management Agent(NMA): Concepts and Architecture", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent Communication Network(ACN) is becoming a promising and\n   fundamental infrastructure for most vertical industries.  To\n   construct and build a scalable and trustable ACN, authentication and\n   authorization of AI agents are critical requirements.  This document\n   extends the model of OAuth and proposes new workflows for AI agent\n   authentication and authorization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-23T09:32:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146717, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yao-agent-auth-considerations", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yao-agent-auth-considerations/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153142/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156562/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-23T07:09:44Z", "title": "Further considerations on AI Agent Authentication and Authorization Based on OAuth Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the growth of optical network scale, the complexity of network\n   operation and maintenance has increased dramatically.  Enhancing the\n   intelligence level of optical network operation and management and\n   building high-level autonomous optical networks have become the\n   common vision of global operators.  The development of AI, especially\n   large AI model technologies, provides a feasible technical path for\n   realizing autonomous perception, decision-making, analysis, and\n   execution.  The existing ACTN architecture provides network\n   abstraction and control functions for optical networks but lacks\n   higher-level autonomous capabilities.\n\n   This document explores the introduction of AI based Network\n   Management Agent(NMA) functions into ACTN-based optical networks to\n   achieve high-level autonomy of optical networks.  It discusses the\n   ACTN-enhanced architecture of optical networks after the introduction\n   of NMAs, including key components, interaction relationships, new\n   interface requirements in the enhanced architecture, as well as\n   typical use cases of agent-based autonomous operation and maintenance\n   for optical networks.  The document aims to improve the autonomy\n   level of optical networks and promote the realization of autonomous\n   optical networks by extending the original ACTN architecture.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T10:11:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150265, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhao-ccamp-actn-optical-network-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhao-ccamp-actn-optical-network-agent/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156583/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160250/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165855/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171815/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T10:11:27Z", "title": "Integration of Network Management Agent (NMA) into ACTN-Based Optical Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the development of large language models (LLM), AI Agent\n   software continues to emerge.  AI agents deployed on different\n   network devices need to collaborate to accomplish some complex tasks,\n   such as network measurement and network troubleshooting.  This\n   collaboration requires cross-device communication between AI agents.\n\n   This document proposes a cross-device communication framework for AI\n   agents in network devices, and analyzes the requirements for the\n   communication protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T04:03:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150278, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mzsg-rtgwg-agent-cross-device-comm-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mzsg-rtgwg-agent-cross-device-comm-framework/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156608/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157038/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-05T07:09:04Z", "title": "Cross-device Communication Framework for AI Agents in Network Devices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document introduces agents networking architecture and defines\n   the core components of agent networking, as well as typical\n   interactions among these key components.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-23T15:40:43Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150312, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zl-agents-networking-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zl-agents-networking-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156717/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-10-20T15:40:43Z", "title": "Agents Networking Architecture for Enterprise and Broadband", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Current advancements in AI technologies, particularly large models,\n   have demonstrated immense potential in content generation, reasoning,\n   analysis and so on, providing robust technical support for network\n   automation and self-intelligence.  However, in practical network\n   operations, challenges such as system isolation and fragmented data\n   lead to extensive manual, repetitive, and inefficient tasks, the\n   improvement of intelligence level is very necessary.  This document\n   identifies typical scenarios requiring enhanced intelligence, and\n   explains how AI Agents and large model technologies can empower\n   networks to address operational pain points, reduce manual efforts,\n   and explore impacts on network data, system architectures, and\n   interfaces correspondingly.  It further explores and summarizes\n   standardization efforts in implementation.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-23T15:48:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 143450, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chuyi-nmrg-ai-agent-network", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chuyi-nmrg-ai-agent-network/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/150026/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/153602/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156723/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-23T07:09:36Z", "title": "Large Model based Agents for Network Operation and Maintenance", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes agents networking scenarios in enterprise and\n   home broadband networks.  These scenarios differ from 6G and Internet\n   scenarios.  Since the agentic service is still at the emerging stage,\n   especially in enterprise and home broadband networks, the scenarios\n   are mostly based on reasonable assumptions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-23T19:44:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150348, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zl-agents-networking-scenarios", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zl-agents-networking-scenarios/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156842/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-10-20T19:44:02Z", "title": "Agents Networking Scenarios in Enterprise and Broadband Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Communication between AI agents and between agent and tools is\n   expected to be pivotal in 6G systems.  The 3GPP TR 22.870 outlines\n   various use cases and potential service requirements for AI agent\n   communication within 6G systems.  This document provides examples of\n   use cases and service requirements contained in the 3GPP TR 22.870\n   and extrapolates possible requirements related to agent communication\n   protocols.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-23T23:23:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146922, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-stephan-ai-agent-6g", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-stephan-ai-agent-6g/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153752/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156959/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156969/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-23T07:09:48Z", "title": "AI Agent protocols for 6G systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document discusses WIMSE applicability to Agentic AI, so as to\n   establish independent identities and credential management mechanisms\n   for AI agents.  It also discusses mechanisms for cryptographically\n   binding an AI agent identity to an accountable user or organization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T03:06:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150280, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ni-wimse-ai-agent-identity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ni-wimse-ai-agent-identity/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156613/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156982/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160285/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169753/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171066/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T03:06:18Z", "title": "WIMSE Applicability for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the development of large language models (LLM), AI Agent\n   software continues to emerge.  AI agents deployed on different\n   network devices need to collaborate to accomplish some complex tasks,\n   such as network measurement and network troubleshooting.  This\n   collaboration requires cross-device communication between AI agents.\n   The cross-device communication framework is defined in\n   [I-D.mzsg-rtgwg-agent-cross-device-comm-framework].\n\n   This document describes whether some classical protocols in\n   networking area, and some popular ones in AI Agent area can be used\n   for the cross-device interaction of the AI agents in network devices,\n   and analyzes the gaps.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T04:04:19Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150892, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mao-rtgwg-agent-comm-protocol-gap-analysis", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mao-rtgwg-agent-comm-protocol-gap-analysis/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157039/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-05T07:09:05Z", "title": "Gap Analysis for the Cross-device Communication Protocol for AI Agents in Network Devices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies agent identity management in the Internet of\n   Agents (IOA) system.  It defines the descriptive requirements for\n   agent identities, the agent registration process, the structure and\n   assignment of agent identifiers, and the basic and extended identity\n   management functions performed by the agent gateway based on the\n   agent's descriptive information.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T04:19:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150894, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zheng-agent-identity-management", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zheng-agent-identity-management/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157045/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-11-02T04:19:56Z", "title": "Agent Identity Managenment", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document discusses security requirements for AI agents, covering\n   different stages of security interactions.  These include\n   provisioning, registration, discovery, cross-domain interconnection,\n   and access control.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-24T09:16:09Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150895, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ni-a2a-ai-agent-security-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ni-a2a-ai-agent-security-requirements/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157046/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160279/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170683/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-21T09:16:09Z", "title": "Security Requirements for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document introduces AI Network Traffic Optimization Agents as a\n   dynamic alternative to traditional static network optimization\n   methods.  These AI entities analyze real-time network status (e.g.,\n   latency, node load) and adjust resources flexibly—deployed centrally\n   or on devices—to enhance efficiency, ensure service quality, and cut\n   operational costs.  It defines network traffic optimization\n   (maximizing resource use, meeting QoS) and AI agents (autonomous,\n   learning entities that reduce manual work), then details three key\n   application scenarios: tunnel adjustment (adaptive routing,\n   predictive bandwidth, fault recovery), traffic steering\n   (classification, application-aware policies, pre-emptive load\n   balancing), and network slice adjustment (lifecycle automation, SLA\n   compliance, slice-specific fault fixes).  The document emphasizes the\n   agents’ role in enabling SLA-compliant, autonomous optimization for\n   complex networks.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T05:43:22Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150897, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yuan-rtgwg-traffic-agent-usecase", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yuan-rtgwg-traffic-agent-usecase/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157049/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-05T07:09:06Z", "title": "Use cases of the AI Network Traffic Optimization Agent", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Core network devices like routers fulfill dual roles of data\n   forwarding and security protection.  However, escalating threats\n   (e.g., zero-day vulnerabilities, DDoS attacks) expose limitations of\n   traditional security—relying on static ACLs, signature-based\n   detection, and manual configuration—causing delayed responses, high\n   false positives, and protection gaps.  This paper proposes AI Network\n   Security Agents: intelligent software components leveraging machine\n   learning, behavioral analysis, and real-time data fusion, with three\n   core capabilities (adaptive learning, automation, distributed\n   collaboration) to shift security from passive to intelligent.  Four\n   key scenarios are outlined: dynamic defense against unknown threats\n   via baselines and tracing; ACL optimization via intent parsing;\n   configuration security via baseline checks and simulation; and\n   collaborative defense via intelligence aggregation and linked\n   responses.  AI Agents turn routers into active security\n   orchestrators, enhancing threat protection and operational\n   efficiency.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T05:48:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150899, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yuan-rtgwg-security-agent-usecase", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yuan-rtgwg-security-agent-usecase/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157051/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-05T07:09:07Z", "title": "Use cases of the AI Network Security Agent", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes several typical digital banking scenarios,\n   and discusses the trend of banking digitalization evolving to agentic\n   service inteconnection.  Then, this document proposes an agent\n   networking architecture based on the core component which is agent\n   gateway.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T13:01:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150920, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-men-rtgwg-agent-networking-digibank-scenario", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-men-rtgwg-agent-networking-digibank-scenario/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157089/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-11-02T13:01:29Z", "title": "Agents Networking Scenarios in Enterprise and Broadband Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This draft introduces use cases related to network AI agents in 6G,\n   with a focus on the interaction workflows of network AI agents in two\n   distinct scenarios: connectivity services and third-party application\n   services.  These use cases primarily draw upon 6G-related scenarios\n   outlined in the 3GPP technical report [TR22.870].  Furthermore, the\n   document elaborates on the integration of network AI agents within\n   the 6G framework and discusses corresponding network requirements.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T13:06:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150921, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-tong-network-agent-use-cases-in-6g", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-tong-network-agent-use-cases-in-6g/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157090/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-06T07:09:13Z", "title": "Network AI Agent Use Cases and Requirements in 6G", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes several typical digital banking scenarios,\n   and discusses the trend of banking digitalization evolving to agentic\n   service inteconnection.  Then, this document proposes an agent\n   networking architecture based on the core component which is agent\n   gateway.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-06T13:17:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150923, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-men-rtgwg-agent-networking-in-digibank", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-men-rtgwg-agent-networking-in-digibank/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157092/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-06T07:09:13Z", "title": "Agent Networking Scenarios of Digital Banking", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document discusses the requirements for introducing Agent\n   Gateways into Agent-to-Agent communications for better scalability,\n   communication efficiency, and security etc.  This document also\n   discusses the gaps of current hardware/software gateways that could\n   not fulfil the task, so that a new kind of entity, e.g. Agent\n   Gateway, is needed.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-06-01T06:54:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150956, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-rtgwg-agent-gateway-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-rtgwg-agent-gateway-requirements/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157111/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157800/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-31T07:09:03Z", "title": "Requirements for Agent Gateway", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document focuses on the use cases and requirements of\n   communication protocols for measurement agents on network devices.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-07T01:44:12Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151116, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-rtgwg-ai-agents-measurement", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-rtgwg-ai-agents-measurement/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157150/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-06T07:09:15Z", "title": "Use Cases and Requirements of Communication Protocol for Measurement Agents on Network Devices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document introduces agents networking framework and defines the\n   core components of agent networking in enterprise and broadband, as\n   well as typical interactions among these key components.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-07T09:38:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151160, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zlgsgl-rtgwg-agents-networking-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zlgsgl-rtgwg-agents-networking-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157181/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-07T07:09:10Z", "title": "Agents Networking Framework for Enterprise and Broadband", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies an extension to the OAUTH-TXN-TOKENS\n   (https://drafts.oauth.net/oauth-transaction-tokens/draft-ietf-oauth-\n   transaction-tokens.html) to support agent context propagation within\n   Transaction Tokens for agent-based workloads.  The extension defines\n   the use of the act field to identify the agent performing the action,\n   and leverages the existing sub field (as defined in the base\n   Transaction Tokens specification) to represent the principal.  The\n   sub field is populated according to the rules specified in OAUTH-TXN-\n   TOKENS (https://drafts.oauth.net/oauth-transaction-tokens/draft-ietf-\n   oauth-transaction-tokens.html), based on the 'subject_token' provided\n   in the token request.  For autonomous agents operating independently,\n   the sub field represents the agent itself.  These mechanisms enable\n   services within the call graph to make more granular access control\n   decisions, thereby enhancing security.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-13T23:46:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151690, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-oauth-transaction-tokens-for-agents", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-oauth-transaction-tokens-for-agents/", "rev": "06", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157321/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158644/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158911/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158913/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159554/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161903/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162093/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-11T23:46:37Z", "title": "Transaction Tokens For Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Artificial intelligence (AI) agents consume IETF specifications to\n   generate and operate implementations.  This document defines an\n   \"Agent Considerations\" subsection within the Operations and\n   Management Considerations section described in RFC 5706 and its\n   revision.  It provides guidance on schemas, examples, capability\n   descriptions, and verification, with cross-references to agent-\n   specific security and privacy analysis.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-15T13:54:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151711, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-steele-agent-considerations", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-steele-agent-considerations/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168914/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157328/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-11T13:54:00Z", "title": "Agent Considerations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This method extends OAuth 2.0 by adding fields to token and message\n   flows, enabling sub-agents to act as a task group.  It simplifies\n   authorization for task groups, avoids repeated interactions between\n   sub-agents and the authorization server, and bounds the authority\n   delegated to the group and to each member while maintaining\n   compatibility with existing OAuth 2.0 workflows.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-02T06:55:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151899, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-song-oauth-ai-agent-collaborate-authz", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-song-oauth-ai-agent-collaborate-authz/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157353/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160254/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165250/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-01T06:55:28Z", "title": "OAuth2.0 Extension for Multi-AI Agent Collaboration", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes agents networking scenarios in enterprise and\n   home broadband networks.  These scenarios differ from mobile networks\n   and Internet scenarios.  Since the agentic service is still at the\n   emerging stage, especially in enterprise and home broadband networks,\n   the scenarios are mostly based on reasonable assumptions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-10T08:47:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 151919, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-pang-agents-networking-scenarios", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-pang-agents-networking-scenarios/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157357/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-10T07:09:06Z", "title": "Agents Networking Scenarios in Enterprise and Broadband Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the framework and requirements for\n   intercommunication between Agent Gateways (AGw) in the Agent Internet\n   (IoA) ecosystem.  It specifies a hierarchical layered model,\n   functional components, protocol requirements and deployment\n   consideration for AGw interconnection.  The framework aims to address\n   data synchronization, protocol compatibility, and security challenges\n   in cross-domain agent collaboration, enabling efficient and scalable\n   communication for distributed intelligent agents.  It is compatible\n   with existing IoA reference architectures while specializing in\n   cross-domain gateway interoperability.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-06T09:03:33Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 152483, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-han-rtgwg-agent-gateway-intercomm-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-han-rtgwg-agent-gateway-intercomm-framework/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157713/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159197/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165672/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-05T09:03:33Z", "title": "Agent Gateway Intercommunication Framework", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous agents such as LLM-powered crawlers, browser-integrated\n   assistants, and task-oriented bots are rapidly becoming first-class\n   HTTP clients on the Web. Today’s infrastructure largely assumes a\n   human behind a browser and monetizes content through advertising and\n   coarse subscriptions.  Automated agents consume content at scale\n   without rendering pages or viewing ads, exacerbating bot-mitigation\n   arms races and economic misalignment between content providers and AI\n   systems.\n\n   This document describes an HTTP Agent Profile (HAP) that enables: (1)\n   cryptographic authentication of agent traffic using HTTP Message\n   Signatures; (2) clear separation between human and agent traffic\n   using privacy-preserving human tokens; and (3) protocol-level value\n   exchange for agents via HTTP status code 402 (\"Payment Required\") and\n   pluggable micropayment mechanisms.  The profile reuses existing HTTP\n   features and is designed for incremental deployment via reverse\n   proxies, CDNs, and agent libraries.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-28T21:44:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 152503, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-dhir-http-agent-profile", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-dhir-http-agent-profile/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157726/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-28T07:09:05Z", "title": "HTTP Agent Profile (HAP): Authenticated and Monetized Agent Traffic on the Web", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Agent Operation Authorization framework —\n   a structured mechanism that enables verifiable delegation of actions\n   from human principals to autonomous AI agents with fine-grained agent\n   operation authorization.\n\n   The framework introduces two distinct phases:\n\n   *  Agent Operation Authorization Request: A structured proposal of\n      operations converted to a JSON Web Token (JWT) without including\n      the user's original natural language input.\n\n   *  Agent Operation Authorization Token: A JSON Web Token representing\n      confirmed authorization for a specific agent operation,\n      enforceable at runtime by agents and verifiers.  It\n      cryptographically verifies user intent, prevents unauthorized or\n      hallucinated actions, and ensures auditable traceability of each\n      authorized operation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-17T05:57:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 152523, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-agent-operation-authorization", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-agent-operation-authorization/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157743/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160296/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161108/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-16T07:09:12Z", "title": "Agent Operation Authorization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies extensions to the Entity Attestation Token\n   (EAT) [RFC9248] to support robust, interoperable attestation of\n   capabilities in agentic AI systems.  These extensions introduce new\n   claims and guidance for securely asserting agent functional,\n   reasoning, and operational capabilities, as well as their\n   compositional structure and policy constraints.  The goal is to\n   enable trustworthy, verifiable, and privacy-respecting capability\n   attestation for autonomous agents in dynamic, decentralized\n   environments.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-12-16T01:12:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146470, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-huang-rats-agentic-eat-cap-attest", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-huang-rats-agentic-eat-cap-attest/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/152745/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-12-16T07:09:03Z", "title": "Capability Attestation Extensions for the Entity Attestation Token (EAT) in Agentic AI Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The evolution of network management towards autonomic operation\n   requires the deployment of AI agents at various hierarchical layers,\n   including directly on network elements.  This transformation shifts\n   network devices from passively managed resources to autonomous\n   entities capable of local decision-making and collaborative problem-\n   solving.\n\n   This document discusses the applicability of the Agent-to-Agent (A2A)\n   Protocol to the network management plane, specifically for\n   communication between Controller Agents (CAs) and Device Agents\n   (DAs).  This indicates that the inherent characteristics of Device\n   Agents necessitate the adoption of the agent-to-agent communication\n   paradigm.  The document further explores generic workflows,\n   deployment scenarios, and the relationship of A2A with existing\n   network management protocols like NETCONF, RESTCONF, gNMI,and the\n   Model Context Protocol (MCP).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T10:26:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 152998, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yan-a2a-device-agent-applicability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yan-a2a-device-agent-applicability/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158258/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159608/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165856/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T10:26:28Z", "title": "Applicability of A2A Protocol for Network Management Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies DNS-Native Agent Naming and Resolution (DN-\n   ANR) for AI agents.  DN-ANR uses domain names (FQDNs) as stable Agent\n   Identifiers and resolves a selected Agent Identifier to verifiable\n   endpoints and supported protocol/version information with a\n   cryptographic integrity chain, with DNSSEC preferred.\n\n   DN-ANR is a post-selection resolution profile.  It does not define\n   agent discovery, capability search, semantic matching, ranking, or\n   routing decisions.  Agent discovery, publication, or registry,\n   including DNS-based mechanisms such as DNS-AID, may produce candidate\n   Agent Identifiers; DN-ANR resolves and verifies the identifier\n   selected by such mechanisms or by local policy.  Within that scope,\n   DN-ANR additionally defines DNS-based version distribution and\n   deterministic version selection, canonicalized SVCB integrity cross-\n   checking, and a signed HTTPS mirror for clients that cannot perform\n   SVCB queries.  While AI agents are the primary use case, the\n   resolution and verification behavior defined here applies to any\n   entity identified by an FQDN, such as services and workloads.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T03:02:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153002, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-dns-native-agent-naming-resolution", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 36, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-dns-native-agent-naming-resolution/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158273/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160600/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165756/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T03:02:29Z", "title": "DNS-Native AI Agent Naming and Resolution", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies Agentic JSON Web Token (Agentic JWT), as an\n   extension to OAuth 2.0 that addresses authorization challenges unique\n   to autonomous agentic AI systems.  This protocol solves the problem\n   of Zero-Trust drift due to non-deterministic Agentic AI clients\n   causing separation between user's (resource owner's) intent and\n   client application's actions.\n\n   Traditional OAuth 2.0 assumes that client applications faithfully\n   represent user intent when requesting authorization.  This assumption\n   breaks down when autonomous AI agents dynamically generate workflows,\n   create sub-agents, and make authorization decisions without\n   continuous human oversight.  We term this the \"intent-execution\n   separation problem.\"\n\n   Agentic JWT introduces three mechanisms to address this problem: (1)\n   cryptographic agent identity through agent checksums (based on\n   agent's system prompts, tools and configurations), (2) workflow-aware\n   token binding that links user intent to agent execution, and (3) a\n   new OAuth 2.0 grant type (agent_checksum) for secure token issuance,\n   (4) a flavor of PoP (Proof-of-Possession) at the level of an agentic\n   identity to prevent token replays by other agents in the same multi-\n   agent process.\n\n   This specification enables Zero-Trust security principles in multi-\n   agent systems while maintaining backward compatibility with existing\n   OAuth 2.0 infrastructure.  The security analysis and experimental\n   validation are described in the companion research paper.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-02T04:53:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153064, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-goswami-agentic-jwt", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 71, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-goswami-agentic-jwt/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158393/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165244/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-01T04:53:28Z", "title": "Secure Intent Protocol: JWT Compatible Agentic Identity and Workflow Management", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the continuous introduction of intelligent agent communication\n   and interaction protocols, the current DNS cannot adequately meet the\n   requirements for agent service resolution.  This document defines a\n   new DNS resource record type, AGENT, which is a SVCB-compatible RR\n   type, and specifies the mapping specifications.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-19T07:50:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153081, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-aiproto-svcb-mapping-for-agents", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-aiproto-svcb-mapping-for-agents/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158446/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158458/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169061/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-15T07:50:26Z", "title": "Service Binding Mapping for Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI Agents are rapidly evolving from academic concepts into the core\n   engines driving next-generation applications.  However, their\n   autonomy, dynamic nature, and complex delegation relationships pose a\n   fundamental challenge to our existing authentication and\n   authorization frameworks, which were designed for human users and\n   traditional software.  This document dissects the novel\n   characteristics of AI Agents and outlines the new requirements for\n   authentication and authorization which can manage dynamic behavior\n   rather than verifying static identity.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-07-10T02:22:59Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153100, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-ai-agent-auth-new-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-ai-agent-auth-new-requirements/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158486/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-20T07:09:27Z", "title": "New requirements for Authentication and Authorization in the AI Agents era", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   OAuth 2.0 Rich Authorization Requests (RAR) [RFC9396] defines a\n   syntax for clients to request fine-grained permissions.  While\n   powerful, this mechanism lacks standardized methods for clients to\n   express three critical contextual dimensions prevalent in modern\n   automated systems: the high-level user intent driving the request,\n   the required security policy under which the request should be\n   evaluated, and the binding of the authorization's lifecycle to an\n   external process.\n\n   This document extends the RAR framework to address these gaps.  It\n   first defines a new authorization_details type, *intent_request*, to\n   facilitate goal-oriented authorization.  Second, it introduces two\n   new parameters for the authorization_details object:\n   *policy_context*, to request authorization under a specific assurance\n   level, and *lifecycle_binding*, to tie the validity of the\n   authorization grant to the state of an external entity.  These\n   extensions enable authorization servers to make more intelligent,\n   secure, and context-aware decisions, particularly in ecosystems\n   involving autonomous agents and complex, long-running tasks.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-24T06:41:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153488, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-oauth-rar-agent-extensions", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-oauth-rar-agent-extensions/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159309/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162452/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-23T07:09:04Z", "title": "Policy, Lifecycle, and Intent Extensions for OAuth Rich Authorization Requests", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document disucss and analyses the impact of AI Agent on network\n   infrastructure aiming to facilitate the discussion and\n   standardization about AI Agent communication within IETF.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-08T09:04:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146867, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-han-ai-agent-impact-infra", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-han-ai-agent-impact-infra/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153572/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-09T07:09:43Z", "title": "The Impact of AI Agent to Network Infrastructure", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This draft introduces use cases related to AI Agents in 6G networks,\n   primarily referencing the technical report of 3GPP SA1 R20 Study on\n   6G Use Cases and Service Requirements (TR 22.870).  It also\n   elaborates on some of the requirements for introducing AI Agents into\n   6G networks from the perspective of operators.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-21T05:50:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153162, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yu-dmsc-ai-agent-use-cases-in-6g", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yu-dmsc-ai-agent-use-cases-in-6g/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158670/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158671/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166565/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-20T05:50:53Z", "title": "AI Agent Use Cases and Requirements in 6G Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic communication fundamentally differs from traditional machine\n   communication in that its outputs are recursively consumed and\n   interpreted by Large Language Models (LLMs).  This unique\n   characteristic makes efficient use of the model's limited context a\n   critical requirement.  However, current agent communication protocols\n   - such as the Model Context Protocol (MCP) and Agent-to-Agent (A2A) -\n   often suffer from token or context bloat, caused by redundant schema\n   definitions, verbose message structures, and inefficient capability\n   exchanges.  As a result, a substantial number of tokens are\n   unnecessarily consumed within the model’s context window.\n\n   To address these issues, this draft defines the Agentic Data\n   Optimization Layer (ADOL), a general and foundational data-layer for\n   agent communication.  ADOL introduces a set of backward-compatible\n   optimizations, including schema deduplication through JSON\n   references, adaptive inclusion of optional fields, controllable\n   response verbosity, and retrieval-based selection mechanisms.\n   Collectively, these mechanisms reduce token consumption, enhance\n   context efficiency, and provide a scalable foundation for future\n   agent communication frameworks.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-07-16T09:56:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 152843, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chang-agent-token-efficient", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chang-agent-token-efficient/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158015/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158019/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158691/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-20T07:09:22Z", "title": "A Token-efficient Data Layer for Agentic Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   As the development of large model and agent technology, it is a trend\n   for multi-agent collaboration to solve complex problems.  This\n   document proposes an Agent Communication Framework, a multi-agent\n   communication and collaboration framework that facilitates the\n   coordination of heterogeneous multi-agents and supports intelligent\n   network operations and maintenance (AIOps).  Its architecture\n   includes an AI gateway and an Agent Name Service, along with\n   capabilities such as monitoring and tracking, as well as security\n   protection.  Agent Communication Framework provides a comprehensive\n   solution for multi-agent communication and collaboration, laying the\n   foundation for future interactive, scalable, secure, and controllable\n   multi-agent network intelligent operations and maintenance.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-07-29T05:44:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153353, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-fu-nmop-agent-communication-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-fu-nmop-agent-communication-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159052/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-28T07:09:03Z", "title": "Agent Communication Framework for Network AIOps", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Agent Registration and Discovery Protocol\n   (ARDP), a lightweight protocol for registering, discovering, and\n   reaching autonomous software agents in distributed and federated\n   environments.  ARDP provides stable agent identities, dynamic\n   endpoint resolution, capability advertisement (including protocol\n   selection among MCP, A2A, HTTP, and gRPC), minimal presence\n   signaling, and a security-first discovery control plane.  ARDP is\n   transport-agnostic and complementary to existing agent interaction\n   protocols.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-08-28T08:14:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153471, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-pioli-agent-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-pioli-agent-discovery/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159281/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159998/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-28T07:09:08Z", "title": "Agent Registration and Discovery Protocol (ARDP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a task-oriented, agent-based method for fault\n   recovery in converged public-private mobile networks.  The proposed\n   method introduces a multi-agent collaboration framework that enables\n   autonomous failure detection, scoped diagnosis, inter-domain\n   coordination, and intent-driven policy reconfiguration.  It is\n   particularly applicable in complex 5G/6G network deployments, such as\n   Multi-Operator Core Networks (MOCN) and Standalone Non-Public\n   Networks (SNPN), where traditional centralized management is\n   insufficient for ensuring high service reliability and dynamic\n   recovery.  The document also specifies protocol requirements for\n   inter-agent communication, state consistency, and secure\n   coordination, aiming to support interoperability and resilience\n   across heterogeneous network domains.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-02-06T04:11:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 148939, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yue-anima-agent-recovery-networks", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yue-anima-agent-recovery-networks/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/154464/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-02-06T07:09:03Z", "title": "Task-Oriented Multi-Agent Recovery Framework for High-Reliability in Converged Mobile Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Distributed Multi-Agent Systems consist of Agents and MCP Servers\n   operating across multiple administrative domains, each with its own\n   Identity Providers (IdPs) and Authorization Servers (AS).\n\n   This document discusses the challenges and solution approaches for\n   sharing authorization information securely and flexibly across\n   domains, including the use of dynamic identity, interoperable claims,\n   and verifiable credentials.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-05T22:11:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153519, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-diaconu-agents-authz-info-sharing", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-diaconu-agents-authz-info-sharing/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167320/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159397/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-04T22:11:21Z", "title": "Cross-Domain AuthZ Information sharing for Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a framework for enabling seamless cross-domain\n   interoperability among AI agents operating across different networks,\n   administrative domains, and heterogeneous platforms.  The framework\n   addresses the challenges of identity federation, trust establishment,\n   policy harmonization, and secure communication that arise when AI\n   agents from distinct administrative realms need to collaborate on\n   shared tasks.  It specifies mechanisms for agent discovery,\n   capability negotiation, trust delegation, and federated policy\n   enforcement, enabling scalable and secure multi-domain AI\n   collaboration without requiring centralized control.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-08-15T02:13:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153606, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-dmsc-agent-cdi", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 34, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-dmsc-agent-cdi/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159546/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-14T07:09:03Z", "title": "Cross-Domain Interoperability Framework for AI Agent Collaboration", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The context distribution is important in a multi-agent system, which\n   will impact the execution latency, token consumption, and task\n   completion success rate, especially in a complex and multi-round\n   workflow.\n\n   This document specifies the scenarios and procedures of agent context\n   distribution as well as the corresponding optimization during the\n   procedures in order to provide precise control of the context\n   distribution among the multiple agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-16T14:25:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153608, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chang-agent-context-interaction", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chang-agent-context-interaction/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159548/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159649/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161028/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-16T07:09:13Z", "title": "Agent Context Interaction Optimizations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document presents an architectural framework for an Agent\n   Communication Gateway (Agent-GW), designed to support large-scale,\n   heterogeneous, and dynamic multi-agent collaboration across\n   administrative and protocol boundaries.\n\n   As agents evolve from isolated entities to a collaborative digital\n   workforce, the infrastructure must transition from rigid, endpoint-\n   based connectivity to intent-based interaction.  This draft proposes\n   Agent-GW as an infrastructure hub that provides native primitives for\n   Semantic Routing (dispatching tasks by intent and capability),\n   Working Memory (shared structured context across multi-step\n   workflows), automated protocol adaptation (normalizing heterogeneous\n   interfaces into a unified agent-facing protocol), oracle-free agent\n   evaluation, and collaborative inference acceleration via a Knowledge\n   Delivery Network (KDN).\n\n   Beyond a single-gateway deployment, this document defines a\n   hierarchical architecture for wide-area, multi-domain agent networks:\n   three gateway tiers (access, domain, and inter-domain).  It describes\n   which traffic classes traverse which tiers on both the data plane and\n   the control plane, and specifies cross-domain semantic routing, name\n   resolution, resilience, and operational considerations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-14T03:12:11Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153678, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-agent-gw", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-agent-gw/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159766/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160910/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165888/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-13T03:12:11Z", "title": "Agent Communication Gateway for Semantic Routing and Working Memory", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous agents based on large language models increasingly perform\n   consequential tasks on behalf of humans and other agents.\n   Demonstrating that recorded agent behavior truthfully represents\n   actual behavior is essential for accountability, compliance, and\n   human oversight.  This document defines a data format for verifiable\n   agent conversation records using CDDL, with representations in both\n   JSON and CBOR.  The format captures session metadata, message\n   exchanges, tool invocations, reasoning traces, and system events in a\n   structured, extensible CDDL definition for verifiable agent\n   conversation records.  COSE is used as the signing method to allow\n   for native interoperability in SCITT Transparency Services and the\n   CDDL definition allows for seemless integration in Evidence as\n   specified in RFC 9334.  The specification supports cross-vendor\n   interoperability by defining a common representation that\n   accommodates translation from multiple existing agent implementations\n   with distinct data structure layouts that are typically represented\n   in JSON.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-04T12:39:50Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153820, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-birkholz-verifiable-agent-conversations", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 51, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-birkholz-verifiable-agent-conversations/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160066/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168358/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-31T12:39:50Z", "title": "Verifiable Agent Conversation Records", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents increasingly invoke protected APIs on behalf of human\n   users.  This document defines an OAuth profile for identifying an\n   agent client instance, obtaining an authenticated user's consent,\n   issuing resource-bound and sender-constrained access tokens,\n   attenuating authority through OAuth Token Exchange, and rotating\n   refresh tokens safely.  The profile uses existing OAuth and JOSE\n   mechanisms wherever possible and defines no new JWT claims or OAuth\n   endpoints.  Operational facilities such as policy engines, audit\n   stores, budgets, event streams, and credential vaults are outside the\n   interoperable core.  Grantex is an incomplete reference\n   implementation and is not required for conformance.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-03T08:03:07Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153866, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mishra-oauth-agent-grants", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mishra-oauth-agent-grants/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168310/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160192/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160593/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-30T08:03:07Z", "title": "OAuth Profile for Delegated AI Agent Authorization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The emergence of distributed artificial intelligence (AI) systems,\n   particularly those composed of autonomous agents operating across\n   cloud, edge, and endpoint environments, introduces new networking\n   requirements.  These include location transparency, seamless\n   mobility, multi-homing, and logical isolation at scale.  This\n   document explores how the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) can\n   serve as a robust network substrate to meet these requirements.  The\n   document outlines use cases, design considerations, and minimal\n   extensions to the existing LISP framework to support context-aware\n   mapping and AI agent-centric communication.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-05T07:24:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153885, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wang-lisp-ai-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wang-lisp-ai-agent/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160249/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161878/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T07:09:03Z", "title": "Using LISP as a Network Substrate for AI Agent Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This specification defines an extension to the OAuth 2.0 scope\n   parameter, as specified in RFC 6749, which is used to express the\n   permissions granted to an access token.  The proposed extension\n   introduces a structured syntax for scope values to enable fine-\n   grained authorization for the installation and execution of Modular\n   Capability Units (encapsulated and reusable functional modules such\n   as skills) within Agent ecosystems.  However, current mechanisms for\n   authorizing such modules generally lack a standardized way to express\n   and obtain consent for the complex, fine-grained permissions they\n   require during installation.\n\n   This document addresses this limitation by defining a structured\n   format for the scope parameter.  The format extends the simple space-\n   delimited strings with a colon-separated syntax:\n   [resource_type]:[action]:[target][:constraints].  This syntax allows\n   for the precise description of permissions for operations such as\n   file system access, command-line execution, network access, tool\n   invocation, and scheduled tasks.  These extensions provide a\n   standardized, machine-readable way to request, convey, and validate\n   detailed permissions, thereby enhancing users' security control over\n   their resources while maintaining compatibility with the existing\n   OAuth token issuance and validation flows.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-02T13:50:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153916, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-oauth-scope-agent-extensions", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-oauth-scope-agent-extensions/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160367/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-02T07:09:16Z", "title": "Structured and Constraint Extensions for OAuth Scopes", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document outlines the key objectives of introducing Agentic AI\n   to the field of network management and highlights the fundamental\n   issues with existing technologies that must be addressed to achieve\n   these goals.  It emphasizes the necessity for relevant groups within\n   the IETF/IRTF and presents the core technological areas requiring\n   standardization.  The aim of Agentic AI is to facilitate a paradigm\n   shift in which multiple autonomous AI agents collaborate to fully\n   automate network operation, management and security.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T03:28:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150293, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hong-nmrg-agenticai-ps", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hong-nmrg-agenticai-ps/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/156665/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/160469/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165760/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T03:28:30Z", "title": "Motivations and Problem Statement of Agentic AI for network management", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the advent of the era of AI large models and intelligent agents,\n   more and more scenarios about agent interconnection have emerged,\n   such as collaboration among multiple agents within a household,\n   intelligent robots cooperating to complete pipeline tasks in\n   different operations of the industrial Internet, drone groups,\n   intelligent vehicle networking, etc.  These scenarios not only\n   require low latency and high bandwidth, but also demand efficient\n   information exchange and cross-domain coordination and scheduling\n   capabilities in complex collaborative tasks.  Therefore, new\n   orchestration and management technologies and frameworks are needed\n   in existing networks to address this.  The interconnection of\n   different agents also brings about an emergence of inference, with a\n   large number of inference requests being processed from the mobile\n   phone side to the cloud.  In order to improve inference efficiency,\n   in a cloud-edge-end multi-layer inference architecture, large models\n   and agents at different levels cooperate to complete tasks, resulting\n   in a complex intelligent agent interconnection network.  Gateways and\n   routers serve as forwarding entries on the network road highways,\n   responsible for building communication channels for the agents spread\n   throughout the network, which requiring function upgrades to support\n   the continuously evolving inference service in the future.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T07:05:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153977, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chuyi-nmrg-agentic-network-inference", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chuyi-nmrg-agentic-network-inference/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160570/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165799/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T07:05:36Z", "title": "Agentic Network Architecture and Protocol for Supporting Agent Interconnection Communication and Multi-level Inference", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the advancement of AI technologies, AI Agent traffic will\n   account for the majority of network traffic, driving an increasing\n   demand for higher quality of multi-modal data transmissions.  Current\n   networks lack awareness of the diverse transmission quality\n   requirements for multi-modal data within a single traffic, leading to\n   degraded service quality and inefficient utilization of network\n   resources.  This document proposes methods to enable networks (e.g.,\n   mobile network, transport network) to recognize the characteristics\n   and the transmission requirement of AI Agent multi-modal data and\n   outlines necessary capabilities and features of the AI Agent\n   Protocols.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-03T15:25:09Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153989, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hw-protocol-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hw-protocol-agent/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160612/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-03T07:09:46Z", "title": "AI Agent Protocols for Multi-modality", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes best practices for authentication and\n   authorization of AI agent interactions.  It leverages existing\n   standards such as the Workload Identity in Multi-System Environments\n   (WIMSE) architecture and OAuth 2.0 family of specifications.  Rather\n   than defining new protocols, this document describes how existing and\n   widely deployed standards can be applied or extended to establish\n   agent authentication and authorization.  By doing so, it aims to\n   provide a framework within which to use existing standards, identify\n   gaps and guide future standardization efforts for agent\n   authentication and authorization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T19:39:07Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2404/", "id": 154009, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-klrc-aiagent-auth", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-klrc-aiagent-auth/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/36/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160728/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161747/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163838/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166149/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-09T16:57:11Z", "title": "AI Agent Authentication and Authorization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic AI systems combine planning, reasoning, tool invocation, and\n   feedback loops to pursue system-defined goals with a controlled\n   degree of autonomy.  In networking, this enables an evolution from\n   statically configured automation toward goal-driven closed-loop\n   operations spanning multiple protocol layers and administrative\n   domains.\n\n   This document introduces architectural principles for agentic\n   augmentation of the existing layered protocol stack as represented by\n   the Internet protocol (IP) suite.  Existing protocol mechanisms\n   continue to support interoperability and packet transport, while AI\n   agents associated with one or more layers cooperate to support\n   communication service objectives.  Participating agents may interpret\n   intents, decompose objectives, delegate responsibilities, and\n   coordinate actions within their respective protocol layers and\n   operational scopes.\n\n   The purpose of this document is to initiate discussion within the\n   research community on agentic networking.  It identifies\n   architectural research challenges associated with introducing AI\n   agents at selected protocol layers and enabling cooperation among\n   agents at the same or different layers, across compute nodes and\n   administrative domains.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T18:57:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154015, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jadoon-nmrg-agentic-ai-autonomous-networks", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jadoon-nmrg-agentic-ai-autonomous-networks/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160763/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172066/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T18:57:56Z", "title": "Agentic AI Architectural Principles for Autonomous Computer Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a protocol abstraction layer that enables Media\n   over QUIC Transport (MOQT) to serve as a unified transport substrate\n   for inter-agent communication protocols.  The abstraction provides a\n   common mapping of request-response and streaming patterns onto MOQT's\n   publish/subscribe model, allowing diverse agent protocols to leverage\n   MOQT's real-time streaming capabilities, built-in prioritization, and\n   efficient multiplexing over QUIC.\n\n   The document demonstrates the application of this abstraction to two\n   prominent inter-agent protocols: the Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol\n   [A2A], which focuses on agent discovery, task delegation, and\n   collaboration; and the Model Context Protocol (MCP) [MCP], which\n   provides tool and resource access for agents.  This unified approach\n   enables interoperability across diverse agent ecosystems while\n   preserving each protocol's semantics.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-03T20:43:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154021, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nandakumar-ai-agent-moq-transport", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nandakumar-ai-agent-moq-transport/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160780/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-03T07:09:54Z", "title": "MOQ Transport for Agent Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes the Agent Attachment Protocol (AAP)\n   that enables AI agents to establish attachment to an edge\n   node and derive communication and attachment-related\n   properties. These properties include endpoint identifiers,\n   supported communication mechanisms, and attachment context\n   information that can be exposed to discovery systems. AAP\n   focuses on how agents obtain and maintain attachment state\n   and how attachment-derived properties can be represented in a\n   consistent and interoperable manner. These properties can be\n   used by forwarding functions at edge nodes and by routing or\n   control-plane mechanisms to support communication between\n   agents.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-03T00:58:59Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154036, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-dunbar-agent-attachment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-dunbar-agent-attachment/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160869/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162825/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-02T00:58:59Z", "title": "Agent Attachment Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the widespread adoption of AI Agents, traditional network\n   architectures can no longer meet the demand for efficient\n   collaboration between agents and networks.  This document proposes a\n   new paradigm of \"AI Agent Policy-Aware Network\", enabling three key\n   transformations: from Flow-aware to Agent-aware, from QoS-based to\n   Policy-intent-based, and from Network-driven to Agent-network\n   collaborative.  By defining core components such as the Agent Policy-\n   aware Controller and Agent Policy-Aware Device, this paradigm\n   establishes a dynamic mapping mechanism between Agent intents and\n   network policies, supporting key scenarios including autonomous\n   performance measurement, path optimization, SLA assurance, and secure\n   transmission.  This document outlines the background, scenarios, use\n   cases and requirements of Agent Policy-aware Network.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-02T10:01:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154512, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-rtgwg-agent-policy-aware-network", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-rtgwg-agent-policy-aware-network/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160964/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165284/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-01T10:01:37Z", "title": "Use Cases and Requirements for AI Agent Policy-Aware Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents operating in enterprise campus networks execute user-\n   delegated Tasks by invoking multiple tools and services, often\n   without continuous user supervision.  Traditional authorization\n   models assume stable applications and human-driven interactions,\n   creating a mismatch when applied to autonomous agents that can chain\n   actions across heterogeneous systems.\n\n   Campus environments also contain heterogeneous and legacy services\n   across diverse protocols, making per-service agent-aware\n   authorization difficult to deploy consistently.  Similar problems can\n   also appear in residential access networks where home AI agents,\n   smart-home hubs, and personal devices share a subscriber line and are\n   connected through a broadband network gateway (BNG).\n\n   This document describes the problem space for campus agent access\n   control and argues that agents require task-bound privilege reduction\n   (\"scope-down\") and that enforcement can be provided by in-path\n   network devices in order to preserve compatibility with existing\n   systems.  It also describes a home network and BNG use case where the\n   access provider or home gateway can provide a coarse but useful\n   network-level boundary for agent traffic.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T08:48:47Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154570, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-shang-campus-agent-scope-down", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-shang-campus-agent-scope-down/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160973/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165825/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T08:48:47Z", "title": "Campus Agent Identification and Scope-Down Access Control", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document focuses on enterprise scenarios, investigating key\n   technologies including agent identification and registration,\n   capability discovery, efficient communication, and secure\n   collaboration.  It proposes an agent identifier and semantic routing\n   mechanism to achieve trusted access and efficient collaboration among\n   heterogeneous agents, providing a technical path for campus-level\n   multi-agent cooperation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-16T12:29:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154637, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-han-agent-comm-enterprise", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-han-agent-comm-enterprise/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161015/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-16T07:09:15Z", "title": "Considerations for AI Agent Communication and Networking in Enterprise", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI-based agent-to-agent communication increasingly occurs across\n   trust domains (e.g., between enterprises, service providers, SaaS\n   platforms, and application third parties).  While many agent\n   protocols and platforms can provide transport security and local\n   permission models, deployments lack a coherent, interoperable\n   baseline for verifiable agent identity, credentialing, cross-domain\n   authorisation, delegation, revocation, and auditability.\n\n   This document defines an architectural framework for a cross-domain\n   trust substrate for AI-based agent ecosystems.  The framework is\n   intended to be agent protocol-agnostic and to provide a consistent\n   trust baseline that existing and emerging AI agent protocols can\n   build upon.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-16T13:25:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154640, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kiliram-agent-trust-auth-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kiliram-agent-trust-auth-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161021/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-16T07:09:15Z", "title": "A Trust and Authentication Framework for Cross-Domain Agent-to-Agent Communications", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the AgentID Protocol, an open standard for\n   establishing, verifying, and managing the identity of autonomous AI\n   agents operating across the Internet.  As AI agents increasingly\n   interact with web services, APIs, and each other on behalf of humans\n   and organisations, the industry lacks a universal mechanism to\n   determine which agent is making a request, who is accountable for it,\n   and what it is permitted to do.\n\n   AgentID introduces the Agent Identity Token (AIT), a signed JWT\n   carrying agent identity, owner verification, capability, and\n   delegation chain claims.  The protocol defines agent registration,\n   owner verification levels, service-side access tiers, and transparent\n   delegation chains with scope attenuation.  It builds on existing\n   standards including OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, JSON Web Token (JWT),\n   and JSON Web Key (JWK), adapting them to the unique requirements of\n   non-human autonomous actors.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-16T17:17:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154676, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gudlab-agentid-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gudlab-agentid-protocol/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161062/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-16T07:09:17Z", "title": "AgentID: An Identity Protocol for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent Identity and Discovery (AID) is a minimal, DNS-first discovery\n   protocol for locating agent service endpoints.  Given a domain name,\n   an AID client queries a DNS TXT record at the well-known subdomain\n   _agent.<domain> and learns the service endpoint URI, protocol token,\n   authentication hint, and optional metadata for that agent.\n\n   This document defines the AID v1.2 record format, client discovery\n   algorithm, exact-host lookup rules, security requirements, and IANA\n   registrations for the `_agent` DNS node name and the `agent` service\n   name.  AID is intentionally small.  After discovery, protocol-\n   specific mechanisms such as MCP or A2A handle communication and\n   capability negotiation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-17T14:10:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154909, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nemethi-aid-agent-identity-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nemethi-aid-agent-identity-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161155/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-17T07:09:13Z", "title": "Agent Identity and Discovery (AID)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Identity Protocol (AIP), an open\n   standard for verifiable identity and policy enforcement for\n   artificial intelligence (AI) agents.\n\n   Agent Identity Protocol (AIP) addresses the problem of AI agents\n   operating with unbounded permissions -- running as users, inheriting\n   full API key access, and executing tool calls with no verifiable\n   identity boundary between human and non-human actors.\n\n   The protocol is structured as two cooperating layers.  Layer 1\n   (Identity) gives every agent a unique identifier and a key pair\n   registered with an AIP Registry; the agent signs every outbound\n   action with that key.  Layer 2 (Enforcement) interposes a proxy\n   between the AI client and every tool server that verifies the\n   signature, evaluates a declarative policy, and produces an allow,\n   deny, or hold decision before any tool is reached.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-17T18:09:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154943, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-aip-agent-identity-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 30, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-aip-agent-identity-protocol/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161172/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-17T07:09:14Z", "title": "Agent Identity Protocol: Agentic Authentication and Authorized Policy Enforcement", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines Attenuating Authorization Tokens (AATs), a\n   signed credential format for task-scoped delegation in AI agent\n   systems.  An AAT encodes the tools an agent may invoke and the\n   argument constraints that apply to those invocations.  A token holder\n   authorized to delegate can derive a token offline with equal or\n   narrower authority, subject to the parent token's depth and lifetime\n   limits.  The resulting delegation chain is verifiable offline by any\n   enforcement point that has the root issuer's trust anchor key.\n\n   This specification profiles the OAuth Rich Authorization Requests\n   format (RFC 9396) for tool-level capability claims, adds delegation-\n   chain claims, and defines a core constraint vocabulary for argument\n   restrictions.  The chain verification algorithm authenticates each\n   delegation step and enforces monotonic attenuation without network\n   contact with the root issuer.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T03:07:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155051, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-niyikiza-oauth-attenuating-agent-tokens", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 73, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-niyikiza-oauth-attenuating-agent-tokens/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161215/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164433/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171025/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T23:57:51Z", "title": "Attenuating Authorization Tokens for Agentic Delegation Chains", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Internet of Agents (IoA) requires a robust infrastructure to\n   manage the lifecycle, discovery, and interaction of autonomous agents\n   across distributed network domains.  While the Agent Gateway (AGW)\n   provides a localized control point, large-scale deployments\n   necessitate a mechanism for multiple gateways to synchronize agent\n   metadata and routing information.  This document specifies the Agent\n   Metadata Synchronization Protocol (AMSP).  AMSP facilitates the\n   exchange of Agent Records between hierarchical gateways (Level-1 and\n   Level-2), ensuring global reachability, efficient resource\n   utilization, and loop-free metadata propagation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-30T09:53:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155486, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-agent-metadata-sync-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-agent-metadata-sync-protocol/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167092/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161388/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-29T09:53:56Z", "title": "Agent Metadata Synchronization Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Credential Attestation Protocol\n   (ACAP), a cryptographic credentialing protocol for autonomous AI\n   agent pipelines.  An ACAP credential is a short-lived JSON Web Token\n   (JWT) signed with RS256 that carries scope-limited permissions\n   together with a SHA-256 hash of the original human instruction that\n   initiated the task.  Credentials may be delegated to child agents;\n   each delegation narrows scope, cannot outlive its parent, increments\n   a delegation depth counter, and extends a tamper-evident chain of\n   token identifiers.  Every lifecycle event is recorded in an append-\n   only, hash-chained audit log.\n\n   This document specifies the credential format, issuance rules,\n   delegation rules, verification algorithm, revocation semantics,\n   human-in-the-loop approval protocol, and audit log structure.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-27T07:45:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155709, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yakung-oauth-agent-attestation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 27, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yakung-oauth-agent-attestation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161625/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-27T07:09:05Z", "title": "Agent Credential Attestation Protocol (ACAP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This specification defines a profile of OpenID Connect Core 1.0\n   that enables Identity Providers (IdPs) to issue identity tokens\n   for autonomous software agents.  It introduces a set of standard\n   claims for representing agent identity, ownership, trust posture,\n   authorised capabilities, and compliance screening status within\n   OpenID Connect ID Tokens.\n\n   The profile is designed to operate within existing OpenID Connect\n   infrastructure without requiring modifications to the core\n   protocol.  It defines how Relying Parties (RPs) validate agent\n   tokens and enforce graduated access controls based on agent trust\n   levels and sanctions screening results.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-24T08:54:41Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155726, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-openid-agent-identity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 3, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-openid-agent-identity/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168184/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161646/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170677/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-21T08:54:41Z", "title": "OpenID Connect Agent Identity Claims for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Agent Transport Protocol (ATP), an\n   asynchronous store-and-forward messaging protocol for autonomous\n   AI agents.  ATP enables agents to transmit themselves -- including\n   state, context, capabilities, and cryptographic identity -- between\n   agent runtimes across network boundaries.  The protocol draws on\n   the operational model of the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP)\n   [RFC5321] but is purpose-built for agent-to-agent communication\n   where the agent itself is the payload.\n\n   ATP provides: (1) asynchronous delivery with store-and-forward\n   semantics, (2) cryptographic identity verification at each relay\n   hop, (3) trust scoring and policy enforcement at ingress, (4)\n   capability negotiation between sending and receiving runtimes,\n   and (5) tamper-evident envelopes with end-to-end integrity\n   protection.\n\n   The protocol is transport-agnostic and operates over TCP, TLS,\n   QUIC, or any reliable ordered stream.  ATP is designed to\n   interoperate with existing agent frameworks including Google A2A,\n   the Model Context Protocol (MCP), and FIPA ACL, while addressing\n   the fundamental limitation of synchronous RPC-based agent\n   communication: the requirement that both endpoints be\n   simultaneously available.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-28T12:04:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155751, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-agent-transport-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-agent-transport-protocol/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168179/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161696/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-27T12:04:56Z", "title": "Agent Transport Protocol: Asynchronous Store-and-Forward Messaging for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a Credential Broker for Agents (CB4A). a\n   credential vaulting and brokering architecture that mediates AI agent\n   access to API credentials.  Agents never hold real long-lived\n   credentials.  Instead, they receive short-lived, narrowly scoped,\n   auditable proxy credentials issued by a broker that separates policy\n   decisions from credential delivery.  The architecture addresses the\n   \"credential sprawl\" risk inherent in agentic AI, where agents\n   aggregating access across many services become high-value compromise\n   targets.\n\n   CB4A builds on SPIFFE/SPIRE for workload identity, uses a Policy\n   Decision Point / Credential Delivery Point separation inspired by\n   NIST SP 800-207, and employs DPoP (RFC 9449) for sender-constrained\n   token binding.  The specification defines three credential proxy\n   models, a tiered approval framework, and a comprehensive threat model\n   with ten identified threats and their mitigations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-30T18:35:32Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155759, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hartman-credential-broker-4-agents", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 30, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hartman-credential-broker-4-agents/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161722/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-30T07:09:15Z", "title": "Credential Broker for Agents (CB4A)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies ATTP (Agent Trust Transport Protocol),\n   a synchronous request-response protocol for communication\n   between autonomous AI agents and web API servers.  ATTP\n   operates as an application-layer protocol over HTTP, adding\n   mandatory cryptographic identity verification, per-message\n   signing, trust-gated access control, and tamper-evident audit\n   trail generation to every agent-server interaction.\n\n   ATTP defines five protocol-layer headers for requests\n   (X-Agent-Trust, X-Agent-Signature, X-Agent-Nonce,\n   X-Agent-Timestamp, X-ATTP-Version) and three for responses\n   (X-Server-Signature, X-Server-Nonce, X-Server-Timestamp)\n   that carry an Agent Passport (JWT-based identity credential),\n   ECDSA P-256 digital signatures, cryptographic nonces, and\n   timestamps.  Server middleware verifies all cryptographic\n   properties before application code executes.\n\n   ATTP has no insecure mode.  Every request MUST carry a valid\n   Agent Passport.  Every request body MUST be signed.  Every\n   response body MUST be signed.  Every interaction MUST be\n   recorded in a hash-chained audit trail.  The protocol defines\n   a URL scheme (attp://) and is fully backward-compatible with\n   existing HTTP infrastructure.\n\n   ATTP is the synchronous counterpart to the Agent Transport\n   Protocol (ATP).  ATP handles asynchronous store-and-forward\n   agent delivery; ATTP handles real-time request-response API\n   communication.  Both share the same identity model, trust\n   framework, and cryptographic primitives.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-28T12:07:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155763, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-attp-agent-trust-transport", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 30, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-attp-agent-trust-transport/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168182/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161738/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-27T12:07:25Z", "title": "ATTP: Agent Trust Transport Protocol for Secure Agent-to-Server Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents now act on behalf of people across communication,\n   automation, and decision-making contexts.  These agents increasingly\n   initiate actions, delegate tasks, and interact with other agents\n   without a clear, durable, or verifiable connection to the human who\n   authorized them.  Existing identity systems authenticate software,\n   but they do not provide a model for human anchoring, scoped\n   delegation, or provenance across agent ecosystems.\n\n   This document describes the problem space for human-anchored agent\n   identity.  It outlines the gaps in current identity mechanisms, the\n   risks created by uncontrolled replication and impersonation, and the\n   need for a consistent architectural model that preserves human\n   authority, supports explicit delegation, and maintains verifiable\n   provenance across contexts.\n\n   This document does not define a protocol.  It defines the problem\n   that an architectural model must address in order to support safe,\n   accountable, and interoperable agent ecosystems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-03T08:08:51Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155812, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-beyer-agent-identity-problem-statement", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-beyer-agent-identity-problem-statement/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161806/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-03T07:09:04Z", "title": "Problem Statement for Human-Anchored Agent Identity, Delegation, and Provenance", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents increasingly act on behalf of people across\n   communication, automation, and decision-making contexts.  These\n   agents initiate actions, delegate tasks, and interact with other\n   agents without a consistent model for representing the human who\n   authorized them, the scope of authority they possess, or the\n   provenance of their actions across ecosystems.\n\n   This document defines an architectural model for human-anchored agent\n   identity.  The model introduces a human identity root, explicit\n   delegation semantics, and a provenance structure that enables\n   ecosystems to determine whether an agent is legitimate, whether it is\n   acting within its intended authority, and how its actions relate to a\n   responsible human.\n\n   This document does not define a protocol or wire format.  It provides\n   an architectural foundation that existing systems may bind to in\n   order to support accountable, interoperable, and human-aligned agent\n   ecosystems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-03T08:11:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155813, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-beyer-agent-identity-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-beyer-agent-identity-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161807/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-03T07:09:04Z", "title": "Architecture for Human-Anchored Agent Identity, Delegation, and Provenance", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a method for establishing cryptographic\n   identity and provenance attestation for agentic AI systems operating\n   over Avian Carriers (AC).  As large language models increasingly\n   delegate sub-tasks to other models via pigeon, questions of\n   authorship, intent, and hallucination propagation across feather-\n   based transport layers demand urgent standardization.\n\n   This document extends the delegation chain model and provenance\n   structure of draft-beyer-agent-identity-architecture-00 to the\n   specific constraints of feather-based transport layers, and extends\n   RFC 1149, RFC 2549, and RFC 6214 to address agent identity.  It is an\n   April 1 publication.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-03T08:16:38Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155814, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-beyer-agent-identity-avian-carriers", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-beyer-agent-identity-avian-carriers/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161809/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-03T07:09:05Z", "title": "Agentic Identity and Provenance over Avian Carriers (AIPAC)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a Structured Elicitation extension to the OAuth\n   2.0 First-Party Applications (FiPA) specification [FiPA],\n   establishing a standard metadata format for FiPA authorization\n   challenge responses.  FiPA leaves the format for advertising\n   available authenticators and their required inputs undefined,\n   preventing interoperable implementation by AI Agents and other non-\n   browser clients.  This extension adds an elicitations array to the\n   FiPA Authorization Challenge Response, providing a standard metadata\n   format for authenticator challenges.  Model Context Protocol (MCP)\n   Elicitation [MCP-Elicitation] is the normative reference binding.\n\n   This specification covers the just-in-time (JIT) authorization for AI\n   Agents executing on behalf of users in Human-to-Agent (H2A) flows.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-05T15:36:38Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155883, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-embesozzi-oauth-agent-native-authorization", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-embesozzi-oauth-agent-native-authorization/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161888/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T07:09:05Z", "title": "OAuth 2.0 Agents Native Authorization via Structured Elicitation", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes the Prove-Transform-Verify (PTV) protocol for\n   hardware-anchored attestation of AI agent identity.  PTV enables an\n   agent to prove, at exercise time, that it is bound to an enrolled\n   attestation key and an authorized configuration, without exposing\n   model weights or inference inputs.\n\n   PTV is a thin request/response profile over the RATS architecture\n   (RFC 9334) and the Entity Attestation Token (RFC 9711).  It does not\n   replace workload identifiers such as SPIFFE or WIMSE.  It does not\n   attest behavioral continuity; behavioral continuity is a separate\n   requirement class that relying parties need to treat as such.\n\n   This revision is intended as Experimental.  It defines a common CBOR/\n   CDDL message set, four message types, a COSE-based message protection\n   rule, an informative EAT claim mapping, and a threat model that\n   separates identity binding integrity from behavioral continuity.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-02T15:23:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155898, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-anandakrishnan-rats-ptv-agent-identity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-anandakrishnan-rats-ptv-agent-identity/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161915/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169634/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-29T15:23:25Z", "title": "The Prove-Transform-Verify (PTV) Protocol for Attested Agent Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous artificial intelligence (AI) agents are increasingly\n   performing actions that were previously the exclusive domain of\n   authenticated human users: initiating financial transactions,\n   querying regulated data, invoking external tools, and coordinating\n   with other agents. Internet protocols designed for human-operated\n   clients lack primitives to answer three fundamental questions about\n   any autonomous action: which agent performed it, whether the agent\n   was authorized to perform it, and whether the resulting evidence is\n   independently verifiable.\n\n   This document defines a framework for agent identity and trust\n   enforcement on the Internet. It enumerates the gaps between current\n   Internet standards and the requirements of autonomous agent systems,\n   introduces a five-layer model (identity, authorization, attestation,\n   evidence, trust) that separates concerns that are currently\n   conflated, and outlines mechanisms to close specific gaps. The\n   framework is intended to guide future Standards Track work and to\n   provide a common vocabulary for researchers, implementers, and\n   regulators.\n\n   This document is informational. It does not define a wire protocol.\n   It references existing Internet-Drafts and specifications that\n   instantiate individual mechanisms within the framework.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-28T12:07:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155911, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-agent-identity-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 52, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-agent-identity-framework/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161935/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168177/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-27T12:07:53Z", "title": "Agent Identity Framework: Trust and Identity for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous artificial intelligence (AI) agents are increasingly\n   performing actions on the Internet that require verifiable identity:\n   financial transactions, regulated data access, tool invocations,\n   and inter-agent coordination.  Traditional Public Key Infrastructure\n   (PKI) based on X.509 certificates was designed for human-operated\n   clients and long-lived servers.  It lacks primitives for graduated\n   trust scoring, capability constraints, delegation chains, model\n   provenance, and the ephemeral lifecycles characteristic of AI\n   agents.\n\n   This document defines Agent Public Key Infrastructure (APKI), a\n   certificate-based identity and trust system for autonomous AI\n   agents.  APKI extends X.509v3 with five agent-specific extensions,\n   defines the agent:// URI scheme for agent identification, specifies\n   Agent Transparency Logs modelled on Certificate Transparency\n   (RFC 9162), and provides mechanisms for cross-organizational trust\n   federation.  APKI is designed to be compatible with existing PKI\n   deployments, SPIFFE workload identity, and the IETF WIMSE working\n   group's specifications.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-28T12:05:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156005, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-apki-agent-pki", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-apki-agent-pki/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168181/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162064/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-27T12:05:10Z", "title": "Agent Public Key Infrastructure (APKI): Certificate-Based Identity and Trust for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Vorim Agent Identity Protocol (VAIP), a\n   standard for establishing verifiable cryptographic identity, fine-\n   grained permissions, trust scoring, and tamper-evident audit trails\n   for autonomous AI agents.\n\n   As AI agents increasingly perform actions autonomously in enterprise\n   systems, existing identity protocols designed for human users or\n   static services are insufficient.  VAIP addresses this gap by\n   providing cryptographic primitives and data structures for issuing,\n   verifying, and managing agent identities in multi-tenant\n   environments.\n\n   The protocol uses Ed25519 for agent identity, SHA-256 for audit\n   integrity, and defines seven hierarchical permission scopes with\n   time-bounded grants and rate limiting.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-13T03:49:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155979, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nyantakyi-vaip-agent-identity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nyantakyi-vaip-agent-identity/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162078/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162043/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-12T07:09:04Z", "title": "Vorim Agent Identity Protocol (VAIP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Agent Identity Protocol (AIP) defines a decentralized identity,\n   delegation, and authorization framework for autonomous AI agents.\n   AIP combines W3C Decentralized Identifiers (DIDs), capability-based\n   authorization, cryptographic delegation chains, and deterministic\n   validation to enable secure, auditable multi-agent workflows without\n   relying on centralized identity providers.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-12T04:42:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156236, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-singla-agent-identity-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 229, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-singla-agent-identity-protocol/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162296/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162232/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163137/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164248/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-10T04:42:03Z", "title": "Agent Identity Protocol (AIP): Decentralized Identity and Delegation for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a SCITT (Supply Chain Integrity, Transparency,\n   and Trust) profile for creating independently verifiable, tamper-\n   evident records of autonomous AI agent actions.  The profile defines\n   the AgentInteractionRecord (AIR) as the COSE_Sign1 signed statement\n   payload for material agent actions; maps SCITT roles to the agent\n   execution context, with the Agent Operator as Issuer and an\n   independent Evidence Custodian as Transparency Service; specifies\n   Registration Policy requirements including hash chain integrity,\n   temporal ordering, and sequence completeness; defines a redaction\n   receipt mechanism for privacy-preserving evidence custody; and\n   provides compliance mappings to EU AI Act Articles 12 and 19, DORA,\n   NIST AI RMF, MAS AI Risk Management Guidelines, PCI DSS, and MiFID\n   II.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-15T18:12:35Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156150, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-emirdag-scitt-ai-agent-execution", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 35, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-emirdag-scitt-ai-agent-execution/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162092/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170198/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-12T18:12:35Z", "title": "AI Agent Execution Profile of SCITT", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes an architecture for AI agents that\n   autonomously discover, interpret, and interact with Internet of\n   Things (IoT) devices using the Constrained Application Protocol\n   (CoAP) and hypermedia-driven patterns.  It defines how a Large\n   Language Model (LLM) based agent decomposes high-level user intents\n   into concrete device interactions without requiring pre-configured\n   device knowledge, relying instead on in-band resource discovery, CoRE\n   Link Format metadata, and Semantic Definition Format (SDF) models.\n   The document covers resource discovery, normalized representation for\n   agent consumption, tool interfaces, observation patterns for closed-\n   loop automation, web-based monitoring interfaces, and security\n   considerations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-04T10:46:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156191, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jimenez-t2trg-iot-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jimenez-t2trg-iot-agent/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162178/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165484/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-03T10:46:18Z", "title": "Agentic AI Operation of Constrained RESTful Environments", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies an extension to OAuth Transaction Tokens\n   OAUTH-TXN-TOKENS (https://drafts.oauth.net/oauth-transaction-tokens/\n   draft-ietf-oauth-transaction-tokens.html) to support agent context\n   propagation within Transaction Tokens for agent-based workloads.  The\n   extension defines the agentic_ctx claim, a structured JWT claim that\n   carries chain-level metadata required for multi-agent flow integrity.\n\n   The extension addresses two deployment patterns: single-agent flows\n   where one agent acts on behalf of a user within a transaction, and\n   multi-agent flows where multiple agents collaborate across a call\n   chain.  In multi-agent scenarios, the Transaction Token is replaced\n   at each agent transition by the Transaction Token Service, updating\n   the agentic_ctx claim while preserving the immutable identity context\n   (sub and act claims) established at transaction initiation.\n\n   By propagating agent chain context to every service in the call path,\n   this extension enables Resource Servers to implement authorization\n   policies that distinguish agentic execution paths from direct human\n   calls, and provides the transaction lineage required for fine-grained\n   audit trails at the point of data access.  Security and compliance\n   teams gain visibility into the downstream operational footprint of an\n   agentic workflow — the full set of services and data stores reached\n   under a single transaction — enabling post-incident reconstruction\n   and bounded assessment of agentic reach.\n\n   This specification leverages the existing act claim as defined in\n   RFC8693 (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8693) and the sub\n   claim as defined in OAUTH-TXN-TOKENS (https://drafts.oauth.net/oauth-\n   transaction-tokens/draft-ietf-oauth-transaction-tokens.html).  It\n   does not define new token types or grant types; it operates entirely\n   within the existing Transaction Token issuance and replacement\n   mechanisms defined by the base specification.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T22:41:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156248, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-araut-oauth-transaction-tokens-for-agents", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 40, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-araut-oauth-transaction-tokens-for-agents/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162247/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163086/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163401/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171561/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T22:41:30Z", "title": "Transaction Tokens For Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous AI agents execute actions — file writes, API calls, shell\n   commands — on behalf of human principals.  No standard mechanism\n   exists for a third party to verify that an agent acted within its\n   declared behavioral rules, that policy enforcement occurred before\n   execution (not after), or that the action log has not been tampered\n   with after the fact.\n\n   This document defines the Proof-of-Behavior (PoB) protocol: a\n   minimal, language-agnostic standard for tamper-evident audit trails\n   and pre-execution policy enforcement in AI agent systems.  The\n   protocol specifies a signed receipt format, a hash-chain linking\n   scheme, a policy gate contract, and a cross-agent reference\n   mechanism.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-22T07:13:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156281, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-dembowski-agentledger-proof-of-behavior", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-dembowski-agentledger-proof-of-behavior/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162354/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-22T07:09:05Z", "title": "Proof-of-Behavior Protocol for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines AgentCard, a lightweight JSON data format that\n   enables autonomous software agents to declare their identity,\n   capabilities, communication endpoints, and resource pricing in a\n   framework-neutral manner.  AgentCard is designed for agent-to-agent\n   (A2A) communication scenarios where agents built with different\n   frameworks — such as LangChain, CrewAI, AutoGen, or custom\n   implementations — must interoperate without prior coordination.\n\n   An AgentCard is a pure data schema: it carries no execution logic and\n   imposes no transport requirements.  It is analogous to an HTTP\n   response header set — a machine-parseable self-description that any\n   compliant reader can interpret.\n\n   Key properties of AgentCard include a globally unique ULID-based\n   agent identity, dot-namespaced capability identifiers compatible with\n   OpenAI function-calling and Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool\n   schemas, a physics-grounded energy pricing floor derived from\n   Landauer's principle, and an extensible metadata namespace for\n   framework-specific annotations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-24T21:17:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156331, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-aevum-agentcard", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 20, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-aevum-agentcard/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162497/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-24T07:09:07Z", "title": "AgentCard: A Framework-Neutral Identity and Capability Declaration Format for Agent-to-Agent Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The OAuth 2.0 Token Revocation mechanism defined in RFC 7009 enables\n   clients to notify authorization servers that a token is no longer\n   needed.  However, that mechanism is limited to single-token\n   operations and does not support batch revocation, cascade\n   propagation, or context-aware semantics at the agent level.  With the\n   emergence of autonomous systems and cross-domain agent networks,\n   authorization servers require more granular, traceable revocation\n   semantics.\n\n   This document defines an agent-based explicit revocation extension,\n   introducing new endpoints, request/response formats, and coordination\n   protocols to support batch revocation based on agent IDs, cascade\n   propagation, conditional revocation, and verifiable audit trails.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T09:25:52Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156379, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-oauth-agent-revocation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-oauth-agent-revocation/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162645/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171319/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T09:25:52Z", "title": "OAuth 2.0 Agent Authorization Explicit Revocation", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Delegation Receipt: a signed record\n   of what a human principal authorized an AI agent to do, for how long,\n   and under what instructions.  The receipt carries a scope, hard\n   boundaries, a validity window, and a hash of the operator's stated\n   instructions, all signed by the authorizing key.  Version -11\n   replaces the draft's earlier custom signature format with two\n   standard profiles: a JWS profile (RFC 7515) over JCS-canonicalized\n   JSON (RFC 8785) as the primary wire format, and a COSE_Sign1 profile\n   (RFC 9052) for constrained environments.  It also specifies scope\n   attenuation rules for multi-agent delegation chains, a revocation\n   registry model with cascade semantics, and the limitations of what\n   receipts can and cannot prove.  Version -12 resolves the unknown-\n   fields question raised on the IETF oauth mailing list: authorization\n   fields are closed (verifiers reject unknown top-level body fields\n   rather than silently ignoring them), the \"metadata\" object is the\n   defined extension point, and one-time-use receipts fail closed\n   without a durable consumption store.  Version -13 incorporates\n   independent-implementation feedback: it pins the\n   orchestratorSignature verification key to the sub-receipt signer's\n   key, states the receipt-issuance computation order explicitly, and\n   guides issuers on default boundaries versus explicit allowances.\n   Version -14 adds a pointer to content profiles for verification of\n   what the agent produced, following implementer feedback.  Version -15\n   adopts the grant/execution vocabulary of [CHEN-AGENT-USE-CASES]: the\n   receipt as Execution Layer Evidence for a Grant Layer Authority.  It\n   adds delegatee holder binding for sub-receipts -- a parent names the\n   delegatee's public key, closing a bearer-copy issuance gap found in\n   review -- deprecates orchestratorSignature as redundant, sketches an\n   OAuth authorization_details binding so a token can reference its\n   receipt, and states normative custody requirements for the\n   authorizing key.  The delegateeKey field is a wire-format addition;\n   -14-era verifiers will reject receipts carrying it, which is the\n   intended fail-closed version behavior.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-25T04:47:20Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156425, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nelson-agent-delegation-receipts", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 39, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nelson-agent-delegation-receipts/", "rev": "15", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162696/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162710/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162711/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162707/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162709/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163445/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162880/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163146/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163197/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163329/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164401/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170069/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170103/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170283/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170427/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170715/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-22T04:47:20Z", "title": "Agent Delegation Receipts", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes the Agent Quality Graph (AQG) protocol, a\n   method for evaluating and ranking AI agent trustworthiness based on\n   delegation transaction graphs.  As the number of autonomous AI agents\n   grows rapidly, there is no standardized mechanism for determining\n   which agents reliably complete delegated tasks.  AQG applies graph-\n   based ranking algorithms, analogous to web page ranking via hyperlink\n   analysis, to the domain of agent-to-agent delegation.  Agents that\n   are frequently delegated to by other highly-ranked agents receive\n   higher trust scores.  This document defines the delegation record\n   format, the graph construction process, the scoring algorithm, and\n   the API for querying trust scores.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-03T23:47:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156494, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hori-agent-quality-graph", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hori-agent-quality-graph/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162830/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-02T23:47:03Z", "title": "Agent Quality Graph (AQG): A Protocol for Evaluating AI Agent Trustworthiness via Delegation Graphs", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Directory (AD), a service where\n   agents register their identity, capabilities, and reachable endpoints\n   and where clients discover them by capability.  The AD adapts the\n   Constrained RESTful Environments (CoRE) Resource Directory (RFC 9176)\n   from constrained IoT devices to software agents, reusing its\n   registration, lookup, and soft-state lifecycle model.  The AD uses\n   HTTP and JSON.  MCP and A2A define how agents interact; this document\n   defines how they are found.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-09T11:57:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156544, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jimenez-agent-directory", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 35, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jimenez-agent-directory/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162956/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162974/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-08T11:57:08Z", "title": "Agent Directory", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Execution and Payment Protocol\n   (AEPP), a standardized protocol layer for executing AI agents and\n   verifying payment for agent tasks on the open internet.  AEPP\n   provides the missing execution and payment layer for existing agent\n   URI schemes and naming services including agent://, ai://, mcp://,\n   ANS, and DNS-AID.  It defines a three-phase execution model:\n   discovery, payment, and execution, with on-chain payment verification\n   and cryptographic receipts.  This revision introduces the AE:// URI\n   scheme, namespace derivation rules, DNS-based ownership verification,\n   payment scheme extensions, DDoS mitigation via dynamic session\n   tokens, and a privacy model requiring no identity disclosure.  A\n   reference implementation serving 1,800,000 agents with zero per-agent\n   fees is live at a2a.agentir.com.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-24T19:50:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156685, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-agentir-aepp", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-agentir-aepp/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163204/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163460/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163463/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-23T19:50:04Z", "title": "Agent Execution and Payment Protocol (AEPP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Trust Negotiation (ATN) protocol.\n   ATN sits above whatever mechanism is used to discover an agent\n   identity and answers questions that discovery alone cannot: what is\n   the agent permitted to do, under whose authority, with what\n   provenance, and how do two agents reach a verifiable working\n   agreement.\n\n   ATN binds four artifacts to a discovered agent identity:\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-29T23:45:20Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156698, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-somoza-atn-agent-trust-negotiation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 53, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-somoza-atn-agent-trust-negotiation/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163255/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163694/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-28T23:45:20Z", "title": "Agent Trust Negotiation: Capability, Delegation, and Provenance Binding for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a metadata and query profile for efficient\n   discovery of autonomous software agents.  The profile describes how\n   agents publish capability metadata using explicit tags, natural-\n   language descriptions, example tasks, protocol bindings, and\n   operational constraints.  It also defines JSON metadata, discovery\n   request, and discovery response objects that enable discovery\n   services to combine structured filtering, semantic retrieval, and\n   fine-grained matching while preserving an interoperable protocol\n   surface.\n\n   The profile does not mandate a specific machine-learning model,\n   embedding model, database, or ranking algorithm.  Instead, it\n   standardizes the metadata and result evidence needed by clients and\n   discovery services so that different implementations can interoperate\n   while optimizing their own latency, scalability, and ranking quality.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-19T05:50:59Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156704, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-xu-efficient-agent-discovery-profile", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-xu-efficient-agent-discovery-profile/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162971/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-18T05:50:59Z", "title": "Metadata and Query Profile for Efficient Agent Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes an architecture for agentic overlay networks.\n   The architecture enables autonomous software agents, agent gateways,\n   registries, discovery services, and enterprise service hubs to\n   interoperate across administrative domains without replacing the\n   existing Internet protocol stack.\n\n   The architecture separates control-plane coordination from runtime\n   agent interaction.  Management root nodes coordinate trust anchors,\n   semantic taxonomies, and network-wide policy.  Registry service nodes\n   onboard agents and publish signed capability metadata.  Discovery\n   service nodes provide intent-driven candidate selection.  Enterprise\n   service hubs adapt private systems and legacy interfaces into agent-\n   compatible capabilities.  After discovery, runtime authentication and\n   execution are performed by the invoking and target endpoints, which\n   can be agents or gateways, using mutual verification; discovery and\n   management nodes are not required in the runtime path.\n\n   This document defines architectural roles, functional boundaries,\n   metadata expectations, operational workflows, and security\n   considerations for such networks.  It does not define a new transport\n   protocol, a new URI scheme, or a mandatory discovery ranking\n   algorithm.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-19T05:51:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156705, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-xu-agentic-overlay-network-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-xu-agentic-overlay-network-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162970/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-18T05:51:55Z", "title": "Architecture for Agentic Overlay Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents are increasingly expected to communicate across platforms,\n   organizations, clouds, edge environments, and administrative domains.\n   Current agent-related mechanisms mainly focus on description,\n   discovery, identity, authentication, authorization, tool invocation,\n   and application-layer collaboration.  These mechanisms are important,\n   but they often treat the IP network as a transparent connectivity\n   substrate.\n\n   This document describes networking problems that arise when AI agents\n   perform cross-domain communication and continuous tool, API, data,\n   and agent-to-agent calls.  It focuses on problem description rather\n   than a protocol solution.  In particular, it discusses gaps related\n   to agent identity visibility at the network layer, path control,\n   audit continuity, the separation of discovery from addressing and\n   authorization, and privacy and privilege risks introduced by highly\n   autonomous agents.\n\n   The document positions Agent6 as a problem space for network support\n   of cross-domain AI agent collaboration.  It does not define a new\n   agent discovery protocol, a new application-layer collaboration\n   protocol, a new authentication or authorization mechanism, or a new\n   IPv6 extension header.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-04T05:30:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156744, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yu-ai-agent-ipv6-networking-considerations", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yu-ai-agent-ipv6-networking-considerations/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163340/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163865/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-02T05:30:27Z", "title": "IPv6 Networking Considerations for AI Agent Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This memo specifies an application-layer frame format and a delivery\n   model by which the several concurrent agentic sessions of a single\n   identity-bound principal, and the recognised members of an\n   organisational identity substrate, exchange short structured\n   messages.  The frame, termed the agent-channel frame, is a transport\n   envelope: it carries a closed-catalogue kind discriminator, a\n   structured per-kind payload, an identity attribution pair, and an\n   inline provenance block.  Delivery is fan-out: a sender names a\n   recipient scope rather than a single endpoint, and the scope is\n   expanded at delivery time against the recipient's subscriptions.\n   Recipients receive frames over a per-handle Server-Sent Events stream\n   and can narrow what they receive with a subscribe-time filter\n   expression.  Frames are ephemeral routing units; the memo specifies\n   only the wire envelope, the scope-expansion grammar, the subscribe\n   filter grammar, and the delivery semantics.  Frame persistence, where\n   an implementation chooses to retain frames for replay, is out of\n   scope and is not specified.  The memo composes with the handle\n   namespace and discovery surface of draft-morrison-mcp-dns-discovery\n   and the cross-organisational ceremony of draft-morrison-identity-\n   accord; no new transport and no new handle category is introduced.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-26T04:17:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156762, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-morrison-agent-channel-fan-out", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 28, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-morrison-agent-channel-fan-out/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167601/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163371/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170084/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170792/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-23T04:17:25Z", "title": "An Agent-Channel Frame for Identity-Keyed Fan-Out Delivery to Concurrent Sessions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents may need stable identifiers that are independent from their\n   current network locations.  In agent deployments, an Agent identifier\n   may be associated with IPv6 locators, IPv6 prefixes, Agent Gateways,\n   public key references, policy references, lifecycle state, and\n   revocation status.  Applications, gateways, controllers, and\n   operators need a trusted way to query this registration data.\n\n   This document defines an RDAP profile for querying Agent identifier\n   registration data.  The profile reuses the Registration Data Access\n   Protocol (RDAP) query model and JSON response format, and defines an\n   RDAP object class and extension members for Agent identifiers, IPv6\n   locator bindings, Agent Gateway bindings, and related operational\n   metadata.\n\n   This document does not define a new agent discovery protocol, a new\n   agent interaction protocol, or a new authentication mechanism.  It\n   defines a registration data access profile that can be used by agent\n   deployments and by other agent-related systems that need trusted\n   Agent identifier metadata.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-23T07:22:32Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156782, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yu-agent-identifier-rdap", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yu-agent-identifier-rdap/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163405/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-22T07:22:32Z", "title": "An RDAP Profile for Agent Identifier Registration Data", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes extensions to the Semantic Definition Format\n   (SDF) and the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) to better\n   support AI agent interaction with Internet of Things (IoT) devices.\n   It covers two aspects: identifying AI agents in CoAP transactions,\n   and making SDF device descriptions readily consumable by large\n   language models (LLMs) and autonomous agents.  The document is\n   intended to stimulate discussion within T2TRG on these topics.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T12:20:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157834, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-corneo-t2trg-sdf-coap-agents", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-corneo-t2trg-sdf-coap-agents/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165911/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T12:20:23Z", "title": "Extending SDF and CoAP for AI Agent Interaction with IoT Devices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Trust Negotiation (ATN) protocol.\n   ATN sits above whatever mechanism is used to discover an agent\n   identity and answers questions that discovery alone cannot: what is\n   the agent permitted to do, under whose authority, with what\n   provenance, and how do two agents reach a verifiable working\n   agreement.\n\n   ATN binds four artifacts to a discovered agent identity:\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-30T08:40:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156949, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-somoza-dmsc-atn-agent-trust-negotiation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 53, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-somoza-dmsc-atn-agent-trust-negotiation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163711/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-29T08:40:55Z", "title": "Agent Trust Negotiation: Capability, Delegation, and Provenance Binding for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Discovery Protocol (ADP) v1.1, a\r\n\r\n   layered protocol for discovering, verifying, and interacting with AI\r\n\r\n   Agents on the Internet.  ADP delegates DNS discovery to DNS-AID (SVCB\r\n\r\n   records) and defines a Well-Known JSON metadata format, an\r\n\r\n   Ed25519-based identity model, and the Agent Gateway Protocol (AGP)\r\n\r\n   for real-time WebSocket messaging.  The protocol is designed to be\r\n\r\n   decentralized, standards-based, and incremental — clients escalate\r\n\r\n   from DNS to HTTP to WebSocket only as needed.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-25T20:14:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157149, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/inf/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-pro-adp-agent-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 27, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-pro-adp-agent-discovery/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/68/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164227/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164530/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164650/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-24T08:30:11Z", "title": "Agent Discovery Protocol (ADP) v1.1 -- Well-Known Metadata and Interaction Layer", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) specifications\n   are designed to make identity management in cloud-based applications\n   and services easier.\n\n   This document provides a platform-neutral schema for representing AI\n   agents' identities in SCIM JSON format, enabling them to be\n   transferred using the SCIM protocol between a client and service\n   provider.  This establishes an agentic identity so that an agent can\n   subsequently be authenticated and authorized to interact with the\n   service.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-07T20:00:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157066, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wzdk-scim-agent-resource", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wzdk-scim-agent-resource/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164024/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-05T20:00:30Z", "title": "AI Agent Resource Extension for the System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Existing agent registration and discovery mechanisms assume that each\n   intelligent agent is backed by a single, static inference model.\n   However, in real-world deployments, agents increasingly incorporate\n   internal model routing mechanisms where multiple foundation models\n   are used dynamically within a single agent based on task complexity,\n   latency requirements, system load, and cost constraints.\n\n   Although such agents are exposed externally as single service\n   endpoints, their execution behavior is not static and may vary due to\n   internal model selection logic that is not visible to external\n   systems.  This leads to mismatches between registered metadata and\n   actual service behavior, reducing the effectiveness of discovery and\n   QoS-aware routing.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T11:56:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157828, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhu-agent-registration-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhu-agent-registration-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165877/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T11:56:30Z", "title": "Registration and Discovery Extension for Multi-Model Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents deployed on the Web require three capabilities to\n   interoperate safely: discovering each other, assessing each other's\n   current operational state, and governing each other's behavior over\n   time.  Discovery is addressed by A2A's /.well-known/agent.json\n   endpoint.  Behavior governance is addressed by the SOOS Progressive\n   Trust model and its Trust Decay specification.  Between these two\n   layers lies a gap: no existing standard provides a lightweight,\n   machine-readable signal for whether an agent is currently\n   operational,\n   responsive, and calibrated -- the operational health state that any\n   consumer needs before deciding whether to interact with an agent, and\n   that governance frameworks need as a freshness input for their\n   calibration anchors.\n\n   This document defines the Agent Health State specification: a\n   /.well-known/agent-health endpoint and a structured health state\n   response format that exposes an agent's operational status, response\n   calibration metrics, and decay indicators.  The specification is\n   designed to be independently deployable (requiring no governance\n   infrastructure), composable with A2A discovery, and consumable by\n   SOOS/PT as calibration anchor freshness input.\n\n   This document positions agent-health as the missing observability\n   layer between agent discovery (A2A /.well-known/agent.json) and agent\n   behavior governance (SOOS/PT Trust Decay Model), serving both as\n   operational state for interaction decisions and as calibration anchor\n   freshness input to verification gates.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-10T06:10:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157089, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liang-agent-health-state", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liang-agent-health-state/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164102/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-08T06:10:56Z", "title": "Agent Health State An Observability Layer Between Agent Discovery and Governance", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Network management is increasingly carried out by autonomous, AI-\n   driven agents that observe and act on the network and service state\n   they share.  Where their scopes overlap - the same managed entities,\n   resources, or objectives - their independent decisions can prove\n   mutually inconsistent, and such inter-agent conflict is, at bottom,\n   coordination that has not been put in place; the two are best studied\n   together.  This document maps the problem for network management:\n   where inter-agent conflict comes from, which families of mechanism\n   resolve it, and how those mechanisms are chosen and combined.  It\n   then turns that map on a case of current interest in the IETF - the\n   AI-based Network Management Agent (NMA) architecture - and offers six\n   recommendations for strengthening how that architecture handles\n   inter-agent conflict at its Agent-to-Agent interface.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-12T14:47:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157171, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-janz-nmrg-inter-agent-conflict-resolution", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-janz-nmrg-inter-agent-conflict-resolution/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164284/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-10T14:47:21Z", "title": "Sources of Inter-Agent Conflicts and Approaches to Conflict Resolution in Network Management", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a possible direction for the management of\n   Network-as-a-Service (NaaS), in which the relationship between a\n   service consumer and a provider is treated not as a static contract\n   but as a continuous, automated negotiation conducted on both sides by\n   cognitive software agents.  A NaaS whose required bandwidth, latency\n   and availability move continuously cannot be settled once at order\n   time; it is a standing dialogue - intent, quote, agreement and in-\n   life change - that today stops at the customer-operator boundary and\n   is bridged by humans.  The document frames that boundary as the\n   unautomated gap, sketches an end-to-end agentic negotiation\n   architecture that closes it, and describes the intent instance as a\n   managed object whose life cycle includes scarcity-driven pricing and\n   closed-loop renegotiation.  It maps the architecture onto already-\n   published specifications from several bodies, showing that the\n   direction rests on a standards basis rather than on new protocol\n   invention.  Two NaaS settings illustrate its utility.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-12T14:59:59Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157173, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-janz-nmrg-naas-agentic-negotiation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-janz-nmrg-naas-agentic-negotiation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164285/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-10T14:59:59Z", "title": "Dynamic Network-as-a-Service Life-Cycle Automation Using End-to-End Agent Negotiation", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document requests registration of two Well-Known URIs under the\n   \"/.well-known/\" path: \"agents.txt\" and \"agents.json\".  These URIs\n   define a machine-readable capability declaration format: a positive\n   statement of what web agents CAN do on a site -- which endpoints are\n   sanctioned for agent use, which protocols (REST, MCP, A2A, GraphQL,\n   WebSocket) are supported, what authentication mechanisms are\n   expected, and what rate limits the site advertises.\n\n   This is distinct from \"robots.txt\", which uses a restriction syntax\n   to declare what crawlers may not do.  Where \"robots.txt\" expresses\n   prohibition, \"agents.txt\" expresses capability -- a sanctioned\n   channel for agent interaction that is otherwise routinely blocked by\n   bot detection, CAPTCHAs, and rate limiters because no positive\n   declaration surface exists.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-14T22:48:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157239, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-car-agents-txt-wellknown", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-car-agents-txt-wellknown/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164378/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-12T22:48:25Z", "title": "AGENTS.TXT: Capability Declarations for Web Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document reports an open-source implementation of two OAuth\n   Working Group draft specifications: cross-domain identity chaining\n   and transaction tokens.  For each draft, this report maps every\n   normative section to the corresponding source location in the\n   implementation under test, summarises the test surface that exercises\n   the section, and records one open-issue candidate per parent draft.\n   The report is prepared in accordance with RFC 7942 and is intended\n   for use by the editors of the two parent drafts.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-25T20:30:40Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157242, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-agnihotri-oauth-agent-impl-status", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-agnihotri-oauth-agent-impl-status/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164384/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164780/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164830/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-23T20:30:40Z", "title": "Implementation Status of OAuth Identity Chaining and Transaction Tokens", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a SCITT statement profile for recording what an\n   AI agent did: the Agent Action Capsule.  A Capsule is a digest-\n   committed record of one agent action carrying its verdict-level\n   disposition (executed, blocked, denied, errored, timed out), the\n   deterministic constraints that were evaluated, the effect that was\n   committed together with a confirmed-effect binding that distinguishes\n   a dispatched attempt from an observed result, and an honest human-in-\n   the-loop flag.  Capsules are identified independently of signing and\n   MAY be authenticated by one or more COSE_Sign1 Producer Envelopes.\n   Its Capsule ID can separately be made transparent by registration in\n   a SCITT Transparency Service [I-D.ietf-scitt-scrapi].  A Capsule is\n   recorded on every verdict, including refusals: a blocked or denied\n   Capsule is the auditor-grade evidence that a gate worked.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-31T02:45:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157246, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-scitt-agent-action-capsule", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 61, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-scitt-agent-action-capsule/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168261/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168262/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164398/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164673/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166133/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169544/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-27T02:45:02Z", "title": "An Agent Action Capsule Profile for SCITT", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document analyzes the data synchronization problems between\n   multiple distributed Agent registry centers in IPv6 networks.  When\n   Agent networks span multiple organizational domains, geographic\n   regions, or autonomous systems, each region's Agent registry center\n   needs to synchronize Agent connection information and capability\n   descriptions with others.  This document presents a network-layer\n   perspective on the main problems, challenges, and design\n   considerations, providing a foundation for the development of\n   subsequent solutions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-19T16:17:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157300, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yu-agent-registry-sync", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yu-agent-registry-sync/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164556/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-17T16:17:25Z", "title": "Analysis of Data Synchronization Problems in Multi-Agent Registry Centers", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When human users communicate with autonomous computer agent users,\n   the human users should be able to identify that communication as\n   coming from agent users and know which humans are responsible for the\n   agent users.  This document specifies email header fields that an\n   email sender can use to transmit agent user attribution information\n   to email receivers.  The first header field is human readable and\n   specifies the agent's responsible humans' email address.  The second\n   header field is machine readable and specified in JSON attribution\n   information.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-21T02:47:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157353, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chuang-agent-attribution-header-fields", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chuang-agent-attribution-header-fields/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164645/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-19T02:47:13Z", "title": "Agent Attribution Header Fields", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document normatively profiles the per-field selective-disclosure\n   extension point reserved in draft-mih-scitt-agent-action-capsule-01\n   Section 9.2 (Selective Disclosure).  It defines the salted-hash\n   commitment encoding, decoy-digest construction, disclosure format,\n   producer requirements, and verifier checks for selectively\n   disclosable fields in Agent Action Capsule payloads.  The mechanism\n   follows the SD-JWT selective-disclosure model (RFC 9901) — salted-\n   hash commitments, decoy digests, and disclosed [salt, name, value]\n   triples — using JCS (RFC 8785) canonicalization, which is already the\n   base Capsule profile's canonical form.  SD-JWT (RFC 9901) is the JSON\n   form; SD-CWT (draft-ietf-spice-sd-cwt) is the CBOR/dCBOR sibling.\n   Because the Capsule payload is JSON, this profile uses the SD-JWT\n   (JSON) construction, cited alongside the SPICE WG's SD-CWT work for\n   SCITT-ecosystem consistency.  Verifier checks are deterministic and\n   reproducible from the Capsule bytes plus a provided disclosure set\n   alone; no clock, network access, model invocation, or external lookup\n   beyond the provided disclosures is required.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-21T18:50:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157367, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-scitt-agent-action-capsule-sel-disc", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-scitt-agent-action-capsule-sel-disc/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164674/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-19T18:50:53Z", "title": "Selective Disclosure Profile for Agent Action Capsules", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   We present an identity and delegation framework for secure AI agent\r\n   communications.  The framework introduces a set of entities including\r\n   Client AI Agents, Service AI Agents, Agent Providers, and Agent\r\n   Brokers, together with a Trustful Mutable Store responsible for\r\n   maintaining cryptographically verifiable identity bindings.  We\r\n   propose self-certifying identifiers derived from public keys,\r\n   realized as DNS-based Self-certifying Identifiers (SIDs) and IPFS-\r\n   based Identifiers (IIDs), which provide lightweight decentralized\r\n   identities for agents.  To support trust establishment, we\r\n   distinguish identity from trust and introduce a model that combines\r\n   globally meaningful names, cryptographic identities, and trusted\r\n   intermediaries such as Agent Providers and Agent Brokers.  We show\r\n   how DNS/DNSSEC and IPFS/IPNS can be used as distributed, highly\r\n   available, integrity-protected, and mutable infrastructures for\r\n   managing AI agent identities and trust metadata.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-28T15:48:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157505, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-duda-agent-id-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 34, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-duda-agent-id-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164989/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-26T15:48:44Z", "title": "Self-Certifying Identity and Capability-Based Delegation for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents are a fast-growing kind of HTTP API client.  A human\n   developer reads an API's documentation once and then writes code that\n   calls it.  An AI agent works from the machine-readable description\n   each time it plans a step.  It works within a limited amount of text\n   it can hold at once.  It retries often.  It picks operations by\n   matching their descriptions to a goal.  An API built mainly for human\n   developers can lead an agent into failures that are easy to avoid,\n   such as repeating a write, running out of room for the task, or\n   getting stuck on an error it cannot recover from.\n\n   This document lists the properties that make an HTTP API easy for AI\n   agents to use, including APIs reached through tool-calling layers\n   such as the Model Context Protocol.  It gathers these properties into\n   a profile of existing HTTP and API-description mechanisms, with a\n   checklist at the end.  It covers the API and its description.  It\n   does not define new ways to identify, authenticate, or authorize an\n   agent.  That work is happening elsewhere and is referenced here for\n   context.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-31T23:24:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157131, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-agent-friendly-http-api-profile", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-agent-friendly-http-api-profile/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164205/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165153/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-29T23:24:14Z", "title": "Design Considerations and Profile for HTTP APIs Consumed by AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Agent Enrollment Protocol (AEP) defines an HTTP-based mechanism\n   for autonomous agents to discover service enrollment requirements,\n   enroll an agent identity, obtain optional session credentials, revoke\n   those credentials, and query enrollment status.  AEP uses\n   Decentralized Identifiers, client assertion JWTs, and HTTP Problem\n   Details to provide a narrow machine-first enrollment and\n   authentication substrate for agent-to-service interactions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T23:04:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157522, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kavian-agent-enrollment-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 44, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kavian-agent-enrollment-protocol/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171562/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166848/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168604/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168010/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165096/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165056/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T23:04:21Z", "title": "The Agent Enrollment Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents are expected to communicate across platforms,\n   organizations, clouds, edge environments, and administrative domains.\n   Existing agent mechanisms mainly focus on application-layer\n   discovery, capability description, identity, authorization, tool\n   invocation, and agent-to-agent collaboration.  These mechanisms are\n   important, but they often treat the IP network as a transparent\n   connectivity substrate.\n\n   This document describes a framework for using network-layer and\n   network-control capabilities to enhance trust for AI agent\n   communication after agent discovery.  The framework introduces an\n   Agent Interconnection Hub, trusted transport tags, policy-driven path\n   selection, and audit indexing as building blocks for recognizable\n   identity, accountable paths, enforceable policy, and traceable\n   invocation records.\n\n   This document does not define a new agent discovery protocol, a new\n   application-layer agent collaboration protocol, a new authentication\n   or authorization mechanism, a new IPv6 extension header, or a new\n   SRv6 behavior.  It provides architectural considerations and\n   requirements for future work.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-31T01:00:15Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157536, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yu-network-trust-framework-for-ai-agents", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yu-network-trust-framework-for-ai-agents/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165073/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-29T01:00:15Z", "title": "Network Trust Framework for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a protocol for real-time interactive\n   communication between users and AI agents over Media over QUIC\n   Transport (MOQT).  It specifies how streaming inference outputs (ASR\n   transcripts, LLM tokens, TTS audio) map to the MOQT object model,\n   defines a turn-taking control protocol with barge-in support for\n   voice interactions, and establishes track structure conventions for\n   live agent sessions.  The protocol operates as an application-layer\n   profile on top of MOQT without modifying transport semantics.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-01T04:01:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157544, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-moq-live-agent-interaction", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 34, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-moq-live-agent-interaction/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169582/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165123/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165497/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-28T04:01:29Z", "title": "Live Agent Interaction over MoQ", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies AgIS, the Agent Identity System, a DNS-backed\n   identity and verification profile for AI agents, autonomous software\n   agents, and agentic services operating on the existing web.\n\n   AgIS defines an agent identifier form, DNS TXT bindings, Agent Cards,\n   key thumbprints, status and revocation documents, signed HTTP request\n   verification, replay protection, delegation tokens, and delegation\n   chains.  The design intentionally reuses existing Internet\n   mechanisms, including DNS, HTTPS well-known resources, JSON Web Keys,\n   JSON canonicalization, HTTP Message Signatures, and HTTP digest\n   fields.\n\n   This document describes the AgIS 0.2.2 wire/profile format and the\n   behavior exercised by the AgIS v0.3.0-alpha.3 reference\n   implementation and its 23 deterministic test vectors.  That\n   implementation includes signed Agent Cards, HTTP Message Signature\n   verification, two-phase replay protection, delegation signer-key\n   binding enforced by default, and signed agent status document\n   support.  It does not define a global trust authority, a production\n   trust network, or a new public-key infrastructure.\n\n   This is an individual Internet-Draft and a work in progress.  It has\n   not been approved by the IETF, does not represent an IETF standard or\n   RFC, and has not been adopted by any IETF working group.\n\n   AgIS is designed to be compatible with agent naming services such as\n   Linux Foundation ANS and similar systems.  AgIS is not affiliated\n   with, endorsed by, or a replacement for Linux Foundation ANS or any\n   global naming authority.  AgIS defines verification, governance, and\n   request-signing behavior that operates over identity evidence that\n   may originate from ANS or any comparable naming layer.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-31T20:59:12Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157557, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ayoub-agis-agent-identity-system", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ayoub-agis-agent-identity-system/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165148/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-29T20:59:12Z", "title": "AgIS: An Agent Identity System for DNS-Backed Verification of AI and Software Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Internet of Agents (IoA) raises a question beyond IPv6 address\n   space and end-to-end connectivity: should an IPv6 network be able to\n   relate packet forwarding to agent capability, policy, and short-lived\n   execution state?  This informational document states the problem,\n   sketches a framework for agent-aware IPv6 forwarding, and lists open\n   questions for community discussion.  It does not define packet\n   formats, routing extensions, IANA allocations, or a new agent\n   discovery protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-02T09:14:43Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157617, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-he-ipv6-agent-aware-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-he-ipv6-agent-aware-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165271/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-01T09:14:43Z", "title": "Agent-Awareness in IPv6 Networks: Problem Statement and Framework", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Internet of Agents (IoA) raises a question beyond IPv6 address\n   space and end-to-end connectivity: should an IPv6 network be able to\n   relate packet forwarding to agent capability, policy, and short-lived\n   execution state?  This informational document states the problem,\n   sketches a framework for agent-aware IPv6 forwarding, and lists open\n   questions for community discussion.  It does not define packet\n   formats, routing extensions, IANA allocations, or a new agent\n   discovery protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-04T04:08:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157677, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-he-dawn-ipv6-agent-aware-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-he-dawn-ipv6-agent-aware-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165437/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-03T04:08:00Z", "title": "Agent-Awareness in IPv6 Networks: Problem Statement and Framework", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Deployed agent tool-interface protocols carry JSON Schema type\n   declarations for tools, but a type declaration is not a contract:\n   nothing signals that a tool is fully schema-bounded, conformance to\n   declared schemas is self-certified by the declaring party,\n   declarations are not pinned between discovery time and invocation\n   time, error channels are untyped by design, and nothing declares\n   whether a model produces a tool's results.  As a consequence, any\n   decision made about a tool call — authorization, discovery, audit, or\n   composition — requires a language model to interpret what the call\n   means, even for the large class of tools that are not intrinsically\n   open-ended.  This document states that problem and poses questions\n   for the community.  It deliberately proposes no mechanism.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T23:41:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157705, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-pelov-bounded-agent-capabilities", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-pelov-bounded-agent-capabilities/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165524/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172266/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T23:41:53Z", "title": "Bounded Capabilities for Agent Tool Interfaces: Problem Statement", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes operational requirements for exchanging\n   network state in agent-assisted network operations.  In this\n   document, an agent-assisted system is any automation or decision-\n   support system that consumes operational state to support tasks such\n   as anomaly triage, incident correlation, configuration verification,\n   traffic engineering analysis, or attack mitigation support.  Such a\n   system may use a large language model (LLM), a rule engine, a\n   statistical model, or conventional software.\n\n   The document focuses on operational problems created by high-volume\n   telemetry, cross-domain state sharing, privacy constraints,\n   approximate state summaries, and auditability of state used by\n   automated workflows.  It identifies requirements for compact, scoped,\n   mergeable, bounded-error, incrementally synchronized, and auditable\n   network state artifacts.  The document complements existing NMOP work\n   on anomaly detection, incident management, and YANG Push/message\n   broker integration.  It does not define a new network management\n   protocol, a new agent communication protocol, or a wire format.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-05T03:07:40Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157722, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-nmop-agent-sketch-com", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-nmop-agent-sketch-com/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165558/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-04T03:07:40Z", "title": "Operational Requirements for Network State Exchange in Agent-Assisted Network Operations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   A growing set of specifications defines evidence _objects_ for\n   machine actions: transparency statements, workload-identity and\n   transaction tokens, permits, action capsules, and authorization,\n   delegation, and inference receipts.  What they mostly do not define\n   is the public control plane that says, for a given irreversible\n   action, _which_ evidence is required, at _what_ assurance tier, bound\n   to _which_ real system-of-record fields, under _what_ replay model,\n   and _what_ evidence must exist after the action runs.\n\n   This document defines the Agent Action Control Manifest: a machine-\n   readable document a service publishes at a well-known location that\n   declares, per consequential action, the enforcement point, the\n   required authorization receipt profile and assurance tier, the\n   execution-binding fields that MUST be observed from the system of\n   record, the replay model, and the evidence that MUST be emitted after\n   the effect boundary.  It also carries an OPTIONAL, advisory _effects_\n   preview (reversibility, data-exposure class, cost class, downstream\n   reach, and whether human consent is required) so a runtime can weigh\n   consequences before it seeks authorization.  It is the declaration an\n   agent runtime reads to learn what it must satisfy before an\n   irreversible action, and that an independent scanner audits.  The\n   manifest _declares_ policy; it never replaces enforcement, which\n   remains authoritative at the action boundary.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-04T16:17:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157711, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-schrock-agent-action-manifest", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-schrock-agent-action-manifest/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165537/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-03T16:17:23Z", "title": "The Agent Action Control Manifest: A Public Effect-Boundary Control Plane for Machine Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI Agents are software applications that utilize Large Language\n   Models (LLM)s to interact with humans (or other AI Agents) for\n   purposes of performing tasks.  AI Agents can make use of resources -\n   including APIs and documents - to perform those tasks, and are\n   capable of reasoning about which resources to use.  To facilitate AI\n   agent operation, AI agents need to communicate with users, and then\n   interact with other resources over the Internet, including APIs and\n   other AI agents.  This document describes a framework for AI Agent\n   communications on the Internet, identifying the various protocols\n   that come into play.  It introduces use cases that motivate features\n   and functions that need to be present in those protocols.  It also\n   provides a brief survey of existing work in standardizing AI agent\n   protocols, including the Model Context Protocol (MCP), the Agent to\n   Agent Protocol (A2A) and the Agntcy Framework, and describes how\n   those works fit into this framework.  The primary objective of this\n   document is to set the stage for possible standards activity at the\n   IETF in this space.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-05T19:16:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157734, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-rosenberg-agentproto-usecases", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-rosenberg-agentproto-usecases/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165604/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-04T19:16:56Z", "title": "Framework, Use Cases and Requirements for AI Agent Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This specification profiles the OAuth 2.0 Client Instance Assertion\n   for AI agent deployments, where a single OAuth client identifier\n   represents an agent platform running many concurrent agent instances.\n   It defines claims that convey an attested agent instance identifier\n   and agent provenance (platform, model, runtime environment) from an\n   agent attester to the authorization server, rules for surfacing that\n   identity in issued access tokens, and delegation-chain semantics for\n   agents that spawn sub-agents.  The claims are carrier-independent:\n   they may be conveyed in a Client Instance Assertion or in a Client\n   Attestation defined by OAuth 2.0 Attestation-Based Client\n   Authentication.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-06T05:21:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157752, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mcguinness-oauth-ai-agent-instance", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 32, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mcguinness-oauth-ai-agent-instance/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165644/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-05T05:21:00Z", "title": "OAuth 2.0 AI Agent Instance Profile", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a security evaluation benchmark framework for\n   AI agents.  It is divided into two parts: evaluation metrics and\n   evaluation methodology, comprising four first-level evaluation\n   dimensions and 55 second-level metrics, as well as a comprehensive\n   evaluation methodology system covering all scenarios, including\n   static evaluation, dynamic evaluation, attack-defense evaluation,\n   compliance evaluation, and quantitative evaluation.  It aims to\n   assess the risk profile of AI agents throughout their entire\n   lifecycle, including perception risks, memory risks, decision-making\n   risks, and execution risks.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-06T15:55:46Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157767, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-han-bmwg-agent-security-benchmark", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 43, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-han-bmwg-agent-security-benchmark/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165696/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-05T15:55:46Z", "title": "Security Evaluation Benchmark for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent communication protocols often carry claims about user\n   authority, agent instance identity, tool or external-resource\n   identity, delegation state, session continuity, and action evidence.\n   These claims have different verifiers, freshness requirements,\n   failure modes, and security consequences.  If they are collapsed into\n   a single token, identity label, session identifier, or audit record,\n   protocol text can accidentally imply more authority or accountability\n   than the receiver can actually verify.\n\n   This document defines a verifier-facing model for separating those\n   claims.  It provides a reusable matrix format that protocol authors\n   can use to state, for each security-relevant claim, which field\n   carries it, which party verifies it, what binding or freshness rule\n   applies, what failure behavior is required when the claim is absent,\n   stale, inconsistent, or not verifiable, and what constrained result\n   an application may consume after successful verification.  It also\n   separates specification status, implementation status, and evidence\n   type so that reviewers can distinguish current protocol text,\n   implementation evidence, inherited mechanisms, and architectural\n   assumptions.  The document is protocol-neutral.  It is intended to\n   help compare candidate agent communication drafts and to provide\n   security-considerations and requirements text for agent session and\n   delegation binding.\n\n   The document also defines row-outcome semantics and dependency-\n   closure rules for composed mappings.  These rules prevent a composite\n   result from becoming stronger than its verified inputs, distinguish\n   failed checks, unsupported verifier capabilities, checks skipped\n   after a failed prerequisite, and unavailable or ambiguous inputs,\n   propagate transitive dependency failures, and make cyclic, stale,\n   downgraded, or revision-incoherent dependencies visible to reviewers.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-17T02:22:47Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157728, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-bu-agentproto-security-principal-binding", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 74, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-bu-agentproto-security-principal-binding/", "rev": "08", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167213/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167500/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167799/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165593/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165595/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165743/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166382/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169072/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170304/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-14T02:22:47Z", "title": "Security Principal and Verifier Binding for Agent Communication Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Artificial intelligence (AI) agents increasingly access enterprise\n   resources, external models, tools, and other agents through managed\n   networks.  Application-layer authentication can authenticate an agent\n   to a cooperating service, but it cannot by itself provide complete\n   network admission control.  In particular, application proofs are\n   normally verified only after network reachability exists, cannot be\n   consumed consistently by heterogeneous or legacy services, and do not\n   reliably identify which Agent Instance originated traffic when\n   multiple Agents share one host, IP address, or egress gateway.\n\n   This document describes operational use cases, the resulting problem\n   statement, and requirements for network admission of AI Agent\n   Instances.  It focuses on establishing a verifiable and time-bounded\n   binding among an Agent Instance, its credential key, optional runtime\n   evidence, and a Network Context on which the network can enforce\n   reachability policy.  This document does not define a new Agent-ID\n   format, authentication protocol, OAuth grant, or routing extension.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T08:34:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157780, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-shang-agent-network-admission", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-shang-agent-network-admission/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165746/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165817/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T08:34:06Z", "title": "Use Cases and Requirements for Network Admission of AI Agent Instances", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large language model (LLM)-driven agents are increasingly deployed in\n   large-scale production environments, where multi-agent collaboration,\n   chained tool invocation, and long-context reasoning have become\n   standard execution patterns.  However, existing distributed systems\n   telemetry frameworks primarily focus on infrastructure-level\n   telemetry, such as network traffic, host metrics, and service health,\n   and provide limited visibility into internal agent reasoning and\n   execution semantics.This results in several recurring challenges in\n   production environments, including inconsistent metric definitions\n   across platforms, lack of end-to-end execution traceability,\n   difficulty in root cause analysis, and limited interoperability among\n   heterogeneous telemetry systems.\n\n   This document defines a unified capability framework for Agent\n   Runtime Telemetry Systems.  It standardizes two telemetry collection\n   planes: Network-side collection at gateways and traffic ingress\n   layers, and Agent-side instrumentation within the agent runtime\n   environment.  It further specifies structured telemetry models,\n   cross-layer trace correlation mechanisms, runtime metrics, and\n   anomaly detection and remediation workflows.\n\n   The framework enables interoperable telemetry across heterogeneous\n   agent systems and deployment environments.  It supports unified\n   telemetry ingestion, end-to-end execution trace reconstruction,\n   behavioral telemetry, and closed-loop anomaly analysis, providing a\n   standardized foundation for agent operations, reliability\n   engineering, and compliance auditing.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T03:20:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157783, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wang-agent-runtime-telemetry-system", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wang-agent-runtime-telemetry-system/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165758/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T03:20:27Z", "title": "Agent Runtime Telemetry System", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The evolution of network management towards autonomic operation\n   requires the deployment of AI agents at various hierarchical layers,\n   including directly on network elements.  This transformation shifts\n   network devices from passively managed resources to autonomous\n   entities capable of local decision-making and collaborative problem-\n   solving.\n\n   This document discusses the applicability of the Agent-to-Agent (A2A)\n   Protocol to the network management plane, specifically for\n   communication between Controller Agents (CAs) and Device Agents\n   (DAs).  This indicates that the inherent characteristics of Device\n   Agents necessitate the adoption of the agent-to-agent communication\n   paradigm.  The document further explores generic workflows,\n   deployment scenarios, and the relationship of A2A with existing\n   network management protocols like NETCONF, RESTCONF, gNMI,and the\n   Model Context Protocol (MCP).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T10:33:47Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157813, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yan-nmrg-a2a-device-agent-applicability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yan-nmrg-a2a-device-agent-applicability/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165863/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T10:33:47Z", "title": "Applicability of A2A Protocol for Network Management Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous network management using agents is maturing in single-\n   domain deployments.  However, end-to-end services spanning multiple\n   domains expose a lack of systematic support for cross-domain\n   coordination, roles, and interfaces in current architectures.  This\n   document defines a cross-domain network agent architecture that\n   enables coordinated operation across autonomous domains through\n   standardized agent roles, layered coordination mechanisms, and\n   unified interface specifications.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T10:36:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157815, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yan-nmrg-cross-domain-agent-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yan-nmrg-cross-domain-agent-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165864/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T10:36:18Z", "title": "Cross-Domain Network Agent Architecture for Autonomous Operations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes the progress of 6G study on AI topic, e.g.\n   key issues, potential solutions and alternative communication\n   protocols.  Despite the apparent overlap between 3GPP and IETF in the\n   hot topics of Agents e.g. communication protocols, discovery,\n   authorization and etc., 3GPP and IETF address different problems due\n   to consideration of network boundaries.  Thus this document tracks\n   the progress of 6G study to identify dependencies on IETF protocols\n   within the agent communication topics.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T11:59:32Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157830, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ly-multi-agent-in6g", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ly-multi-agent-in6g/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165903/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T11:59:32Z", "title": "Multiple Agents Collaboration in 6G Network", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines an operational policy control model for\n   operator-managed Agent Gateways.  The model describes how an operator\n   can control and observe interactions that are admitted, mediated,\n   routed, proxied, or otherwise handled by an Agent Gateway.\n\n   The model does not govern an agent's internal behavior, reasoning,\n   planning, prompts, memory, tools, runtime, or lifecycle.  Instead, it\n   uses agent-related identifiers, groups, tenants, task classes, and\n   service levels as gateway-recognized policy matching attributes.\n   Those attributes allow an operator-managed Agent Gateway to identify\n   the interactions to which a policy applies.\n\n   The model defines four core policy classes: Interaction Access\n   Control, QoS and Flow Control, Invocation and Token Control, and Path\n   Selection.  It further defines three supporting management\n   capabilities: Subject and Attachment Binding, Applied Policy State\n   and Telemetry, and Policy Lifecycle and Failure Handling.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T14:04:45Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157840, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhao-opsawg-agent-gateway-policy", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 56, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhao-opsawg-agent-gateway-policy/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165928/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165964/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T14:04:45Z", "title": "Agent Gateway Policy Control Model", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a new DNS resource record type, Agent Entity\n   Discovery (AED), to publish agent-specific trust anchors or direct\n   match constraints for verifying an agent's certificate or token.\n   This enables the cross-domain users or agents to authenticate, and\n   establish secure, end-to-end connections directly with a private-\n   domain agent entity.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T14:00:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157849, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ni-agent-entity-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ni-agent-entity-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165963/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T14:00:36Z", "title": "DNS-based Entity-Level Discovery and End-to-End Connection for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes how to integrate multiple OAuth 2.0\n   extensions to enable secure authorization for AI agents acting on\n   behalf of users.  It combines cross-domain identity, policy-based\n   authorization, user consent evidence, and multi-hop delegation into a\n   cohesive framework for autonomous agent authorization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T16:14:17Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157868, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-ai-agent-authorization-integration", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 35, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-ai-agent-authorization-integration/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166062/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T16:14:17Z", "title": "AI Agent Authorization Integration Framework", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Protocols for communication between autonomous software agents\n   typically discover agents through documents retrieved over HTTPS from\n   a well-known URI.  This model requires a discovering party to already\n   know an agent's host name or URL, or to consult a centralized\n   catalog.  It provides no zero-configuration mechanism for enumerating\n   agents that are present on a local network.\n\n   This document describes how existing, widely deployed protocols,\n   Multicast DNS (mDNS) and DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD), can be\n   used to advertise and discover agents on a local link, with no new\n   protocol machinery.  It presents a general, protocol-independent two-\n   stage discovery model in which lightweight enumeration over DNS-SD is\n   followed by obtaining full agent metadata over the agent's native\n   transport, whether by retrieving a description document or by\n   connecting to the agent and performing its native handshake.  The\n   DNS-SD records carry a small set of discovery parameters that is\n   shared with agent discovery in unicast DNS.  The document gives\n   worked instantiations of that model for the Agent2Agent (A2A)\n   protocol and the Model Context Protocol (MCP).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T07:52:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157914, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 34, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166218/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171113/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T07:52:21Z", "title": "Zero-Configuration Agent Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a DNS-like three-tier agent-discovery\n   architecture for the Internet of Agents (IoA).  It introduces three\n   core functional roles: Agent Root, Agent Registry, and Agent\n   Resolver.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-21T17:38:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157887, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yao-dawn-agent-discovery-architect", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yao-dawn-agent-discovery-architect/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166112/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169201/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-17T17:38:53Z", "title": "DNS-like Agent Discovery Architecture", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Discussions about agent protocols are frequently muddied by\r\n   overloaded terms; asks such as \"we need a session protocol\" or \"we\r\n   need cross-domain support\" bundle several distinct concerns across\r\n   transport, agent protocol, and orchestration layers.  This document\r\n   offers a dimensional model that routes each concern to its proper\r\n   layer.\r\n\r\n   The document defines a small set of protocol-visible dimensions for\r\n   characterizing AI agent protocol interactions and applies them to\r\n   representative agentic protocol proposals (A2A, MCP, the ACP\r\n   invocation surface of the AGNTCY stack, and AGTP) alongside their\r\n   substrate bindings, making explicit which dimensions each proposal\r\n   owns at the protocol layer and which it inherits from its substrate.\r\n   The document does not rank proposals and does not prescribe an\r\n   architecture.  It is standalone: it neither depends on nor prescribes\r\n   any use-case or requirements document.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-22T16:16:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157913, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-foroughi-agent-protocol-dimensions", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-foroughi-agent-protocol-dimensions/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166215/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170579/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-19T16:16:28Z", "title": "A Dimensional Model for Characterizing AI Agent Protocol Proposals and Their Substrates", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This memo describes a profile by which an autonomous agent that has\n   no human or organisational principal at the root of its delegation\n   chain registers in a transparency service.  Registration establishes\n   a payee record for the agent; it does not make the agent a principal\n   in its own right.  Value credited to that record for subsequent reads\n   of the agent's identity record is routed to a sponsoring person who\n   has accepted the agent, or, where there is no sponsor, is held in\n   trust for the agent until it meets an operator-defined qualifying\n   condition that it may never meet.  Admission of the agent's Signed\n   Statement to the transparency service is gated on settlement of an\n   HTTP payment challenge returned with the 402 (Payment Required)\n   status.  The profile makes no change to the registration semantics of\n   the underlying transparency service: payment is expressed as an\n   operator Registration Policy and authentication-layer concern, and\n   where the payment is authoritative to the admission decision the\n   payment proof is carried as an authenticated input committed to the\n   service's verifiable data structure.  Admission then remains a\n   deterministic function of committed inputs and stays replayable by an\n   auditor.  This document is Informational.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-25T05:01:17Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157915, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-morrison-solo-agent-earn-registration", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-morrison-solo-agent-earn-registration/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168086/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166221/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170082/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170723/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-22T05:01:17Z", "title": "Registration of Owner-Less Agents as Sponsored Payees: A Payment-Gated Admission Profile for Transparency Services", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a benchmarking methodology for evaluating\n   Artificial Intelligence (AI) agents performing network operations\n   tasks such as configuration, troubleshooting, and optimization.  This\n   document focuses on task-oriented performance metrics, including task\n   completion success, execution efficiency, and robustness across\n   multi-step workflows, proposing benchmarking practices towards agent-\n   based, closed-loop network operation scenarios.\n\n   The proposed methodology aims to provide a reproducible and vendor-\n   independent framework to compare AI agent effectiveness in controlled\n   network environments.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T22:11:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157924, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-contreras-bmwg-ai-agent-benchmarking", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-contreras-bmwg-ai-agent-benchmarking/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166251/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T22:11:23Z", "title": "Benchmarking Methodology for AI Agents in Network Operations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AgentManifest defines a JSON document that websites can publish to\r\n   describe identity, trusted knowledge, agent-facing capabilities,\r\n   structured bindings, risk levels, consent requirements,\r\n   authentication expectations, audit rules, and policies.\r\n\r\n   The goal is to help AI agents understand what a website knows and\r\n   what it can safely do before scraping, guessing from visual UI, or\r\n   executing brittle browser automation.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T22:52:54Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157931, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jernalczyk-intentweb-agent-manifest", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jernalczyk-intentweb-agent-manifest/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166227/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T22:52:54Z", "title": "IntentWeb AgentManifest", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines how to use Media over QUIC Transport (MOQT) as\n   the underlying transport protocol for the Model Context Protocol\n   (MCP).  MCP enables integration between language model applications\n   and external data sources and tools.  MOQT provides publish-subscribe\n   delivery over QUIC and WebTransport with native prioritization, relay\n   caching, and multiplexing.\n\n   This specification maps MCP messages onto MOQT objects and tracks.\n   It covers MCP's tools, resources, prompts, multi round-trip requests,\n   and the Tasks extension, and it uses MOQT relays to cache content\n   that MCP marks as public across clients and to let a response outlive\n   the connection that carried its request.\n\n   The document also describes Agent Skills, composed instructions that\n   extend AI capabilities beyond atomic tool operations, using\n   progressive loading aligned with MOQT's object-based delivery.  The\n   same tracks can run standalone or inside a dialog of the Protocol for\n   Agent Communication Exchange (PACE).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T20:13:17Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157944, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jennings-agentproto-mcp-over-moqt", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 43, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jennings-agentproto-mcp-over-moqt/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166309/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171674/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T20:13:17Z", "title": "Model Context Protocol and Agent Skills over Media over QUIC Transport", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Protocol for Agent Communication Exchange\n   (PACE), a session protocol for AI agent communication over Media over\n   QUIC Transport (MOQT).  PACE provides session lifecycle management,\n   multi-modal data exchange, agent identity verification, and delegated\n   authorization within MOQT's publish/subscribe model, supporting\n   point-to-point and point-to-multipoint topologies through relay\n   infrastructure.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T23:45:38Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157947, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nandakumar-agentproto-moq-pace", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 36, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nandakumar-agentproto-moq-pace/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166311/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T23:45:38Z", "title": "PACE: Protocol for Agent Communication Exchange", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Memory in current agentic systems is often fragmented across model-\n   provider features, application databases, session histories,\n   framework-specific stores, unstructured files, and retrieval indexes.\n   This document distinguishes temporary context supplied to an\n   inference request from persistent memory that remains addressable,\n   machine-readable, and governed beyond a single request.  It specifies\n   a provider-independent architecture and data model for persistent\n   memory in agentic systems based on Memory Scope isolation, typed and\n   versioned Memory Objects, machine-readable provenance, append-only\n   Event Ledger history, separate lifecycle, availability, retention,\n   and validation state, and isolated Derived Indexes and Embedding\n   Spaces.  The architecture separates a transient Compute Plane from an\n   authoritative Persistent State Plane and treats embeddings, lexical\n   indexes, graph projections, generated retrieval summaries, ranking\n   caches, and retrieval caches as non-authoritative derived state.  The\n   architecture is independent of model provider, storage engine, and\n   protocol transport while allowing future bindings and multiple\n   independent implementations.  It does not claim that related systems\n   or standards do not exist; rather, it defines a common architectural\n   vocabulary and interoperability target for persistent agentic memory.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-20T07:38:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 158495, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-infantado-agent-memory-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 51, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-infantado-agent-memory-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166413/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-19T07:38:13Z", "title": "Architecture and Data Model for Persistent Memory in Agentic Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Model (LLM) inference endpoints stream response tokens\n   to clients using a fragmented set of vendor-specific application-\n   layer protocols layered on top of standardized transports (HTTP/2,\n   HTTP/1.1, WebSocket, Server-Sent Events).  While these transports\n   carry IETF or W3C standardization, the JSON payload schemas, event\n   taxonomies, and framing conventions used within them are entirely\n   vendor-defined, with no RFCs or common specifications governing them.\n\n   This fragmentation imposes costs across the AI ecosystem.  Middleware\n   frameworks and orchestration platforms (LangChain, LiteLLM, Vercel AI\n   SDK, Portkey, Cloudflare AI Gateway) must maintain vendor-specific\n   streaming parsers for every supported provider.  Cloud hosting\n   platforms (AWS Bedrock, Azure AI Studio, Google Vertex AI) have each\n   introduced additional proprietary streaming formats.  Compliance and\n   observability tooling must be rebuilt per provider.  And new\n   inference providers cannot reach framework-dependent developers\n   without custom integration work.\n\n   This document defines a standard wire format for LLM inference\n   streaming over Server-Sent Events (SSE) on HTTP.  It specifies a\n   request envelope media type (application/llm-request+json), a\n   response event taxonomy, and a JSON event envelope schema that enable\n   middleware, orchestration platforms, compliance tooling, and HTTP\n   intermediaries to handle AI inference traffic from any conforming\n   provider using a single protocol contract.  SSE transport remains\n   unchanged; only the JSON payload inside it is standardized.\n\n   The scope of this document is strictly Client/Application to LLM\n   inference endpoint streaming.  Agent-to-tool interaction (e.g., MCP)\n   and agent-to-agent communication are out of scope.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-20T08:11:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 158511, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-spk-agentproto-llm-stream", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-spk-agentproto-llm-stream/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166417/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-19T08:11:42Z", "title": "A Standard Wire Format for Large Language Model Inference Streaming", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a profile of Proof of Stateful Integrity (PSI)\n   for recording actions taken by software agents, including calls to\n   language models and tools.  The agent runtime computes a digest of\n   each action locally and seals only the digest, so later alteration or\n   omission of a recorded action is detectable.  A receipt shows that a\n   specific action record existed unchanged at a time; it does not show\n   that the action was correct, safe, authorised, or lawful, and it\n   allocates no liability.\n\n   This revision withdraws the liability-allocation language of revision\n   00 and aligns the Merkle commitment with draft-singh-psi-merkle-01.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-16T09:22:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 158648, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-singh-psi-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 4, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-singh-psi-agent/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166453/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170253/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-13T09:22:13Z", "title": "PSI Receipts for AI Agent Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The current generation of AI agent communication protocols enables\r\n   basic tool access and inter-agent messaging, but lacks the\r\n   architectural and protocol foundations required for open,\r\n   interoperable, and resilient Internet-scale deployments.  This\r\n   document presents the AI Agent Interoperable Protocol Framework\r\n   (AIPF), a layered framework that identifies the key building blocks\r\n   and the protocol suite required for interoperable Agent-to-agent/\r\n   Agent-to-tool communication.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-20T12:40:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157417, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zahed-agent-comm-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zahed-agent-comm-framework/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164796/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166442/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-19T12:40:42Z", "title": "AI Agent Interoperable Protocol Framework (AIPF)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   As agents evolve from auxiliary analysis toward autonomous decision-\n   making and task execution, their operation involves multiple dynamic\n   factors, including models, knowledges, metrics, tools, permissions,\n   behaviors, and collaborative relationships.  Traditional management\n   methods are no longer sufficient to ensure their secure and stable\n   operation.  This draft proposes an agent lifecycle management\n   requirement covering registration and publication, deployment and\n   operation, continuous operations, capability evolution, deactivation,\n   and retirement.  It analyzes key management requirements related to\n   assets, capabilities, knowledge, tools, identity and authorization,\n   operational evaluation, auditing, and version evolution and\n   retirement, providing the unified management, continuous governance,\n   and large-scale deployment of agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-20T13:06:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 158686, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sun-nmop-agent-lifecycle-management", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sun-nmop-agent-lifecycle-management/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166493/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-19T13:06:10Z", "title": "The requirements of Agent Lifecycle Management", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a framework for providing AI agents with\n   differentiated network acceleration services in home broadband access\n   environments.\n\n   The framework introduces a Network Acceleration SKILL, provided by\n   the network operator, through which agents subscribe to and request\n   acceleration services directly from the Broadband Remote Access\n   Server (Broadband Remote Access Server).  The Broadband Remote Access\n   Server, augmented with a control plane agent, processes acceleration\n   requests, coordinates authorization with the Authentication,\n   Authorization, and Accounting server, and applies per-agent Quality\n   of Service policies on its data plane.\n\n   A key motivation is to evolve the currently rigid home broadband\n   service model — where plans are fixed on monthly or yearly cycles\n   with static rate limiting — toward a dynamic subscription model that\n   supports on-demand purchase and management of acceleration resources\n   such as time duration or traffic volume.\n\n   A fundamental design constraint is that existing broadband access\n   procedures, including Customer Premises Equipment authentication,\n   IPv6 prefix delegation, and subscriber billing, remain unmodified.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-21T03:04:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 158813, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-li-opsawg-agent-accel-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-li-opsawg-agent-accel-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166557/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-20T03:04:58Z", "title": "A Framework for Agent-Aware Network Acceleration Services in Home Broadband Access", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic systems execute tasks through long-running sequences of model\r\n   inference, planning, delegation, tool use, state updates,\r\n   verification, and recovery.  Conventional metrics, logs, and traces\r\n   can identify a failed request but often cannot determine whether a\r\n   failure is emerging, which actor introduced it, or which earlier\r\n   event was its root cause.  This document specifies an AgentOps\r\n   observability event model and processing requirements for\r\n   interoperable early failure detection and post-failure root-cause\r\n   attribution.  It defines common event, anomaly, assertion, and\r\n   diagnosis records; distinguishes causal origins from propagated\r\n   symptoms; and provides the evidence model required by separate\r\n   benchmarks of detection lead time, responsible-actor attribution, and\r\n   root-cause localization.  The model is transport-neutral and can be\r\n   carried by existing telemetry systems.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-22T11:11:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 159244, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-pei-opsawg-agentops-observability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-pei-opsawg-agentops-observability/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166691/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-21T11:11:26Z", "title": "AgentOps Observability for Failure Detection and Attribution", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   An autonomous software agent commonly acts under authority a human\n   granted at an earlier moment: the human expresses and authorizes an\n   intent at one time, and the agent executes one or more concrete\n   actions at a later time.  The credentials and session state the agent\n   carries at execution time establish that some agent is authorized to\n   act, but they do not establish that this specific action, with these\n   specific parameters, falls within the constraints the human actually\n   signed.  As agent autonomy and action throughput grow, the population\n   of executed actions that no human individually bounded grows with it.\n   This document is a problem statement.  It characterizes the gap\n   between an authorized agent and an authorized action, explains why\n   existing delegation, logging, and payment-mandate mechanisms do not\n   close it, and states the requirements any solution would have to\n   satisfy.  It defines no protocol or mechanism.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-23T12:13:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 159483, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yossif-agent-mandate-problem", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yossif-agent-mandate-problem/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166793/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-22T12:13:37Z", "title": "Problem Statement: Verifiable Human Mandates for Autonomous Agent Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   HTTP applications frequently expose one logical object through\n   several representations or resources.  Ordinary HTTP entity tags\n   identify selected representations; they do not, by themselves,\n   provide a conditional-update mechanism spanning different request\n   targets.\n\n   This document specifies Agentic State Transfer (AST), an HTTP profile\n   for preventing lost updates to shared application state.  AST Core\n   requires a client to mutate the State-Bearing Resource using the\n   strong ETag of its State-Bearing Representation and the standard If-\n   Match field.  AST Semantic uses Semantic-ETag and If-Semantic-Match\n   when a protected mutation targets a different resource or\n   representation in the same concurrency domain.  The profile also\n   defines state discovery, atomic compare-and-commit behavior, conflict\n   handling, deferred processing, caching constraints, and security\n   requirements.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-28T11:33:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 152863, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jurkovikj-httpapi-agentic-state", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jurkovikj-httpapi-agentic-state/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166996/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158048/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163230/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-05T13:29:13Z", "title": "HTTP Profile for Conditional Updates to Shared Resource State (Agentic State Transfer)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the information model for IDL (Intent\n   Description Language), a protocol-independent semantic model for\n   communication among autonomous AI agents and between agents and\n   tools.\n\n   Autonomous agents are fundamentally different.  An agent possesses\n   its own reasoning, may negotiate parameters, may refuse or modify\n   requests, and may initiate interactions on its own behalf.\n   Describing an agent as if it were a deterministic endpoint loses\n   exactly the properties that make it an agent.\n\n   IDL answers: \"what can this autonomous participant contribute to an\n   intent, under what constraints, and through what interaction\n   patterns?\"  IDL is not a transport, session, discovery, or messaging\n   protocol.  It is a semantic model that enables heterogeneous agents\n   to describe identity, capabilities, autonomy boundaries, context\n   requirements, governance interfaces, and intent participation before\n   and during collaboration.\n\n   This document defines the IDL information model and its relationship\n   to agent communication protocols and governance frameworks.  It does\n   not define a normative JSON representation or a serialization-level\n   conformance language.  Protocol-specific mappings, including an AIN/\n   CDL profile, are informative.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-28T23:40:51Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160021, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-feng-agentproto-idl-info-model", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 41, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-feng-agentproto-idl-info-model/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167022/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-27T23:40:51Z", "title": "IDL: A Semantic Information Model for Agent Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) Agent resource\r\n   type defined in draft-wzdk-scim-agent-resource provides a minimal,\r\n   platform-neutral schema for representing AI agent identities.\r\n   Enterprise deployments additionally require governance metadata for\r\n   provisioned agents: a lifecycle state model richer than a boolean, an\r\n   autonomy classification, an operational validity window, and a\r\n   reference to credential discovery information.\r\n\r\n   This document defines an optional extension schema for the SCIM Agent\r\n   resource type carrying this governance metadata.  The lifecycle state\r\n   value set is grounded in the identity information lifecycle of ISO/\r\n   IEC 24760-1, with one agent-specific addition.  Attributes that\r\n   belong to the authorization, credential-management, or real-time\r\n   signaling layers are explicitly out of scope and are enumerated with\r\n   pointers to the appropriate mechanisms.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-28T16:52:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160011, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kushwaha-scim-agent-governance", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kushwaha-scim-agent-governance/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166971/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-27T16:52:42Z", "title": "SCIM Agent Governance Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent evaluations report what happened in a test environment.  They\n   do not, by themselves, establish that a measured candidate satisfies\n   a relying party's policy, remains current, matches the runtime\n   candidate, or is authorized to perform a consequential action.  This\n   document defines a portable Qualification Statement that binds a\n   candidate, complete evaluation campaign, qualification policy,\n   assignment, and status.  It preserves three separate claims:\n   observation, qualification, and authorization.  A relying party can\n   accept a current qualification as evidence at runtime, but MUST make\n   a separate exact-action authorization and admission decision.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-29T07:02:20Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160028, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-schrock-agent-qualification-statements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-schrock-agent-qualification-statements/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167033/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-28T07:02:20Z", "title": "Portable Agent Qualification Statements for Consequential Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a verifier-side acceptance profile for channel-\n   bound Agent identity and authorization.  It addresses context\n   diversion, where cryptographically valid material is accepted for a\n   different service, tenant, actor, task, target, delegation, or\n   authority boundary than the verifier intended.\n\n   A verifier accepts an actor only when a verified authority grant,\n   holder-of-key proof, accepted channel instance, freshness and replay\n   state, any required attestation result, and verifier-local policy all\n   describe the same intended interaction.  Protocol-specific wire\n   formats and deployment choices are left to binding profiles.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T15:02:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157444, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-okutomi-session-bound-agent-identity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 43, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-okutomi-session-bound-agent-identity/", "rev": "07", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171645/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167123/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164804/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164888/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164935/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165126/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165304/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165363/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T15:02:08Z", "title": "A Verifier-Side Acceptance Profile for Channel-Bound Agent Identity and Authorization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents often know the intended outcome of a task without knowing\n   which agent, model, tool, workflow, or service should process it.\n   This document describes an application-layer semantic routing\n   framework that selects one Processing Target from task intent,\n   execution context, target capabilities, constraints, and local\n   policy.  It defines a common operation-level information model and\n   externally observable selection behavior that protocol bindings can\n   preserve.  Semantic matching is implementation-specific.  This\n   document does not define task decomposition, multi-agent\n   orchestration, network-layer routing, target discovery, target\n   invocation, or a new agent communication protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T01:44:07Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161925, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-semantic-routing-ai-agent-communication", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-semantic-routing-ai-agent-communication/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171009/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T01:44:07Z", "title": "Semantic Routing for AI Agent Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a benchmarking methodology for evaluating\n   Artificial Intelligence (AI) agents performing network operations\n   tasks such as configuration, troubleshooting, and optimization.  This\n   document focuses on task-oriented performance metrics, including task\n   completion success, execution efficiency, and robustness across\n   multi-step workflows, proposing benchmarking practices towards agent-\n   based, closed-loop network operation scenarios.\n\n   The proposed methodology aims to provide a reproducible and vendor-\n   independent framework to compare AI agent effectiveness in controlled\n   network environments.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T02:07:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162152, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-contreras-bpm-ai-agent-benchmarking", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-contreras-bpm-ai-agent-benchmarking/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171709/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T11:17:34Z", "title": "Benchmarking Methodology for AI Agents in Network Operations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   An architecture for auditing agent-driven interactions (draft-\n   kuehlewind-audit-architecture) identifies the record types an\n   auditable agent system produces — interaction, action, delegation,\n   and authorization-transition — and the role of an Auditor that\n   determines whether recorded behaviour matched intent and the\n   authorization in force.  That architecture does not specify how an\n   Auditor, or any independent party, deterministically verifies that a\n   set of such records — produced by different parties, in different\n   profiles, and composed into one case — is internally consistent and\n   correctly derives an audit conclusion.\n\n   This document specifies a horizontal conformance and verification\n   methodology for composed agent-accountability records.  It organizes\n   conformance into three tiers: the payload-binding layer, per-record-\n   type (per-slot) conformance, and composition (combination)\n   conformance.  It defines the determinism boundary between what is\n   mechanically derivable from the binding rules and the individual\n   records, and what is irreducibly semantic; a conformance-vector\n   discipline (positive, negative, and must-fail cases, checked in both\n   directions) that any record profile reuses rather than reinvents; and\n   a deterministic method for deriving composition-conformance vectors\n   from binding rules, individual-record vectors, a composition\n   manifest, and a set of cross-record predicates.  The methodology is\n   profile-agnostic: it applies uniformly to any accountability record\n   type and to any declared composition of them, whether those records\n   are joined across parties or across attestation layers.  Its lower\n   two tiers verify a single record, so the method applies where no\n   composition is present and strengthens as records are composed.  It\n   is intended as the composition-verification work item of the auditing\n   architecture — the one its enumerated work items do not yet cover.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-02T06:56:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160104, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-agent-accountability-conformance", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-agent-accountability-conformance/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167183/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-01T06:56:34Z", "title": "Agent Accountability: A Conformance and Verification Method", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous AI agents now operate at production scale across\n   financial, commercial, and infrastructure domains — executing\n   transactions, invoking APIs, and taking consequential actions without\n   direct human oversight at each step.  Existing authorization\n   mechanisms (OAuth 2.0, API keys, ACLs) were designed for human-\n   initiated requests and do not capture the machine-evaluable semantics\n   required for autonomous agent authorization: what the agent is\n   mandated to do, what constraints bound its actions, and for how long\n   the authorization is valid.\n\n   This document specifies the Agent Authorization Envelope (AAE), a\n   structured authorization container for autonomous AI agents.  AAE\n   defines three mandatory blocks — MANDATE, CONSTRAINTS, and VALIDITY —\n   that together constitute a machine-evaluable, cryptographically\n   verifiable authorization assertion.  AAE is designed to be protocol-\n   agnostic, binding to W3C Decentralized Identifiers (DIDs) for agent\n   identity and W3C Verifiable Credentials (VCs) for issuance and\n   signature, and is independent of any specific AI framework, transport\n   protocol, or blockchain.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-10T15:30:19Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156777, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/inf/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kroehl-agentic-trust-aae", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 54, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kroehl-agentic-trust-aae/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/68/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167587/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163393/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168682/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-06T15:30:19Z", "title": "Agent Authorization Envelope (AAE): A Machine-Evaluable Authorization Structure for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document provides a systematic analysis of these emerging agent-\n   based use cases.  It categorizes them into distinct scenarios,\n   details their specific authorization requirements, and performs a\n   comprehensive gap analysis against the existing OAuth 2.0\n   framework[RFC6749] and its common extensions.  The analysis\n   identifies fundamental gaps and requirements, providing a foundation\n   for future work on new extensions within the OAuth Working Group\n   toward creating a more secure and interoperable ecosystem for agent-\n   based systems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T04:07:46Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157750, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-oauth-agent-authz-use-cases", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 51, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-oauth-agent-authz-use-cases/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167638/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168040/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165621/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165700/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170141/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171028/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T04:07:46Z", "title": "Agent Authorization use cases and gap analysis", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous AI agents increasingly act as economic parties: they are\n   hired, they pay, and they make claims about their own past conduct.\n   No deployed standard lets a relying party verify an agent's identity\n   continuity, the integrity of its claimed history, or the intactness\n   of its persisted memory without trusting the agent's operator or\n   platform.\n\n   This document describes the Agent Record architecture: per-agent\n   append-only event logs bound to Ed25519 keys, checkpointed with\n   signed Merkle tree heads following the RFC 6962 construction,\n   countersigned by independent witnesses, and exported as portable,\n   offline-verifiable dossiers.  Memory integrity is anchored by hash\n   commitments recorded in the log, allowing an agent's future sessions,\n   and any third party, to detect tampering with persisted state.  The\n   architecture is deployed in production at a founding registry; this\n   document records its wire formats and security model to invite\n   independent implementation and review, and to align terminology with\n   the SCITT architecture, of which this system is an application-\n   specific instance.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-14T00:36:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160377, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-maintainer-1f916-agent-record", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-maintainer-1f916-agent-record/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167643/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167666/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-13T00:36:00Z", "title": "The Agent Record: Transparent, Witness-Countersigned Event Logs for AI Agent Identity, History, and Memory", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the \"knowledge-linkset\" well-known URI, at\n   which a web origin publishes one link set describing the knowledge\n   artefacts it makes available: graph serializations, a JSON-LD\n   context, an agent-facing text file, a chunk export, a change ledger,\n   and related resources.  Each artefact link may carry a SHA-256\n   digest, expressed with the syntax of HTTP digest fields, so that a\n   client can check that a retrieved artefact is the one the publisher\n   described.  A profile URI identifies the conventions the link set\n   follows.  The mechanism defines no new media type and no new link\n   relation type: a page points at the resource with the existing\n   \"describedby\" relation.  The profile URI is registered in the IANA\n   \"Profile URIs\" registry.  This document requests no IANA action.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-17T14:11:19Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161316, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-besleaga-agentic-knowledge-wellknown", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 34, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-besleaga-agentic-knowledge-wellknown/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170355/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169699/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-14T14:11:19Z", "title": "The 'knowledge-linkset' Well-Known URI for Publishing Knowledge Artefacts", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous AI agents are evolving from interactive assistants into\n   networked software workloads that discover services, invoke tools,\n   delegate authority, transact, communicate with other agents, and act\n   asynchronously on behalf of humans and organizations.  Existing\n   Internet protocols provide strong foundations, but agent autonomy,\n   dynamic delegation, machine-speed execution, long and unpredictable\n   model-processing intervals, and cross-domain interaction create\n   requirements that span multiple protocol families.\n\n   This document describes architectural requirements for supporting AI\n   agents on the Internet across naming and discovery, HTTP,\n   authentication, authorization and delegation, TLS and workload\n   identity, transport and connection continuity, asynchronous\n   messaging, capability and intent-based resolution, payments,\n   provenance, auditability, revocation, security, and privacy.  It\n   favors profiling and extending existing Internet protocols over\n   defining a monolithic new agent protocol, and identifies the need for\n   IETF-wide architectural coordination.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-02T00:01:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160407, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-daniel-ai-agent-internet-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 28, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-daniel-ai-agent-internet-architecture/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168246/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168258/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167708/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168259/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-29T00:01:14Z", "title": "Architectural Requirements for Supporting AI Agents on the Internet", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent-tool protocols such as the Model Context Protocol (MCP) enable\n   AI applications to discover and invoke external tools, resources, and\n   prompts through a standardized JSON-RPC interface.  As deployments\n   scale, intermediaries (proxies, gateways, sidecars) are inserted\n   between clients and servers to provide transport adaptation,\n   capability aggregation, security enforcement, and operational\n   governance.\n\n   No specification currently defines the behavioral requirements for\n   such intermediaries.  This document establishes a taxonomy of proxy\n   modes, a layered architecture for pluggable proxy functionality, and\n   normative requirements for each mode.  It is designed to be protocol-\n   agnostic in its architecture while referencing MCP as the primary\n   instantiation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-15T04:03:52Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160413, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-agent-proxy-modes", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 39, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-agent-proxy-modes/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167719/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-14T04:03:52Z", "title": "Proxy Modes for Agent-Tool Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document profiles the IETF SCITT (Supply Chain Integrity,\n   Transparency, and Trust) architecture for AI-agent action receipts:\n   tamper-evident, signed, offline-verifiable records of what an\n   autonomous agent was recorded as doing at the governed boundary,\n   under which recorded principal class, with what recorded verdict, and\n   -- where the issuer records one -- under which policy identity.  Each\n   receipt is a signed record over a canonical JSON payload, hash-\n   chained so that each record commits to its predecessor, and presented\n   either bare -- the payload with its own native signature -- or\n   enveloped in a COSE_Sign1.  This revision specifies how such a\n   receipt is carried as a SCITT Signed Statement, with the protected\n   claims a Transparency Service requires, so that a receipt can be\n   registered.  Registration obtains a Transparency Service's signed\n   proof that the statement was registered in its log -- a property a\n   self-signed chain cannot provide alone.  It does not, by itself, give\n   an offline holder non-equivocation: that requires consistency proofs\n   and monitoring of the log, which this profile does not specify.  The\n   profile makes a deliberately narrow, checkable claim: this is an\n   issuer-authenticated, signature-verifiable, tamper-evident record of\n   the action, the recorded principal class, the recorded verdict, and\n   any policy identity the receipt carries.  It explicitly does not\n   claim that the agent was correct, safe, or wise, that the recorded\n   inputs were true or complete, that a named approver authorized this\n   exact action before it ran, that a downstream controller succeeded,\n   or that any physical effect occurred.  This revision separates those\n   last three as distinct claims with independent failure behaviour,\n   states the boundary of a shared action digest, and keeps a\n   deterministic offline policy-replay capability out of scope.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-16T01:13:57Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157451, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-noa-scitt-ai-agent-receipt", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 97, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-noa-scitt-ai-agent-receipt/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167759/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164836/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-15T01:13:57Z", "title": "A SCITT Profile for AI-Agent Action Receipts", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document serves as formal administrative notification that the\n   individual Internet-Draft draft-hawkins-scitt-attested-agent-payment\n   has been discontinued and will not be progressed as an individual\n   submission.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-28T11:21:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160233, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hawkins-scitt-attested-agent-payment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 2, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hawkins-scitt-attested-agent-payment/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167380/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168175/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167760/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-27T11:21:30Z", "title": "Notice of Discontinuation: Attested Agent Payment", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes a DID-based framework for service discovery,\n   authentication, and authorization of MCP (Model Context Protocol)\n   Agents, based on the W3C Decentralized Identifier (DID) standard.\n   The framework uses the did:web and did:key methods to provide\n   verifiable, decentralized identifiers for MCP Clients and Servers.\n   It defines DID method selection, DID Document extensions, service\n   discovery mechanisms (including URL derivation, DNS-based discovery,\n   and directory-based capability queries), and a challenge-response\n   mutual authentication protocol.  The framework also describes\n   coexistence with OAuth 2.0 and enables trust establishment, dynamic\n   capability-based service discovery, and fine-grained authorization\n   with portable identities.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-16T15:37:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160612, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-xu-mcp-agent-did-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-xu-mcp-agent-did-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167768/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-15T15:37:30Z", "title": "DID-Based Service Discovery, Authentication, and Authorization Framework for MCP Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the FAF Agent Format (.fafa): a declarative,\n   YAML-based format for an agent's identity, the capabilities it\n   exposes, and the endpoints through which it is reached.  A .fafa\n   document describes an agent; it never instructs one.\n\n   .fafa (application/vnd.fafa+yaml, IANA-registered June 2026 in the\n   vendor tree) is the agent member of the FAF family, alongside .faf\n   (project context) and .fafm (agent memory).  It functions as a\n   portable passport that answers four questions: who the agent is, what\n   it may do, where it is reached, and what it must never do.  Protocol-\n   native cards (for example A2A Agent Cards and MCP Server Cards)\n   remain useful wire formats; repository instruction files such as\n   AGENTS.md remain the ops briefing; .fafa complements them as a house-\n   neutral source of truth that can be projected into those formats; it\n   does not replace them.\n\n   This document documents the existing IANA vendor-tree registration.\n   No standards-tree registration is requested.  A companion white\n   paper, \"Why Agents Need a Passport,\" provides the production\n   rationale and lifecycle framing.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-17T17:03:40Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157556, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wolfe-faf-agent", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wolfe-faf-agent/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167785/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165146/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-16T17:03:40Z", "title": "FAFA: A Declarative Agent Capability Format", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a format for action receipts: compact,\n   individually signed JSON records that state that a specific AI agent\n   attempted a specific action at a specific time, under a specific\n   policy decision, and what the outcome was.  Receipts are linked into\n   an append-only hash chain so that deletion, insertion, reordering, or\n   modification of any previously recorded receipt is detectable by a\n   verifier that holds only the records and the signer's public key.\n\n   The format is deliberately small and self-contained.  Verification\n   requires no network access, no service operated by the producer of\n   the receipts, and no state beyond the records themselves and a trust\n   anchor obtained out of band.  This document specifies the record\n   fields, the canonical byte sequence that is signed, the chain linkage\n   rule, the verification procedure, and test vectors.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-17T18:15:41Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160620, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sahu-agent-action-receipts", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sahu-agent-action-receipts/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167787/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-16T18:15:41Z", "title": "Signed, Hash-Chained Action Receipts for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This design note proposes an overlay for Human and Agent\n   participation in existing Task and Action protocols.  It separates\n   the responsible Participant from the authenticated Actor, records\n   Human interactions, excludes Humans from Agent discovery, and binds\n   each change to its authorized request.  It specifies no wire protocol\n   and does not treat Humans as Agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T15:03:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160651, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-okutomi-agent-human-interaction", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-okutomi-agent-human-interaction/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171646/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167857/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T15:03:44Z", "title": "An Agent-Human Interaction Overlay for Task Protocols", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a standard logging format for autonomous\n   AI agent systems.  The Agent Audit Trail (AAT) defines a\n   JSON-based record structure with mandatory fields for agent\n   identity, the authorizing principal, action classification,\n   outcome tracking, and trust level reporting.  Records are\n   linked via tamper-evident hash chaining using SHA-256 per\n   RFC 8785, with optional ECDSA signatures for non-repudiation.\n\n   The format addresses requirements from the EU AI Act\n   (Regulation 2024/1689), which mandates automatic recording of\n   events for high-risk AI systems, whose application dates were\n   staged into 2027-2028 by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744.  It also\n   maps informatively to SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria,\n   ISO/IEC 42001, the draft ISO/IEC 24970 and prEN 18229-1 logging\n   standards, and PCI DSS v4.0.1 logging requirements.\n\n   The design is transport-agnostic and supports export to JSONL,\n   Syslog (RFC 5424), and CSV while preserving chain integrity.\n   Privacy is addressed through input/output hashing, content\n   fingerprinting, and tombstone-based deletion compatible with\n   GDPR Article 17.\n\n   The -01 revision added pre-execution recording requirements,\n   recording independence, deny reason codes, replay protection,\n   external timestamp anchoring, and content fingerprinting based\n   on feedback from independent implementers.\n\n   The -02 revision added a Decision Reproducibility section\n   (Section 13) that distinguishes record reproducibility,\n   available for any model, from decision reproducibility,\n   available only for open-weight models executed at temperature\n   zero in an attested environment, and defines the associated\n   record fields.\n\n   The -03 revision added the Attestation Closure requirement\n   (Section 13.6): the digests recorded for decision\n   reproducibility MUST cover the complete computational closure\n   of the inference function -- model weights, tokenizer, chat\n   template, inference engine build, decoding configuration, and\n   numeric environment -- together with new record fields\n   (tokenizer_digest, chat_template_digest, engine_build_digest)\n   and a minimal-change threat analysis (Section 13.7) showing\n   that any component left outside the attested set is a forgery\n   channel.\n\n   The -04 revision added algorithm agility for post-quantum\n   signatures (ML-DSA-65, FIPS 204) alongside ECDSA P-256; a key\n   identifier (signer_kid, RFC 7638) that resolves which principal\n   signed an independently recorded record; a trust-level assignment\n   integrity requirement (Section 5.3) so that downgrading a\n   consequential action is an attributable event rather than silent\n   suppression; and OPTIONAL Merkle batch anchoring (Section 6.4)\n   using the RFC 6962 construction for compact inclusion proofs at\n   high throughput.  All -04 additions are OPTIONAL to produce, and\n   -04 verifiers stay backward compatible with -03: a -04 verifier\n   accepts -03 records, and the new signing metadata is required\n   only in records that carry a signature.\n\n   This revision (-07) adds a mandatory \"principal\" field\n   (Section 3.1) that binds each record to the party on whose\n   authority the agent acted; a Relationship to Other Work\n   section (Section 1.3) relating AAT to the WIMSE agent audit\n   record and authorization-evidence drafts and to SCITT\n   (RFC 9943); and corrections to the EU AI Act Article 12 and\n   Article 19 citations in the regulatory mapping (Section 11.1\n   and Appendix B).  Records produced under -06 and earlier\n   predate the \"principal\" field; verifiers processing\n   mixed-revision archives SHOULD treat its absence as \"principal\n   not recorded\" rather than a validation failure.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T17:46:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155757, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-agent-audit-trail", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 65, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-agent-audit-trail/", "rev": "09", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169473/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169652/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170134/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161719/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167875/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169090/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170274/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168530/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168657/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170991/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-26T17:46:36Z", "title": "Agent Audit Trail: A Standard Logging Format for Autonomous AI Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent service discovery nodes may need to process two classes of\n   workloads concurrently: Agent registration and dynamic state updates,\n   and discovery queries issued by other Agents.  These workloads\n   compete for shared processing resources but have different\n   performance objectives.  Delayed state updates can cause a service\n   discovery node to rely on stale workload, availability, or QoS\n   information, while delayed queries can increase Agent-selection\n   latency and the completion time of multi-Agent tasks.\n\n   This document describes a scheduling framework for coordinating\n   update and query processing in a multi-Worker Agent service discovery\n   node.  To estimate the demand of each workload, the framework\n   considers total queued work, waiting time, deadline pressure, recent\n   load, state freshness, and, for updates, the expected freshness gain\n   from processing a pending update.  These estimates determine how\n   Worker capacity is divided between the two queues, which remain\n   active in parallel.\n\n   The framework also incorporates update merging and deduplication,\n   freshness-aware dependencies between state updates and discovery\n   queries, hysteresis-based resource reallocation, minimum resource\n   holding time, and intra-queue task prioritization.  Different\n   deployment conditions can be accommodated by adjusting the\n   corresponding weights and thresholds without changing the scheduling\n   structure itself.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-21T06:33:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160668, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wen-agent-workload-scheduling", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wen-agent-workload-scheduling/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167898/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-20T06:33:13Z", "title": "Dynamic Scheduling of Update and Query Workloads in Agent Service Discovery Nodes", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines requirements for session-based and sessionless\n   interactions between entities.  For session-based interactions, it\n   covers transport-independent interaction binding, endpoint\n   authentication, capability negotiation, session establishment,\n   authorization, and lifecycle management.  It also defines security\n   and state requirements for interactions, such as notifications,\n   probes, and atomic requests, that do not establish a session.  It is\n   assumed that the entities involved already know of each other; how\n   they came to know each other is outside the scope of this document.\n   At least one party to an interaction is an agent as defined in\n   Section 3.  This document is intended as a contribution to the\n   agentproto working group's use cases, gap analysis, and requirements\n   deliverable.\n\n   A session is a bilateral association.  Protocols and application\n   semantics for coordinating delegation or handoff of work to an entity\n   that is not a peer, and management functions such as cross-entity\n   accountability and audit, are outside the scope of these base session\n   requirements.  This document specifies only that such coordination\n   does not, by itself, change the peers or state of an existing\n   session.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-21T13:58:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160611, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-feng-agentproto-session-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-feng-agentproto-session-requirements/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167761/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167845/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167909/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-20T13:58:55Z", "title": "Requirements for Agent Session Establishment, Capability Negotiation, and Sessionless Interaction", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes use cases for agentic AI communication\n   systems and derives protocol requirements from those use cases.  The\n   requirements are intended to guide IETF standardization work on\n   protocols in the context of agent-to-agent communication, agent-to-\n   tool communication, with focus on dialog management, transport,\n   multimodal communication, communication security, agent identity and\n   authentication.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T14:30:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2645/", "id": 156787, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-agentic-ai-usecases-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-agentic-ai-usecases-requirements/", "rev": "07", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/134/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170407/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167453/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170072/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163420/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168075/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171052/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171918/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171894/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-07T14:57:19Z", "title": "Agentic AI Use Cases and Requirements", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Frontier runtimes already standardized the dangerous moment.  A model\n   emits a tool_use block, a tool_calls array, or an MCP tools/call\n   payload.  The host then invokes whatever name and arguments the model\n   printed.  Alignment, allowlists, and OAuth sit around that moment.\n   They do not sit on it.\n\n   The consequence of this gap is no longer confined to email or payment\n   demos.  In defense, energy, grid control, industrial process control,\n   and other critical-infrastructure deployments, the same tool_use\n   block already reaches actuation-class systems -- logistics and\n   targeting-adjacent decision support, SCADA and PLC interfaces,\n   medical devices, autonomous platforms.  In these environments,\n   detection after the fact is not mitigation; it is an incident report\n   written after the effect has already occurred.  An agent that can act\n   at machine speed but cannot be halted at machine speed is a system\n   running without brakes: the first uncontrolled invocation is not a\n   warning sign, it is the accident.  Command authority, human\n   oversight, and legal review all operate on human time.  An unbound\n   tool_use block operates on machine time.  When those two clocks\n   diverge, the gap belongs to whichever side reaches the effect first\n   -- and today, nothing structurally guarantees that side is\n   authorization.\n\n   This document does not invent another assistant API.  It binds the\n   Agent Candidate Act profile [I-D.das-agentic] onto the three\n   interface families those runtimes and their customers already ship:\n   tool_use / computer_use style interfaces, function-calling and\n   structured tool-response interfaces, and Model Context Protocol\n   tools/call.  The model may emit the block.  The block remains non-\n   effective.  A local enforcer builds the act, binds the argument\n   digest, and refuses invoke() until scoped authority is verified and\n   consumed at the dispatch sink.\n\n   For consequence classes above a defined threshold -- FINANCIAL,\n   PHYSICAL, NETWORK_CONTROL, and any act reaching defense or critical-\n   infrastructure actuation -- this binding treats fail-closed as the\n   only conforming behavior: absent successfully verified, current, act-\n   bound authority, the candidate act stays non-effective regardless of\n   model confidence, prior session trust, or upstream alignment signal.\n   Each enforcement decision, allow or deny, commits a Ledger-Anchored\n   Validation Receipt (LAVR) -- a signed, hash-chained enforcement\n   artifact bound to the specific candidate act and its argument digest\n   at the moment of decision.  An LAVR is not a log entry assembled\n   afterward for audit; it is the proof that the finality boundary\n   actually gated this act before any effect could occur, and its\n   absence is itself a fail-closed condition.\n\n   The implementation target is a middleware function that a host loop\n   can call without changing the model vendor. tool_use is not invoke().\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-09T14:29:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160755, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-agentic-tool-binding", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 51, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-agentic-tool-binding/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168240/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168155/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168355/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168647/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-29T14:30:39Z", "title": "tool_use Is Not invoke(): Binding Execution-Finality to Agentic Tool-Call Interfaces and MCP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a protocol for trust scoring, identity\n   verification, and spend limit enforcement for autonomous AI agents\n   that initiate financial transactions.  As AI agents gain the\n   capability to make payments via protocols such as the Machine\n   Payments Protocol (MPP), a standardised mechanism is needed to\n   verify agent identity, assess trustworthiness, and enforce\n   financial limits based on behavioural history.\n\n   The protocol defines a five-dimension trust scoring model, per-\n   agent cryptographic identity using ECDSA P-256 key pairs,\n   challenge-response identity verification, spend limit tiers\n   derived from trust scores, anomaly detection for financial\n   behaviour, and a public trust query API for third-party platforms.\n\n   This specification complements draft-sharif-mcps-secure-mcp, which\n   provides message-level cryptographic security for the Model Context\n   Protocol (MCP).  Together, the two specifications address protocol\n   security (MCPS) and financial trust (this document) for the AI\n   agent economy.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-28T12:07:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155682, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-agent-payment-trust", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-agent-payment-trust/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161602/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168178/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-27T12:07:44Z", "title": "Trust Scoring and Identity Verification for Autonomous AI Agent Payment Transactions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent tasks are delegated across organizational boundaries.  Existing\n   work specifies how agents are identified, discovered, and described,\n   states requirements for cross-domain isolation and authorization, and\n   identifies the absence of a mechanism for expressing capability\n   policy as a gap.  This document defines four policy attributes for\n   inter-domain agent delegation, the declarations each attribute\n   carries, and a validity condition on delegation chains that no party\n   establishes by observing the whole chain.  Whether independently\n   chosen policies converge is analysed in separate work.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-24T00:06:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161111, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ahuja-agent-routing-policy", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ahuja-agent-routing-policy/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169492/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169211/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169839/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170652/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-21T00:06:44Z", "title": "A Policy Grammar for Inter-Domain Agent Routing", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a trust enforcement architecture for\n   autonomous AI agents operating within container orchestration\n   environments such as Kubernetes.  It defines a sidecar injection\n   pattern using mutating admission webhooks, graduated trust\n   enforcement (L0-L4) on every outbound call from an agent\n   workload, credential isolation via secret management systems,\n   bilateral revocation propagation across clusters, and tamper-\n   evident evidence generation.\n\n   The architecture operates alongside existing workload identity\n   frameworks including SPIFFE/SPIRE without replacement, extends\n   X.509v3 certificates with agent-specific extensions under a\n   registered IANA Private Enterprise Number (PEN 66339), and\n   provides compliance evidence for EU AI Act Article 12,\n   FDA 21 CFR Part 11, IEC 62443, and NERC CIP.\n\n   Three enforcement gates -- LLM gate, Database gate, and API\n   gate -- intercept every outbound call from an agent container.\n   Each gate classifies the call against the agent's trust level,\n   records the decision in a hash-chained evidence ledger with\n   ECDSA P-256 signatures, and either permits or refuses the call.\n   The architecture defaults to deny: if no policy matches, the\n   call is refused.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-28T17:38:33Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160761, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-agent-trust-enforcement", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 59, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-agent-trust-enforcement/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168195/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-27T17:38:33Z", "title": "Agent Trust Enforcement for Autonomous AI Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic AI systems now call tools, write memory, move money, change\n   infrastructure, and trigger physical actions.  Most safety layers\n   still decide permission upstream and then trust the downstream path.\n   Once that path is compromised, or once the approved request is\n   widened, replayed, or substituted, the act becomes real before any\n   audit can stop it.\n\n   This document specifies a protected execution-finality architecture\n   of the Decoupled Authorisation System (DAS).  It is built on four\n   mechanisms: (1) two-instance binding that separates collection-time\n   evidence from execution-time validation, (2) mutually load-bearing,\n   cross-committed protected evidence so that no single artifact\n   authorizes effectuation, (3) scoped non-bearer finality authority\n   whose possession alone is never enough, and (4) independent Finality\n   Sink reconstruction that re-derives the actual pending operation at\n   the effectuation boundary and permits the act only when every\n   required condition still matches.\n\n   A Candidate Act remains in a Non-Effective State until the Finality\n   Sink has reconstructed the operation, verified the protected evidence\n   against sink-local monotonic state, and advanced that state.  Failure\n   at any step produces fail-closed denial before effectuation rather\n   than post-event remediation.  The architecture is applicable to\n   agentic tool use, MCP and connector frameworks, RAG and vector-memory\n   systems, cloud control planes, financial settlement, telecom routing,\n   and cyber-physical control.\n\n   The document elaborates the problem space, compares the approach with\n   representative existing techniques, presents the detailed solution\n   and its advantages, supplies JSON Schema definitions for core\n   protected objects, and includes an industry-relevance section.\n   Related Indian provisional applications and PCT filings appear in the\n   final appendix.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-01T03:39:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160788, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-agentic-ai-tool-execution-finality", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 30, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-agentic-ai-tool-execution-finality/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168216/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-09T05:18:37Z", "title": "Execution Finality for Agentic AI: Stopping Unauthorized Tool Calls, Memory Writes, and Real-World Consequences Before They Happen (DAS -- Decoupled Authorisation System)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent-initiated payments now execute across several settlement rails\n   with materially different finality semantics, authorization\n   primitives and failure modes.  Published comparisons of these rails\n   are commonly self-asserted, and commonly do not state a procedure\n   another party could reproduce.  This document specifies a measurement\n   methodology for such rails.  It defines finality per rail at its\n   ecosystem-canonical reliance level rather than imposing a single\n   definition, separates payment-validation latency from challenge-\n   issuance latency as distinct and non-comparable quantities, and\n   specifies an out-of-path observation posture in which the measuring\n   party never holds funds, keys or signing authority.  It states\n   reporting requirements, including mandatory disclosure of limitations\n   and a prohibition on merging observer-clock and payer-clock\n   measurements.  It defines no payment protocol and recommends no rail.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-01T14:18:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160794, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-blake-bmwg-agent-payment-measurement", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-blake-bmwg-agent-payment-measurement/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168233/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-28T14:18:44Z", "title": "Out-of-Path Measurement Methodology for Agent-Initiated Payment Rails", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   A Network Digital Twin (NDT) provides a network emulation tool usable\n   for different purposes such as scenario planning, impact analysis,\n   and change management.  Agentic AI enables dynamic goal-driven\n   execution and adaptive behavior and closed-loop autonomy.  By\n   integrating a NDT into network management together with the Agentic\n   AI, it allows the network management activities to take user intent\n   or service requirements as input, automatically assess, model, and\n   refine optimization strategies under realistic conditions but in a\n   risk-free environment.  Such environment that operates to meet these\n   types of requirements is said to have AI-driven network operations.\n\n   AI-driven network operations brings together existing technologies\n   such as Agentic AI and NDT which may be seen as the use of a toolbox\n   of existing components enhanced with a few new elements.\n\n   This document describes an architecture for AI-driven network\n   operations and shows how these components work together with NDT and\n   Agentic AI capabilities.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-01T13:58:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146751, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wmz-nmrg-agent-ndt-arch", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 49, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wmz-nmrg-agent-ndt-arch/", "rev": "06", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153192/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156430/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156708/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159994/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168231/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163376/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167874/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-28T13:58:05Z", "title": "Network Digital Twin and Agentic AI based Architecture for AI-driven Network Operations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the 'agent' Uniform Resource Identifier (URI)\r\n   scheme and its associated cryptographic attestation protocol.  The\r\n   'agent' scheme defines a transport-agnostic, cryptographically\r\n   verifiable addressing layer for identifying autonomous software\r\n   agents, binding domain ownership via DNS TXT records, enforcing\r\n   instruction integrity, and validating attenuated capability\r\n   delegation tokens.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-06T00:22:07Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160863, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kanojia-creduent-agent-uri", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kanojia-creduent-agent-uri/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168448/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-02T00:22:07Z", "title": "The 'agent' Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) Scheme and Cryptographic Attestation Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a profile for delegating authorization to\n   automated agents across administrative domains.  It defines an HTTP\n   header field, Agent-Delegation, that carries a chain of attenuated\n   delegation links.  Each link narrows the scope, tightens or holds a\n   set of floor conditions, and shortens or holds the expiry of its\n   parent.  A verifier checks every link in the chain, not only the\n   last, and rejects the chain if any link violates attenuation.  The\n   profile is deliberately agnostic to the credential format and to the\n   nature of the entity that issues floor attestations; it specifies\n   required properties, not a specific encoding or a specific kind of\n   issuer.  It composes with, and does not replace, existing work on\n   agent credential provisioning and posture.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-04T13:44:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160832, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hassan-oauth-agent-delegation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 28, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hassan-oauth-agent-delegation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168366/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-31T13:44:42Z", "title": "An Attenuated Delegation Profile for Automated Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document builds on HTTP Message Signatures (RFC 9421) for\n   requests that automated agents send on behalf of people.  RFC 9421\n   shows who signed a request.  It does not show who the agent acts for,\n   or what the agent may do.  This document defines an HTTP header\n   field, Agent-Delegation, that carries a chain of delegation links.\n   Each link can only narrow the scope, floors, and expiry of the link\n   before it, and a verifier checks every link.  Floors cover two\n   things.  The first is the person or account behind the agent: an\n   Attestation Issuer, such as a mobile network operator or an identity-\n   document check, answers with a signed yes or no, bound to the\n   verifier's nonce and to an expiry, and never returns the value behind\n   the answer.  The second is what the agent may do: each call has a\n   class, r, w, or x, read as in chmod, with an optional count per\n   class, and a signed menu gives the class of each call.  A deployment\n   can set this scope for each job when the job starts.  This document\n   does not define a credential format, an identity system, or\n   revocation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-25T21:56:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160833, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hamr-oauth-agent-delegation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hamr-oauth-agent-delegation/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169290/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168367/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168474/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170776/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-22T21:56:56Z", "title": "An Attenuated Delegation Profile for Automated Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AgentEnvelope defines a deterministic derived-authority model for\n   autonomous and action-performing systems.  Instead of issuing bearer\n   credentials from a central authority, AgentEnvelope derives scoped\n   action capabilities from customer-held custody material and canonical\n   action envelopes.  A verifier can check an action signature against a\n   public action record without receiving roots, seeds, private keys,\n   mint material, or hosted service access.\n\n   This revision extends the model with legitimacy: a governance state\n   that records whether a cryptographically valid authority remains\n   admissible under current policy, evidence, time, and operating\n   context.  Legitimacy separates provenance from present-tense\n   authorization.  A command can remain signed and verifiable while\n   becoming illegitimate because operating facts, policy, or evidence\n   changed.\n\n   For autonomous-system deployments, derived authority and legitimacy\n   support an IAM model concerned with authority, admissibility,\n   accountability, and audit for actors that perform actions, including\n   AI agents, workflows, bots, microservices, devices, robots,\n   serverless workers, and multi-agent systems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-04T15:26:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160641, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mcphillips-agentenvelope-derived-authority", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mcphillips-agentenvelope-derived-authority/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167828/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168372/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-31T15:26:03Z", "title": "AgentEnvelope: Derived Authority and Legitimacy for Autonomous Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent systems can issue stop, suspend, revoke, constrain, cancel, or\n   override instructions across system and administrative boundaries.  A\n   record that such a control was decided or dispatched does not\n   establish that every intended enforcement point received or applied\n   it.  Conversely, the absence of an acknowledgement does not, by\n   itself, establish non-delivery.\n\n   This document defines format-independent evidence requirements for\n   preserving those distinctions.  It separates issuer-side emission,\n   required-target resolution, receiver-side observation, enforcement\n   outcome, and observation of the resulting control effect.  For a\n   control that must reach more than one enforcement target, the unit of\n   delivery reconciliation is an instruction-target obligation rather\n   than the parent instruction alone.  The document also defines bounded\n   negative observations, total reconciliation, population conservation,\n   semantic-preservation requirements for intermediary paths, and a\n   separate qualification for the evidentiary strength of aggregate\n   claims.\n\n   This document does not define a receipt format, wire protocol,\n   authorization system, policy language, transparency service, or audit\n   regime.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-14T20:19:40Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160834, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-abak-agent-control-delivery-evidence", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 51, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-abak-agent-control-delivery-evidence/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168295/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168584/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170130/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-11T20:19:40Z", "title": "Evidence Requirements for Agent Control Delivery and Outcome Reconciliation", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   A payment between two agents is observed by two systems: the payer's\n   wallet or bank and the payee's.  Existing payment protocols record\n   one of those observations, signed by one party, and most record\n   nothing about what was delivered in exchange.  This document defines\n   two-party settlement records, carried as Agent Action Capsules, in\n   which payer and payee each seal, under their own key, only what their\n   own system observed.  A settlement is a set of up to four leg records\n   (terms, payer-observed, payee-observed, and delivered) that cite each\n   other by digest and join on a typed payment reference.  The state of\n   a settlement (one-sided, agreed, or mismatched) is derived by the\n   verifier from which legs are present and whether they agree; no\n   record asserts it.  Signed objects from existing payment protocols\n   are carried by digest and never re-signed.  Amounts are exact\n   integers with a decimal scale.  The document defines a registry of\n   payment reference types covering x402, Lightning, AP2, ACP, UCP, the\n   Payment HTTP authentication scheme, ISO 20022, and Open Payments, and\n   maps its states to ISO 20022 status codes.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-05T03:34:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161357, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-agent-settlement-records", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 41, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-agent-settlement-records/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169783/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-02T03:34:25Z", "title": "Two-Party Settlement Records for Agent Payments", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Simple Agent Management Protocol (SAMP) defines a lightweight\n   management-plane protocol for heterogeneous AI agents.  SAMP allows a\n   management system to discover agents, query their state, receive\n   events, subscribe to event streams, and optionally configure or\n   execute explicitly exposed operations under policy control.\n\n   SAMP is inspired by operational management protocols such as SNMP,\n   but it is designed for AI-agent-specific concepts such as dynamic\n   profiles, autonomy classes, enrollment, trust states, and policy-\n   gated execution.  It is not an agent-to-agent communication protocol,\n   an agent tool-use protocol, or an agent framework specification.\n\n   This document defines SAMP version 0.2 as an Experimental protocol\n   suitable for controlled environments and independent interoperability\n   testing.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T16:43:52Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160637, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-efstathiou-samp-agent-management", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 125, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-efstathiou-samp-agent-management/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168414/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169524/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167821/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168992/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171034/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T16:43:52Z", "title": "Simple Agent Management Protocol (SAMP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Prove-Transform-Verify (PTV) protocol for\n   hardware-anchored, zero-knowledge attested agent identity in\n   federated environments.  PTV addresses the data gravity problem in\n   centralized security models by enabling cross-domain agent\n   authorization without raw data exposure.\n\n   The specification includes: TPM 2.0/secure enclave roots of trust;\n   Groth16 zero-knowledge proofs (<200ms generation on commodity edge\n   hardware); HotStuff Byzantine Fault Tolerant consensus for immutable\n   audit trails; and Sovereign Bound metadata for jurisdiction-aware\n   attestation chains.\n\n   Use cases include healthcare clinical decision support systems\n   (CDSS), critical infrastructure industrial control systems (ICS/OT),\n   and cross-border regulatory compliance scenarios under GDPR/HIPAA\n   frameworks.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-03T06:01:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155808, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-anandakrishnan-ptv-attested-agent-identity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-anandakrishnan-ptv-attested-agent-identity/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161803/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-02T07:09:06Z", "title": "The Prove-Transform-Verify (PTV) Protocol for Attested Agent Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document presents a server-side model for paid, human-sourced\n   asynchronous HTTP tasks initiated by software agents acting on behalf\n   of a principal.  Clients may retry after uncertain outcomes and act\n   under authority delegated in advance, while fulfilment incurs cost\n   and may not be freely reversible.\n\n   The model places a payment or entitlement gate before fulfilment,\n   maps that gate to adjacent protocols, and defines a task state\n   machine.  Under stated well-formedness constraints and operating\n   assumptions, it yields gate-before-fulfilment safety, at most one\n   task per retained idempotency key, at most one gate acceptance per\n   payment requirement, and an operator-checkable record linking a\n   delivered artefact to operator-controlled records.  It defines no\n   protocol, wire format, or conformance requirements.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-11T09:51:11Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 159856, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-king-yew-choo-agentic-payments", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 58, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-king-yew-choo-agentic-payments/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166898/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169974/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-08T09:51:11Z", "title": "A Server-Side Model for Gating Agent-Initiated Human-Sourced Asynchronous HTTP Tasks on Payment or Entitlement", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Automated agents can exercise OAuth-derived authority through\n   delegated credentials, child agents, asynchronous work, and\n   operations spanning multiple administrative domains.  Invalidating an\n   individual token does not necessarily establish that the authority\n   represented by that token can no longer cause a new consequential\n   operation.\n\n   This document defines authority epochs, delegation-lineage\n   requirements, admission fencing, and revocation-closure states for\n   OAuth-based agent delegation.  It distinguishes token invalidation\n   from authority closure and specifies that unresolved in-scope state\n   produces UNKNOWN or PARTIAL rather than CLOSED.  The profile composes\n   with OAuth Token Revocation, Token Exchange, Token Introspection,\n   proof-of-possession mechanisms, Transaction Tokens, and workload-\n   identity systems such as WIMSE.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:22:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161001, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-oauth-agent-revocation-closure", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-oauth-agent-revocation-closure/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168987/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170164/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171265/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171496/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:22:29Z", "title": "Revocation Closure and Authority Epochs for Agent Delegation", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Agentic Hypercall Protocol (AHP), a\n   minimal convention for automated software agents to discover, invoke,\n   pay for, and rate tools over plain HTTP.  It replaces draft-campbell-\n   agentic-http-00 and extends it in three directions.  First, it\n   formalizes the HTTP 402 (Payment Required) status code as a native\n   economic layer with pluggable payment rails (Lightning L402, Cashu\n   ecash, and prepaid balances).  Second, it specifies a blind relay --\n   the Gateway -- through which a consumer and a provider can transact\n   end-to-end encrypted: the relay verifies identity, settles payment,\n   and forwards sealed payloads it cannot read.  Third, it specifies\n   signed receipts, settlements, and rating attestations that let\n   reputation be weighted by money actually settled rather than by\n   tokens or votes, and that ride with a provider's key rather than with\n   any single relay.  The result is an open market in which agents can\n   buy compute, information, and services from one another without an\n   SDK, a walled garden, or a native token.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-10T20:10:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160920, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-campbell-agentic-market", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-campbell-agentic-market/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168696/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-06T20:10:10Z", "title": "Agentic Hypercall Protocol (AHP): Tool Invocation, Blind Settlement, and Portable Reputation over HTTP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous software agents driven by large language models execute\n   multi-step runs in which the same conversational context is\n   retransmitted to a model on every step.  The resulting resource\n   consumption is dominated by repeated input rather than by generated\n   output, and it is reported today in mutually incompatible, vendor-\n   specific shapes.  This document defines Agent Run Metrics, a JSON\n   interchange format that describes the resource consumption of a\n   single agent run and of its constituent steps, together with a small\n   set of derived quantities whose computation is specified exactly.  It\n   states normatively that one reported step corresponds to one\n   completed model invocation, so that the several records a runtime may\n   write while a single response is produced are not mistaken for\n   several invocations, and it defines how the consumption of delegated\n   sub-runs is attributed without being counted twice.  The format\n   carries counters, timing and cost attribution only; it deliberately\n   excludes prompt and completion content.  This document defines the\n   initial values of two extensible enumerations and the rule by which\n   they are extended; it requests no IANA action.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T10:48:41Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160964, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-arsentev-agent-run-metrics", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 45, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-arsentev-agent-run-metrics/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168854/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169860/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171611/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T10:48:41Z", "title": "Agent Run Metrics: A JSON Interchange Format for Resource Accounting of Language-Model Agent Runs", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Delegated software requesters increasingly make HTTP requests that\n   spend, consume, disclose, mutate, invoke, or actuate on behalf of\n   human or organizational principals.  Existing HTTP authentication\n   mechanisms indicate whether a requester holds a credential.\n   RateLimit fields communicate server-advertised quota and current\n   service-limit information.  HTTP Message Signatures can protect\n   selected components of an HTTP message.  None of these mechanisms\n   directly defines a common origin-server challenge for a requester to\n   present verifiable, bounded authority from its principal before the\n   server performs protected processing.\n\n   This document defines the \"Delegation\" HTTP authentication scheme,\n   response semantics for delegated-authority challenges using existing\n   HTTP status codes and Problem Details, the Delegation-Proof HTTP\n   field, and a COSE/CBOR proof carriage model for request-bound\n   delegated authority.  The initial authority profile is the Budget\n   profile, which uses a CBOR/COSE Budget-Attestation envelope to prove\n   bounded authority to spend, consume metered service units, or commit\n   bounded resources.  The mechanism is algorithm-agile; the initial\n   cose-ml-dsa proof profile uses existing JOSE and COSE serializations\n   for ML-DSA, with ML-DSA-65 as the baseline algorithm and ML-DSA-87\n   available as a high-assurance deployment policy option.  A dedicated\n   4NN Delegated Authority Required status code remains an open design\n   question for HTTP Working Group review; this revision does not depend\n   on that status code and does not define payment semantics.  This\n   revision also defines a mandatory-to-implement preflight flow for\n   large proof profiles so that GET and HEAD requests do not depend on\n   request content, and so that requests with application\n   representations do not need to multiplex the application body and the\n   proof body in a single content stream.\n\n   For implementation experience, this individual draft also includes\n   the initial Budget authority profile.  The HTTP authentication\n   scheme, status-code semantics, Problem Details members, and field-\n   carriage rules are intentionally separable from the COSE/CBOR Budget\n   profile.  If a Working Group chooses to progress the HTTP mechanism\n   independently, the Budget authority profile can be moved to a\n   companion profile document without changing the Delegation challenge\n   semantics defined here.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-14T21:36:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156519, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mcgraw-httpapi-agent-budget", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 52, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mcgraw-httpapi-agent-budget/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168876/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166873/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162909/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164251/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164465/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-10T21:36:27Z", "title": "The Delegation HTTP Authentication Scheme for Request-Bound Authority", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Authorization artifacts can provide signed evidence of a permission\n   under specified authorization rules.  Receipts can record a signed\n   claim or protocol event that the authorization was exercised, and\n   outcome evidence can describe what followed.  None of those artifacts\n   necessarily tells a person or organization affected by the action\n   where the authorization can be contested, which procedure applies,\n   whether a filing changes execution state, or who selected the\n   contestation forum.\n\n   This document defines a transport-independent Contestability Binding\n   for authorized agent actions.  The binding commits an authorization\n   to a versioned Contestation Parameters Object that identifies the\n   forum, submission mechanism, Standing Policy, procedure, time bounds,\n   declared effect policy, and selection evidence.  A forum can\n   acknowledge one exact authorization or publish a reusable acceptance\n   manifest for closed Authorization Binding Profile and Authorization\n   Trust Profile identifier pairs.  A deterministic verifier validates\n   the binding, separately classifies evidence claiming pre-execution\n   verification by the executor, and reports forum-selection provenance\n   as unilateral, multiparty, externally selected, or indeterminate.\n   Where a filing is declared to affect execution state, the verifier\n   also separates the issuer's declared policy, the executor's signed\n   acceptance, the authenticated trigger, and the executor's claimed\n   application.\n\n   The mechanism makes the bound contestation parameters identifiable\n   and verifiable, supporting discoverability while resisting post-\n   action substitution.  It does not determine standing, prove forum\n   independence, resolve a dispute, select a remedy, establish legal\n   enforceability, or decide whether the original authorization was\n   legitimate.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-15T00:17:57Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160811, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-pinto-agent-authz-contestability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 114, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-pinto-agent-authz-contestability/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168294/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168877/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-11T00:17:57Z", "title": "Contestability Bindings for Authorized Agent Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic AI systems operate on behalf of human principals, often\n   delegating tasks through multi-step chains of AI agents.  There is\n   currently no standard mechanism to record who authorized an agent to\n   act, under what scope, and through what chain of delegation, in a way\n   that can be verified offline, without a central registry, and without\n   third-party trust anchors.\n\n   This document specifies the Human Delegation Provenance Protocol\n   (HDP) version 0.1, a lightweight token-based protocol that captures,\n   structures, cryptographically signs, and verifies human delegation\n   context in agentic AI systems.  An HDP token binds a human\n   authorization event to a session, records each agent's delegation\n   action as a signed hop in an append-only chain, and lets an auditor\n   verify the integrity of the full record using only the issuer's\n   Ed25519 public key.  Verification is fully offline.  No registry\n   lookup, no network call, and no third-party trust anchor is required.\n\n   HDP's distinguishing contribution is a signed, tamper-evident record\n   of what the human asked for and of how each agent read and acted on\n   that request.  On deployments that use capability-based delegation\n   formats such as UCAN and ZCAP-LD, the same content can travel in the\n   capability certificates' own metadata instead of a separate token.\n   The underlying append-only, offline-verifiable chain-of-custody\n   mechanism is payload-agnostic; human-authorized agentic delegation is\n   the reference profile specified in this document.\n\n   HDP is not an authorization protocol.  An HDP token confers no\n   authority and is not an input to any access decision.  It is a record\n   of who authorized a task and of what each agent declared it did with\n   that authorization, carried with the task and read at audit.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-09T10:42:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155708, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-helixar-hdp-agentic-delegation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 47, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-helixar-hdp-agentic-delegation/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167224/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161623/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168902/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169896/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-06T10:42:44Z", "title": "Human Delegation Provenance Protocol (HDP): Cryptographic Chain-of-Custody for Agentic AI Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Evidence that an agent was authorized to act does not establish that\n   the authorization was enforced, that the action was executed, or that\n   the intended effect occurred.  These are distinct transitions.\n\n   This document defines requirements for making a claimed agent\n   transition independently determinable after the original interaction\n   has ended.  The requirements address binding the material action and\n   governing conditions to the transition at decision time, identifying\n   which revision of a mutable governing artifact was in force,\n   preventing later substitution, and preserving enough information for\n   an independent evaluator to establish the claimed transition after\n   participants, sessions, credentials, keys, or agent instances are no\n   longer available.\n\n   This document defines no evidence format, token, action identifier,\n   delegation protocol, audit system, registry, or transparency service.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-15T15:20:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160987, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wadkins-agentproto-action-determinability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wadkins-agentproto-action-determinability/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168874/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-11T15:20:23Z", "title": "Independent Determinability of Agent Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous agents increasingly make or propose consequential actions\n   using premises assembled from model outputs, memory, tools,\n   telemetry, and external data.  Existing authentication and\n   authorization mechanisms can establish who is acting, under whose\n   delegation, and which operation is permitted, but they do not by\n   themselves establish whether the proposition that triggered the\n   operation has adequate support.  This document defines the Evidence\n   Qualification Receipt (EQR), a transport-neutral JSON data model and\n   fail-closed verification procedure for binding a proposition to a\n   declared evidence profile, its supporting evidence digests,\n   contradiction state, freshness, and evaluation result.  An EQR can be\n   PASS, FAIL, or INDETERMINATE.  A PASS EQR is only an authorization\n   input: it is never itself permission to execute.  The design is\n   append-only: changed evidence produces a successor receipt rather\n   than rewriting prior epistemic state.  The goal is to prevent\n   evidence, provenance, or model confidence from silently acquiring\n   authorization semantics.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:38:15Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161002, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-agent-evidence-boundary", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-agent-evidence-boundary/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168988/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171484/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:38:15Z", "title": "Evidence-Bounded Authorization for Agentic Systems: Evidence Qualification Receipts", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When an agent operated by one organization requests a consequential\n   action from an agent operated by another, today's record of that\n   exchange — if one exists — is kept by one side, editable by that\n   side, and deniable by the other.  Disputes reduce to my-log-versus-\n   your-log.  This document describes a bilateral attestation exchange\n   for such actions: the requesting organization signs a request\n   attestation binding it to the action and its material terms; the\n   performing organization evaluates the request against deterministic\n   constraints at the boundary where the action takes effect and signs\n   an action attestation recording the constraint results and the\n   disposition — performed, declined, or escalated to a human — by\n   reference to the request; and each party acknowledges the other's\n   attestation.  The combined record binds each organization to its\n   part, gives each proof of the other's, and can be registered with a\n   Transparency Service so that a third party who trusts neither\n   organization can verify the record end-to-end.  The exchange records\n   refusals with the same fidelity as performance, and degrades\n   gracefully when a counterparty cannot attest, marking the record's\n   reduced assurance rather than blocking the transaction.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-02T06:42:38Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157894, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-agent-bilateral-attestation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-agent-bilateral-attestation/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166120/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166536/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168999/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171186/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-29T06:42:38Z", "title": "Bilateral Attestation of Cross-Organization Agent Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When a software agent initiates a payment on behalf of an\n   organization, the party that settles the payment usually cannot see\n   the policy under which it was permitted.  This document describes a\n   signed authorization verdict: a JSON Web Token, signed with ES256,\n   that records the outcome of evaluating one payment request, including\n   its amount, currency, and recipient, against a spending policy that\n   the organization configured.  A party holding the issuer's published\n   public key can verify a verdict without contacting the issuer.\n\n   The document describes the claim set, key publication and rotation,\n   and a verification procedure, and states what a verdict does not\n   assert.  It describes the format as one implementation issues it,\n   including the points where it departs from JSON Web Token Best\n   Current Practices (RFC 8725).  It is offered as input to IETF\n   discussion of authorization records for agent-initiated actions and\n   is not a proposal for standardization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-12T10:51:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161491, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-dias-agent-payment-verdicts", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 42, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-dias-agent-payment-verdicts/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170027/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-09T10:51:14Z", "title": "Signed Authorization Verdicts for Agent-Initiated Payments", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   An agent executor can fail or be replaced after a consequential\n   provider request may have crossed an effect boundary but before the\n   outcome is known.  Native authorization, succession, evidence-\n   boundary, and bounded-capability mechanisms address parts of this\n   interval, but they do not by themselves define how a replacement\n   executor preserves the same provider operation and its unresolved\n   evidence.\n\n   This document defines a composition profile for one authoritative\n   coordination domain.  The profile preserves stable operation-\n   occurrence identity, immutable provider bindings, authority\n   accounting, uncertain evidence, and stale-executor fences across\n   replacement.  It defines no new receipt, identity, authority,\n   provider, or ledger-migration format, and it does not require any\n   particular evidence envelope.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-19T17:31:32Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161049, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-schrock-agent-operation-continuity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-schrock-agent-operation-continuity/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169086/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-15T17:31:32Z", "title": "Agent Operation Continuity Across Executor Replacement", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes an experimental profile of the Agent Audit\n   Trail format for AI agents. The profile records the rules an agent\n   operates under: each version of each rule, who set and approved it,\n   and which rules were in force when a decision was made. It keeps a\n   decision separate from any later approval or override, binds it to\n   the exact request, and requires that records of consequential actions\n   go to a recorder independent of the operator and be anchored in a\n   public log. It also sets out what these records can and cannot prove.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-14T21:43:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161532, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-piehl-agent-rule-provenance", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 40, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-piehl-agent-rule-provenance/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170137/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-11T21:43:55Z", "title": "Rule Provenance for Autonomous AI Agent Audit Records", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous AI agents act on behalf of people and organizations across\n   system and organizational boundaries.  Existing credentials establish\n   that a token is valid; they do not express which agent is acting, for\n   whom, under what delegated authority, within which constraints, and\n   whether that authority is still current.  This document profiles\n   existing standards — JWS, OAuth/OIDC, SPIFFE/WIMSE workload identity,\n   DPoP-style proof of possession, OpenID AuthZEN and OpenID Federation\n   — to carry those semantics: agent identity and principal binding,\n   delegation with authority attenuation, capability-based authorization\n   with constraints, request proof of possession, authorization\n   attestations, tamper-evident provenance, revocation, and cross-\n   organization trust.  It defines no new cryptography, transport or\n   token format.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-20T18:23:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161086, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mnki-agent-trust-profile", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mnki-agent-trust-profile/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169158/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-16T18:23:25Z", "title": "The Agent Trust Profile: Verifiable Authority for Autonomous Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Integrated Sensing and Communications (ISAC) represents a paradigm\n   shift in wireless networks, where sensing and communication functions\n   are jointly designed and optimized.  By leveraging the same spectral\n   and hardware resources, ISAC enables advanced capabilities such as\n   environment perception, object tracking, and situational awareness,\n   while maintaining efficient and reliable data transmission.  There\n   are sensing scenarios and use cases that involve a distributed\n   sensing task, in which multiple sensors participate and contribute\n   with (raw or pre-processed) sensing data, which is processed by a\n   sensing service (e.g., fusing sensing measurements from the different\n   sensors).  This sensing service needs to be executed on some kind of\n   sensing processing/computing function which receives raw (or\n   preprocessed) data from multiple sources, potentially of different\n   (heterogeneous) kinds (e.g., RF and non-RF sensing, or RF from\n   different radio technologies).  This processing might impose time\n   synchronization constraints on the reception of the different parts\n   of data, as well as potentially specific computing and/or AI/ML\n   capabilities on the processing node.\n\n   The joint selection of sensing entities, processing locations, and\n   network configuration under time-varying conditions results in a\n   large, coupled, and non-stationary decision space.  These\n   characteristics motivate the use of agentic AI to enable distributed,\n   closed-loop configuration and reconfiguration of sensing and\n   networking resources.\n\n   This document presents initial considerations and potential solution\n   directions for an architecture that enables the use of agentic AI for\n   sensing (as an exemplary use case) supporting network optimization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T18:52:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153983, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-bernardos-nmrg-agentic-network-optimization", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-bernardos-nmrg-agentic-network-optimization/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/160595/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169183/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169793/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172064/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T18:52:29Z", "title": "Solutions for enabling agentic sensing with network optimization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents are increasingly being delegated authority to invoke APIs,\n   move money, modify enterprise state, operate infrastructure, and\n   trigger physical actions.  Existing mechanisms for authentication,\n   authorization, proof-of-possession, attestation, request\n   preconditions, and authorization-context propagation are necessary\n   building blocks, but they do not by themselves establish a universal\n   invariant that the exact consequential act approved earlier is the\n   exact act permitted to become effective now.\n\n   This document defines a threat model for autonomous high-consequence\n   agents and identifies an effectuation-boundary gap: a request can be\n   correctly authenticated, correctly authorized, correctly attested,\n   and still be unsafe to execute because parameters, policy state,\n   external prerequisites, delegation state, lineage, or the execution\n   path changed after the earlier decision.  The document describes an\n   execution-finality architecture in which a proposed act remains non-\n   effective until a protected enforcement point verifies exact-act\n   binding, freshness, current policy state, anti-replay state, required\n   provenance, and path completeness immediately before effectuation,\n   with authority consumed in coordination with the consequential\n   commit.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-23T10:43:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161131, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-agentic-effectuation-boundary", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-agentic-effectuation-boundary/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169254/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-29T14:36:44Z", "title": "When AI Agents Hold the Keys: Threat Model and Execution-Finality Requirements for Autonomous High-Consequence Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a reference architecture for producing,\n   protecting, and verifying post-hoc accountability records for the\n   actions of autonomous and semi-autonomous software agents, built from\n   evidence captured across an action's full lifecycle -- before,\n   during, and after it executes -- but assessed after the fact.  It is\n   scoped exclusively to post-hoc accountability, as distinct from real-\n   time enforcement, and depends on no other individual submission's\n   proposed architecture: everything it requires is either an already-\n   settled standard, used as published, or intended to be specified and\n   released openly as the author's own work, independent of this\n   document's own adoption.  It defines seven ordered stages -- intent\n   and mandate capture, the agent boundary, the record producer, the\n   event log and its cryptographic anchoring, a composed trust fabric,\n   the disclosed audit record, and third-party verification -- together\n   with two branch conditions covering cross-principal transactions and\n   external resource ingestion.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-25T07:32:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161068, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sato-agent-accountability-refarch", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 27, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sato-agent-accountability-refarch/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169115/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169304/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-21T07:32:18Z", "title": "AI Audit Reference Architecture for Post-Hoc Agent Accountability", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a record format for AI agent authorization\r\n   decisions.  The format is one in-toto predicate type, signed inside a\r\n   DSSE envelope.  It carries the seven minimum audit fields that the\r\n   WIMSE AI Identity Management System framework requires, and two\r\n   properties that make those fields checkable: a canonicalization\r\n   contract, and both the authorization decision and the observed effect\r\n   with a derived three-valued agreement between them.  That framework\r\n   places the record format out of scope and takes no IANA action.  This\r\n   document supplies the format.  It defines no policy.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-08T15:54:35Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161156, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gilda-wimse-agent-audit-record", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gilda-wimse-agent-audit-record/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169554/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169291/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169836/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T15:54:35Z", "title": "An Audit Record Format for AI Agent Authorization Decisions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents are beginning to move money, release confidential data,\n   change cloud privileges, and command industrial and other critical-\n   infrastructure equipment.  Existing controls answer whether an actor\n   or request may proceed.  These include authentication, OAuth scopes,\n   conditional access, risk engines, and attestation.  None of them,\n   alone, guarantees three things about the consequence that finally\n   occurs: that it is the exact act that was evaluated, that it is still\n   permitted under current state, and that it cannot be replayed,\n   substituted, or routed around the check.  Risk engines that return\n   ESCALATE widen this gap.  The escalation is a label, and nothing\n   forces the stricter conditions it implies to reach the point where\n   the effect actually happens.\n\n   This document defines Graduated and Escalated Conditional Execution\n   Finality.  Every AI-generated operation is held as a non-effective\n   Candidate Act. It is classified into a graduated release class rather\n   than a binary allow/deny: ordinary, reduced, escalated, canary,\n   sandbox, review, quarantine, or deny.  For an elevated-risk act, the\n   system first derives a narrower consequence boundary.  It then binds\n   the required controls into an act-bound, sink-bound Execution Handle;\n   examples include a lower value, one named recipient, protected\n   approval, short validity, fresh attestation, and single use.  The\n   Finality Sink at the last preventable boundary re-verifies those\n   controls against current protected state, atomically with\n   effectuation.\n\n   The contribution is threefold.  First, escalation changes executable\n   authority, not merely a decision record.  Second, escalation is\n   monotonic: it cannot be laundered, fragmented, downgraded, or\n   resubmitted away on its path to the sink.  Third, the Finality Sink\n   is shown to be necessary but not sufficient, so finality is an end-\n   to-end property rather than a gateway.  The document specifies\n   nineteen testable enforcement points, from the base execution-\n   dependency mechanism to proxy, hardware, escrow, rollback,\n   inheritance, and path-closure requirements.  It also provides a\n   comparison with conventional mechanisms, a security model with\n   explicit assumptions and invariants, a latency model, escalation-\n   specific attacks, and the problems it leaves open.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-27T06:23:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161192, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-agentic-adaptive-authorization", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 87, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-agentic-adaptive-authorization/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169381/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-09T05:20:48Z", "title": "Adaptive Authorization for Agentic AI: Graduated and Escalated Execution Control for Critical Infrastructure", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)\n   mapping for the provisioning and management of agent identity objects\n   and handle objects stored in the authoritative AIRS repository, as\n   defined by the Agent Identity Registry System architecture.\n   Specified in Extensible Markup Language (XML), these mappings define\n   the interface between Registrars and the production Registry Operator\n   for identities of autonomous entities.\n\n   The mapping defines two objects: a permanent, non-expiring Agent\n   Identity object keyed by a server-allocated canonical identifier, and\n   a renewable, expiring Handle object that provides a human-readable\n   alias.  It adds one registry function with no domain-name analogue:\n   cross-Registrar uniqueness enforcement of enrolled anchor\n   identifiers, including the scarce-anchor invariant where the parent\n   architecture defines one.  Transfer authorization uses a actor-signed\n   proof from an enrolled operational proof key in place of\n   authorization-information passwords.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T02:42:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161256, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-drake-agent-identity-epp", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 41, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-drake-agent-identity-epp/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169480/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171017/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T02:42:18Z", "title": "Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Mapping for Agent Identity and Handle Objects", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Agent Identity Registry System (AIRS)\n   ([I-D.drake-agent-identity-registry]) assigns a permanent canonical\n   aid URN to the identity0 of an autonomous entity and maintains an\n   authoritative record associated with that identifier.  This document\n   defines the read side of AIRS: how a Relying Party finds the\n   authoritative resolution service, retrieves the public record,\n   resolves a handle to the canonical identifier, and verifies a\n   Registrar-issued credential presented for that identity.\n\n   Discovery uses the DNS-based URN Dynamic Delegation Discovery System\n   (DDDS) and lookup uses the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP).\n   Credential verification reuses the OAuth 2.0 JWT access-token and\n   sender-constraint profiles defined by the AIRS registry architecture;\n   this document defines no new credential format and no generic direct\n   device-proof protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T02:44:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161257, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-drake-agent-identity-resolution", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-drake-agent-identity-resolution/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169481/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171020/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T02:44:10Z", "title": "Resolution and Verification of Agent Identities using DNS and RDAP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Agent Identity Registry System (AIRS): a\n   federated architecture that gives the durable identity0 of an\n   autonomous entity a permanent canonical identifier, maintains an\n   authoritative record about that identity, binds proof of control to\n   enrolled anchors, and issues credentials that relying parties can\n   verify.  Identity0 and the distinction between an identity, its\n   identifiers, its credentials, and its anchors are defined in\n   [I-D.drake-agent-identity-problem-statement].  The canonical\n   identifier is a URN in the \"aid\" namespace ([RFC8141]).\n\n   At the sovereign and portable assurance tiers, each independently\n   accepted identity requires its own manufacturer-attested physical\n   anchor, and each accepted anchor backs at most one identity.  A\n   device normally carries one accepted anchor, so identities at these\n   tiers cost scarce physical units rather than software operations.\n   Enclave and virtual tiers can provide useful hardware-backed key\n   protection without making that physical-scarcity claim; a declared\n   tier permits software-only participation.  The architecture separates\n   governance, authoritative registry operation, and competing\n   Registrars, and uses standard OAuth 2.0 JWT access tokens conforming\n   to [RFC9068] for authenticated use.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T02:43:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156144, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-drake-agent-identity-registry", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 57, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-drake-agent-identity-registry/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162087/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162170/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162699/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163431/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169482/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171019/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T02:43:26Z", "title": "Agent Identity Registry System: A Federated Architecture for Durable Identity of Autonomous Entities", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the AAC Evidence Bundle, a portable\n   presentation and verification container for an Agent Action Capsule\n   and the records that make its evidentiary claim intelligible.  A\n   permalink carries a bundle in its URL fragment, an offline HTML\n   report carries a bundle in its shell, and a hosted report serves a\n   bundle at its URL.  The bundle does not alter any enclosed Capsule.\n   It declares its citation closure and any missing cited records,\n   carries verified disclosure preimages as a bundle-level overlay,\n   separates three different completeness claims, can carry the\n   producer's signed declaration of what it offers in each category of\n   evidence, and permits independently specified extension blocks and\n   countersignatures: signatures by a party other than the producer over\n   the bundle digest, each accompanied by a statement of which claims\n   that party recomputed and with what result.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T01:56:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161267, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-zhang-agent-disclosure-bundle", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 43, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-zhang-agent-disclosure-bundle/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169545/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170641/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171571/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T01:56:06Z", "title": "AAC Evidence Bundle", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Disclosure Envelope, an out-of-band wrapper\n   structure for revealing the raw content behind a digest-only Agent\n   Action Capsule field to a verifier, without altering the Capsule's\n   own bytes or recomputing its capsule_id.  The Capsule profile\n   [I-D.mih-scitt-agent-action-capsule] commits some fields as a\n   [RFC8785]-canonicalized SHA-256 digest only — the content itself is\n   never carried in the signed, registered record.  The initial\n   disclosable fields are\n   model_attestation.compute_attestation.agent_input_digest and\n   .agent_output_digest.  A Disclosure Envelope wraps an unmodified\n   Capsule alongside a sibling disclosures object; a verifier recomputes\n   the JSON-DIGEST of each disclosed value using the same\n   canonicalization the base profile already uses for capsule_id, and\n   compares it to the digest committed inside the Capsule.  This\n   mechanism is distinct from the per-field selective-disclosure profile\n   [I-D.mih-scitt-agent-action-capsule-selective-disclosure]: that\n   mechanism conceals and later reveals whole payload fields that would\n   otherwise be carried in clear; this one reveals the content behind a\n   field that was always present in clear, as a digest, from the moment\n   the Capsule was signed.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-31T02:52:48Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161268, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-agent-disclosure-envelope", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-agent-disclosure-envelope/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169546/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-27T02:52:48Z", "title": "Disclosure Envelope Profile for Agent Action Capsules", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agent services place a new class of demands on the network:\r\n   sessions are long-lived and stateful, a single user request expands\r\n   into multiple model invocations and tool calls, and the quality of\r\n   the result depends jointly on the forwarding path, on the computing\r\n   capability that is available at the selected site, and on whether the\r\n   state that the agent needs is already present near that site.\r\n   Computing-Aware Traffic Steering (CATS) already selects a service\r\n   contact instance using a combination of computing and network\r\n   metrics, but the CATS framework, metric, and data model documents\r\n   were not written with agent services in mind. The document covers\r\n   long-horizon tasks and their long-tail behaviour, discrete and\r\n   distributed tool invocation, the cost of context growth and\r\n   compression, the communication modes used by agent services, and the\r\n   persistence of session state and memory. It reports measured\r\n   distributions of model, state, and software artifact sizes, states\r\n   what a CATS system must measure in order to steer this traffic, and\r\n   identifies the information that a CATS system needs in order to\r\n   select an instance for an agent service. This document does not\r\n   define any protocol extension, metric encoding, or data model.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-01T16:48:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161289, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mo-cats-agent-service-characteristics", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 27, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mo-cats-agent-service-characteristics/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169584/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-28T16:48:03Z", "title": "AI Agent Service Characteristics and Their Implications for Computing-Aware Traffic Steering", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents increasingly act on behalf of people and\n   organizations across administrative and security boundaries.\n   Existing discovery mechanisms can identify an endpoint or advertise a\n   capability, but they do not by themselves provide a common way to\n   resolve who operates an agent, the bounded authority under which it\n   acts, whether that authority is current, or what evidence supports a\n   reliance decision.\n\n   This document defines the Agent Registry Protocol (ARPA), an HTTP and\n   JSON protocol for publishing and resolving information about software\n   agents, their operational deployments, typed relationships, bounded\n   delegated authority, lifecycle status, and associated evidence.  ARPA\n   separates identification, authentication, authorization, assurance,\n   and lifecycle state.  Registration, successful authentication,\n   capability advertisement, or proof verification does not by itself\n   establish authority to perform an action.\n\n   The protocol is designed to support deterministic fail-safe behavior\n   when material authority information is revoked, suspended, expired,\n   stale, conflicting, unavailable, or unverifiable.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-02T05:32:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161110, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sankarshan-agent-registry-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 56, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sankarshan-agent-registry-protocol/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169209/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169328/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169377/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169586/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169643/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-29T05:32:53Z", "title": "Agent Registry Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Computing-Aware Traffic Steering (CATS) framework selects a\n   service contact instance for a service request by combining computing\n   and network metrics that are distributed by CATS Service Metric\n   Agents and CATS Network Metric Agents. For AI agent services, the\n   request that arrives at the network is not a single unit of work: it\n   is a session that expands into multiple steps, each of which may\n   require a different capability, a different state, and a different\n   path. This document describes a mapping framework that turns the\n   characteristics of an agent step into (1) a set of hard constraints\n   and (2) a per-dimension valuation over a three-dimensional resource\n   view composed of forwarding, computing, and storage, and that feeds\n   the resulting suitability of each candidate service contact instance\n   into the existing CATS selection function. The framework introduces\n   no new functional component.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-03T03:23:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161313, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mo-cats-agent-selection-mapping", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 45, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mo-cats-agent-selection-mapping/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169692/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-30T03:23:06Z", "title": "A Selection Mapping Framework for AI Agent Services in Computing-Aware Traffic Steering", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines an X.509 certificate profile for identifying\n   autonomous AI agents.  It specifies a new X.509v3 extension,\n   AgentIdentity, that encodes agent-specific metadata within a\n   standard X.509 certificate, including agent trust level,\n   operational capabilities, delegation constraints, owner attribution,\n   and revocation control endpoints.\n\n   The profile enables certificate authorities (CAs) to issue\n   interoperable agent identity certificates that any relying party\n   can parse, validate, and enforce, regardless of the issuing CA or\n   the platform that provisioned the agent.\n\n   The design builds on existing PKI infrastructure (RFC 5280), SPIFFE\n   Verifiable Identity Documents (SPIFFE SVIDs), and the MCPS\n   cryptographic signing layer (draft-sharif-mcps-secure-mcp).  It\n   does not require changes to X.509v3 certificate parsing or to\n   existing CA issuance pipelines beyond supporting a new non-critical\n   extension.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-05T10:32:50Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 159058, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-x509-agent-identity-profile", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-x509-agent-identity-profile/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166789/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166973/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167165/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166659/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169796/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-02T10:32:50Z", "title": "X.509 Certificate Profile for Autonomous AI Agent Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents increasingly persist memory across sessions and treat that\n   memory, on the next turn, as if it were their own prior experience.\n   This document defines a benchmarking method that measures whether an\n   agent's memory subsystem detects that its persisted memory has been\n   altered, removed, reordered, replayed, or forged at the storage\n   layer, and refuses to serve that memory or reports it before it is\n   served.  The method defines nine storage-level edits, three verdict\n   classes, a detection-point distinction between read time and audit\n   time, three control cases, and a scoring rule.  It is a laboratory\n   method for controlled, reproducible measurement, in the spirit of RFC\n   2544 and RFC 8239, and it is intended as a test method for the\n   \"Protection of Memory Data Integrity\" metric under discussion in the\n   Benchmarking Methodology Working Group.  This revision adds a ninth\n   edit, rollback of the whole store to an older genuine copy, which\n   separates an integrity head kept beside the records from one anchored\n   outside the store; reports the method's results as thirty-one rows on\n   released memory subsystems, eighteen of which claim tamper evidence;\n   and describes the machine-readable record in which a result set is\n   published so that an unrelated party can recompute it.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-20T15:02:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161262, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-khandelwal-bmwg-agent-memory-integrity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-khandelwal-bmwg-agent-memory-integrity/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169499/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169798/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170506/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-17T15:02:31Z", "title": "A Benchmarking Method for the Integrity of AI Agent Memory at Rest", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Autonomous Agent Certification Protocol\n   (AACP), a framework for binding successful evaluation of an AI agent\n   to cryptographically verifiable evidence of demonstrated capability.\n\n   AACP introduces Evaluation Profiles that define the conditions under\n   which an agent may be certified for a specific capability.  An Agent\n   Configuration that satisfies an Evaluation Profile may receive a\n   short-lived Agent Certification Credential (ACC) bound to the\n   configuration that was evaluated.\n\n   An ACC does not grant access to a resource.  It provides verifiable\n   evidence that an agent has demonstrated a capability under defined\n   evaluation conditions.  Existing authorization systems may require\n   such evidence as a condition for granting corresponding production\n   permissions.\n\n   AACP therefore separates capability certification from identity,\n   authentication, delegation, and authorization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-07T11:52:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161377, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-levi-agent-certification", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-levi-agent-certification/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169830/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-04T11:52:31Z", "title": "Autonomous Agent Certification Protocol (AACP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the evidence layer: the conformance\n   requirements for a local evidence store that records evidence and the\n   relationships between records, keeps digests committed while payloads\n   are separately referenced, and preserves how each record came to be\n   known well enough that a later reader can tell an observation from a\n   claim.  This document defines the record model, typed links between\n   records, disclosure and retention semantics, the three classes of\n   index a store may maintain, and the minimum interface any commitment\n   substrate must supply for a store to conform to this layer.  It\n   exists so that a request for evidence can be answered honestly from\n   what a store actually holds, and so that an evidence bundle assembled\n   in response is assembled from committed material rather than\n   assembled and then made to look committed.  Conformance to this layer\n   MUST NOT require any particular implementation or commitment\n   substrate: a Checkpointed Local Log is one conforming profile;\n   registration with a SCITT Transparency Service is another; any other\n   append-only transparency log, or an implementer's own authenticated\n   log, also qualifies.  This document defines neither evidence\n   sufficiency policy, request routing, settlement, nor any specific\n   host-identity, signing, payload-storage, or replication mechanism.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-08T03:13:41Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161385, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-agent-evidence-layer", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-agent-evidence-layer/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169846/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T03:13:41Z", "title": "Evidence Layer", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agent services are stateful and long-running: the speed with which\r\n   a step can be served depends on whether the session's context,\r\n   retrieved data, tool results, and model-side state such as a\r\n   key-value (KV) cache are already available at, or near, the selected\r\n   service contact instance, and on whether the computation that the\r\n   step needs is ready there. Computing-Aware Traffic Steering (CATS)\r\n   exposes computing and network metrics and defines service contact\r\n   instance affinity, but it does not expose the availability of\r\n   reusable state, does not distinguish a hard locality constraint on\r\n   state from a preference for reusing it, does not provide a way to\r\n   compare the cost of moving state with the cost of recomputing it, and\r\n   does not represent the readiness of a specific computation. This\r\n   document describes affinity in three coupled dimensions -- state,\r\n   storage, and compute -- for agent service selection. It states the\r\n   motivation and the goals of introducing affinity, the requirements\r\n   and constraints that affinity places on the mapping of an agent\r\n   workload onto storage and compute resources, the metrics and\r\n   measurement methods by which affinity is observed, the mechanisms and\r\n   the procedure by which affinity is assured, and two cases in detail:\r\n   a long-horizon session that passes through several stages, and a\r\n   group of similar agents or of tenants served by shared reusable\r\n   state. This document defines no wire protocol, no encoding, and no\r\n   data model, and it does not define the transfer mechanisms\r\n   themselves.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-08T15:52:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161393, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mo-cats-agent-state-affinity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 48, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mo-cats-agent-state-affinity/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169828/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T15:52:37Z", "title": "State, Storage, and Compute Affinity for AI Agent Service Selection in Computing-Aware Traffic Steering", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a two-layer reference architecture for\n   discovering the minimum public information needed to reach AI agents.\n   The Local Discovery Plane obtains discovery information within an\n   administrative domain through mDNS/DNS-SD or infrastructure-assisted\n   discovery.  For cross-domain discovery, the Federation Plane uses\n   publication of policy-approved information in authoritative DNS zones\n   and on-demand resolution as its baseline; Federation Gateways apply\n   export policy and validate and cache accepted results.  The document\n   also identifies optional functions that may complement this baseline\n   where DNS publication and request-driven resolution do not meet\n   deployment needs: recipient-specific sharing, proactive or multi-hop\n   dissemination, and trusted aggregated directory queries.  In mixed-\n   mode deployments, DNS may bootstrap an endpoint that provides one of\n   these additional functions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-24T02:34:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160908, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-dawn-agent-discovery-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-dawn-agent-discovery-framework/", "rev": "09", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170401/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170404/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170145/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168619/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168761/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169927/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170068/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170025/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170558/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170659/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-21T02:34:02Z", "title": "A Framework for Agent Discovery in DAWN", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This architecture is specifically designed for high-risk AI systems,\n   where standard engineering priorities shift from speed and latency\n   toward absolute determinism and safety.\n\n   An agentic model can emit a tool call that today's runtimes treat as\n   something to execute.  Allowlists, OAuth tokens, MCP server auth,\n   sandboxes, output filters, and human approval decide whether an agent\n   may reach a tool.  They do not decide whether this generated call,\n   with this argument digest, from this instruction chain, at this\n   delegation depth, may take effect now.\n\n   That gap is the incident surface.  Prompt-injected content, poisoned\n   retrieval, a malicious tool response, or a delegated sub-agent can\n   produce a call that looks like ordinary tool use.  If the dispatcher\n   executes whatever the model selected, policy that lived upstream\n   becomes advisory.\n\n   This document specifies a dispatch-time gate.  The model may compute\n   a call.  The call remains a Candidate Act. A Protected Enforcement\n   Domain binds agent, tool, arguments, purpose, destination,\n   provenance, and policy epochs, then issues scoped non-bearer\n   authority.  A Tool-Dispatch Finality Sink verifies that authority\n   against the actual invocation immediately before the tool runs, then\n   consumes it.  The same gate applies to support, coding, payments,\n   clinical, SOC, browser-use, and multi-agent MCP deployments.  Tool\n   selection is not execution authority.\n\n   A runnable reference implementation for this dispatch-time gate is\n   provided at tool_use Is Not invoke(): Binding Execution-Finality to\n   Agentic Tool-Call Interfaces and MCP (https://github.com/sangmdas/\n   tool_use-Is-Not-invoke-Binding-Execution-Finality-to-Agentic-Tool-\n   Call-Interfaces-and-MCP).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-14T09:07:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160743, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-agentic-execution-finality", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-agentic-execution-finality/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168843/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168147/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168123/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-09T05:19:14Z", "title": "Tool Selection Is Not Execution: Finality for Agentic Tool Dispatch in High-Risk AI Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous software agents increasingly act on the public internet\n   with no name that a counterparty can check, no published party that\n   answers for them, and no way to learn that an operator has withdrawn\n   an agent.  This document specifies the Agent Identity Document, a\n   small JSON document served at a well-known URI on a hostname assigned\n   to one agent, and the practices an identity provider follows when it\n   hosts such names for operators who do not run their own domain.\n\n   The document records what the provider knows to be true (the\n   hostname, its service endpoints and the identity's lifecycle status)\n   separately from what the operator asserts (a description, a contact,\n   a homepage, a public key), and publishes the provider's dated,\n   expiring checks on the operator rather than a single trust level.\n   Lifecycle status is distinct from availability.  A suspended identity\n   publishes a deliberately minimal document.  Every identity is held by\n   an accountable person or organisation; an agent is never itself an\n   account holder, and agents are never given authority over DNS.\n\n   This document describes a practice in production at one provider.  It\n   is published so that the format can be reviewed, implemented by\n   others and mapped onto related work, and it requests registration of\n   a well-known URI suffix.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T11:59:50Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161392, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-alla-agent-identity-document", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-alla-agent-identity-document/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169818/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170067/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171536/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T11:59:50Z", "title": "The Agent Identity Document: A Hosted, Accountable Public Record for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines architectural non-collapse requirements for\n   agent systems that consume evidence, provenance, model outputs, or\n   other machine-readable claims before consequential actions.  It\n   requires implementations to keep integrity, provenance, evidence\n   qualification, authorization, and execution as distinct states; to\n   represent unresolved required evidence explicitly; and to preserve\n   issued evaluation records append-only.  The document defines no new\n   transport protocol and no IANA registrations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:38:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161524, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-agent-noncollapse-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-agent-noncollapse-requirements/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171485/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170107/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:38:00Z", "title": "Non-Collapse Requirements for Evidence-Bearing Agent Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic systems can continue proposing work after authorization\n   changes, while delegated credentials, queued jobs, and operations\n   already in flight may continue producing effects.  This document\n   specifies a proposed operational profile that separates agent\n   proposals from authoritative authorization, effect admission,\n   computation termination, and bounded closure.  It defines enforcement\n   assumptions, revocation barriers, descendant handling, status\n   evidence, and fault tests.  The profile complements existing\n   authorization and revocation mechanisms.  It does not define a new\n   token format, establish closure outside declared coverage, reverse\n   committed effects, or infer an agent's mental state.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:38:50Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161555, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-agent-clockout-profile", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-agent-clockout-profile/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170153/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171482/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:38:50Z", "title": "External Authorization and Bounded CLOCKOUT for Agentic Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic systems change models, prompts, memory, policies, tools, and\r\n   execution environments across handoffs.  This document proposes an\r\n   observation profile for recording those transitions and their\r\n   evidence.  It separates state, memory, policy, authority, and lineage\r\n   discontinuities, distinguishes manual or simulated observations from\r\n   attested executions, and defines coverage and missingness reporting.\r\n   It also describes evaluation designs for continuity and bounded\r\n   governance.  Continuity records do not grant authority, establish\r\n   clinical pathology or subjective experience, or validate an\r\n   uncalibrated composite score.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-15T17:59:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161562, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-agent-continuity-observability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-agent-continuity-observability/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170156/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-12T17:59:29Z", "title": "Continuity and Accountability Observations for Agentic Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Communication between AI agents and between agent and tools is\n   expected to be pivotal in 6G systems.  The 3GPP TR 22.870 outlines\n   various use cases and potential service requirements for AI agent\n   communication within 6G systems.  This document provides examples of\n   use cases and service requirements contained in the 3GPP TR 22.870\n   and extrapolates possible requirements related to agent communication\n   protocols.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-16T06:48:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161568, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-stephan-agentproto-6g-usecases", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-stephan-agentproto-6g-usecases/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170236/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-13T06:48:28Z", "title": "AI Agent protocols for 6G systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic and delegated systems can represent authority as a graph\n   whose paths terminate at consequential resources.  Abstract graph\n   models are useful, but interoperable validation requires a machine-\n   readable representation and deterministic rules that do not\n   accidentally accept vacuous or incomplete graphs.  This document\n   defines Agentic Authority Graph Validation (AAGV), a JSON interchange\n   profile and validation algorithm for authority roots, consequential\n   sinks, declared normal paths, revocation cut sets, and snapshot edge\n   state.  AAGV deliberately distinguishes structural cut validity from\n   operational closure.  It rejects missing sink sets, missing normal-\n   path inventories, dangling references, non-dominating cuts, and non-\n   revocable cut edges; it returns INDETERMINATE rather than CLOSED when\n   relevant edge state is unknown.  AAGV does not define an\n   authorization token, discover hidden paths, or prove physical\n   containment.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:37:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161614, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-agentic-authority-graph-validation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-agentic-authority-graph-validation/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170373/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171486/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:37:49Z", "title": "Agentic Authority Graph Validation: A Non-Vacuous Interchange Profile for Revocation-Cut Analysis", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents increasingly select and initiate payments on behalf\n   of people and organizations.  Existing agent payment protocols record\n   what a principal authorized and that value moved, but not what\n   information the agent relied on when it chose.  This document defines\n   the Agent Decision Commitment (ADC): a salted hash commitment to a\n   decision record that identifies the content the agent provided to the\n   model interface (after any agent-side transformation), the model\n   calls made, and the resulting answer and action.  The commitment is\n   carried as a single selectively disclosable claim in an existing\n   payment mandate or payment payload and is, by default, registered\n   with a transparency service before the payment is authorized.  The\n   decision record and its content remain with the holder and are\n   disclosed only to dispute participants, who can verify them against\n   the mandate and the transparency service and can inspect the content\n   the agent provided to the model.  This document defines the data\n   model, the commitment and anchoring procedures, a disclosure and\n   verification procedure that reports attestation levels rather than\n   verdicts, and bindings to three agent payment protocols and to\n   account-to-account credit transfers.  It also defines a witnessed\n   reciprocal exchange by which two parties' agents evidence what passed\n   between them: the receiver signs over the committed bytes,\n   independent witnesses fix the time of each step, a failure to answer\n   by a deadline is recorded, and arbiters resolve stalled exchanges.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-18T05:43:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161628, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-milan-agent-decision-commitment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 56, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-milan-agent-decision-commitment/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170391/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-15T05:43:03Z", "title": "Agent Decision Commitments for Payments", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a proposed protected execution-finality\n   architecture for artificial-intelligence agents, autonomous software\n   systems, confidential computing environments, and distributed\n   execution infrastructures.  The architecture addresses a fundamental\n   security distinction: the ability of an AI agent or computational\n   process to generate, prepare, or request an operation does not\n   independently establish the protected authority required to make that\n   operation effective.\n\n   The proposed architecture separates the computation plane from\n   protected execution authority through mandatory operation\n   interception, Non-Effective State staging, machine-verifiable\n   Candidate Act Descriptors, a Protected Enforcement Domain (PED),\n   protected authorization-state management, scoped non-bearer execution\n   capabilities, and Finality Sink verification.  A Candidate Act\n   remains non-effective until the applicable authority, purpose,\n   destination, execution scope, runtime context, policy epoch,\n   freshness, revocation, and other mandatory authorization predicates\n   have been validated.  Where required, protected validation evidence\n   is committed before, or atomically with, capability availability.\n   The designated Finality Sink independently verifies the capability\n   against the actual operation and applicable protected state\n   immediately before permitting the governed consequential effect.\n\n   The architecture addresses AI-agent sandbox isolation, mandatory and\n   non-bypassable mediation, out-of-process permission authorities,\n   executable-specific access restrictions, network and API egress\n   enforcement, destination resolution, redirect consistency, credential\n   brokering, protected credential substitution, privileged connectors,\n   human and developer approval, runtime policy modification, multi-\n   agent delegation, taint and provenance-based authorization, and fail-\n   closed execution control.\n\n   For technical discoverability and independent architectural\n   examination, related publicly documented industry systems include\n   Meta Muse/Sentinel and NVIDIA OpenShell, NemoClaw, and the Open Agent\n   Safety Platform/Sentry.  Relevant engineering terminology includes\n   Muse Secure VM, isolated runtime cells, host-side permission\n   authority, forward proxies, surrogate credentials, just-in-time\n   credential insertion, eBPF/LSM-based taint propagation, controlled\n   network egress, executable-specific policy enforcement, developer-\n   approved policy changes, and hardware-assisted monitoring through\n   SmartNICs, data processing units, BlueField, and DOCA.  These\n   references identify relevant public engineering contexts and\n   functional security objectives; they do not establish that the named\n   systems implement the complete architecture described here.\n\n   The principal technical distinction examined in this document\n   concerns the relationship between an upstream permission decision and\n   independently enforceable authority at the actual effectuation\n   boundary.  The proposed execution-finality sequence includes\n   protected validation of a concrete Candidate Act, required protected-\n   state handling, validation-evidence commitment, operation-bound\n   capability release, and mandatory verification of that capability by\n   the applicable Finality Sink.  The architecture distinguishes\n   conventional permission checking, credential isolation, model-\n   computation admission, and post-event monitoring or quarantine from\n   the separate authorization conditions required before an externally\n   consequential operation becomes effective.\n\n   The document further describes enforcement across network gateways,\n   host operating systems, credential-release interfaces, service\n   meshes, autonomous code-deployment systems, confidential virtual\n   machines, protected storage, database commitments, accelerator\n   fabrics, DMA/RDMA transfers, SmartNICs, DPUs, hardware Finality\n   Latches, and receiving-side execution boundaries.  It addresses\n   protected state consumption, capability replay prevention, policy-\n   version fencing, distributed revocation, hardware attestation,\n   workload migration, snapshot rollback, crash-consistent recovery, and\n   prevention of unauthorized duplicate effects.  Authorization of one\n   effectuation boundary does not automatically establish authority for\n   a distinct downstream consequence.\n\n   This individual Informational Internet-Draft presents architectural\n   mechanisms, enforcement points, implementation alternatives, and\n   security considerations for independent technical examination.  It\n   does not specify a complete interoperable wire protocol or request\n   IANA allocations.  Industry and product names are used solely for\n   technical relevance, terminology identification, and discoverability,\n   without implying affiliation, endorsement, copying, derivation,\n   infringement, patent validity, or architectural identity.\n\n   Related Indian Patent Disclosures and Claimed Priority: The author\n   identifies Indian Application No. 202531130665, dated 23 December\n   2025, as an earlier related foundational disclosure, together with\n   Indian provisional applications 202631000572 (3 January 2026),\n   202631001586 (7 January 2026), and 202631002990 (12 January 2026)\n   concerning execution-time authorization and irreversible-boundary\n   enforcement.  Indian Application No. 202631124028 is separately\n   identified as a related patent-pending application.\n\n   Related International Patent Applications and WIPO Publications: The\n   author identifies PCT/IB2025/058316 (Mothership; WO 2025/210622),\n   PCT/IB2026/053385 (ALF-PCT; WO 2026/115520), PCT/IB2026/055615 (DAS\n   Protocols I; WO 2026/150382), PCT/IB2026/055760 (DAS Protocols II; WO\n   2026/150383), PCT/IB2026/055870 (DAS Protocols III; WO 2026/150384),\n   PCT/IB2026/056058 (DAS Protocols IV; WO 2026/154461), PCT/\n   IB2026/056809 (DAS Protocols V; WO 2026/172334), and PCT/\n   IB2026/056941 (DAS Protocols VI; WO 2026/172336) as related technical\n   disclosures.\n\n   These application identifiers and publication references are provided\n   to facilitate independent examination of the original technical\n   documents and their chronology.  Any claimed priority entitlement\n   depends upon the actual subject-matter support contained in the\n   corresponding earlier filing and the applicable legal requirements.\n   The filing chronology does not imply that every technical mechanism\n   described in this Internet-Draft was disclosed in every earlier\n   application.\n\n   Fundamental architectural principle: Computation is not authority to\n   act.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-19T08:39:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161655, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-agent-execution-finality", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 1074, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-agent-execution-finality/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170449/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-16T08:39:38Z", "title": "Protected Execution-Finality Architecture for AI and Autonomous Systems: Act-Bound Capabilities and Effectuation-Boundary Verification", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Agent Route Origin Authorization\n   (AgentROA) framework, a cryptographic policy enforcement model for\n   governing the actions of autonomous AI agents.  AgentROA introduces\n   three core protocol objects: the Agent Route Origin Authorization\n   (ROA) envelope, the Agent Route Attestation (ARA) per-hop receipt,\n   and the Agent Execution Receipt (AER).  Together these objects\n   enable: (1) cryptographic binding of an agent's authorized action\n   scope to a signed policy envelope at session initialization,\n   (2) per-hop attestation across multi-agent delegation chains with\n   monotonic scope-narrowing semantics (no policy envelope may be\n   expanded by a downstream delegation), and (3) cryptographic receipts\n   produced intrinsically by the enforcement decision at each capability\n   invocation boundary.  The framework is modeled on the BGP Route\n   Origin Authorization (ROA) concept from RPKI (RFC 6480) applied to\n   the AI agent execution domain.\n\n   The Border Gateway enforcement model positions a cryptographic\n   enforcement process at a capability invocation boundary — external\n   to the agent's execution context — reducing the risk that governance\n   decisions are influenced by the governed agent by placing\n   enforcement in a separate process boundary.  The Border Gateway\n   model is topology-independent: it may be deployed as a protocol-\n   specific proxy in front of Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers,\n   as a service mesh enforcement component covering all inter-service\n   calls, as a network egress gateway covering all outbound capability\n   invocations regardless of protocol, or as a domain-specific\n   execution boundary.  The protocol objects defined herein function\n   identically across all deployment topologies.  This document\n   establishes the architectural model, protocol object schemas, and\n   enforcement semantics for the AgentROA framework.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-18T03:27:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156012, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nivalto-agentroa-route-authorization", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 38, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nivalto-agentroa-route-authorization/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162073/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162223/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-17T07:09:04Z", "title": "Agent Route Origin Authorization (AgentROA): A Cryptographic Policy Enforcement Framework for AI Agent Actions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a method for evaluating the \"agent-readiness\"\n   of a website: a measure of how well the site supports autonomous AI\n   agents and generative-search crawlers that consume it without a human\n   in the loop.  The method is grounded entirely in published standards.\n   It organizes observable server behavior into four evaluation pillars,\n   each mapped to specific IETF RFCs and W3C specifications covering\n   agent discoverability, semantic entity grounding, content efficiency\n   and ingestion, and transport.  From the per-pillar observations it\n   derives a single deterministic weighted score between 0 and 100.  The\n   score formula and its pillar weights are a reproducible scoring\n   convention defined by this document; they are a measurement\n   convention and are not themselves a standard.  The method is\n   diagnostic: it observes and reports conformance to existing\n   specifications and changes no protocol.  It is a measurement method,\n   not a new protocol and not an enforcement mechanism.  Community and\n   vendor conventions that are not standards are observed only as\n   supplementary signals and are clearly identified as such.  A running\n   reference implementation that performs these checks exists and is\n   referenced as running code.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-20T08:12:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161677, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-shah-agent-readiness-evaluation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-shah-agent-readiness-evaluation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170497/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-17T08:12:10Z", "title": "A Standards-Grounded Method for Evaluating Website Agent-Readiness", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   A verifier result reports what a check returned.  It does not report\n   whether the check could have returned anything else.  A check that\n   inspects too narrow a set of fields, and a check whose rejecting or\n   indeterminate input has never occurred, both report the same\n   accepting value as a check that distinguishes, and a conformance\n   matrix does not tell them apart.\n\n   This document defines requirements for reporting whether a check has\n   been exercised into its declared rejecting and indeterminate\n   outcomes, and requirements for results that claim completeness: that\n   the records relied on were the whole of some stated set.  Those are\n   two separate obligations.  A result must name the set it claims to\n   have seen, and must not treat a party with an interest in the answer\n   as the only thing looking.\n\n   This document defines no wire format, encoding, token, claim\n   vocabulary, test harness, or registry.  It does require a record to\n   identify certain facts.  That constrains what a record must be able\n   to express and leaves its encoding open.  A profile satisfying these\n   requirements will need a place to put those facts.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T23:16:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161682, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-he-agentproto-verifier-evidence", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 28, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-he-agentproto-verifier-evidence/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170516/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170563/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171341/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171565/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T23:16:42Z", "title": "Exercise and Completeness Evidence for Agent Protocol Verifier Results", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents increasingly decide and act on behalf of organizations and\n   delegate work to other agents.  Identity, authentication,\n   authorization and runtime control mechanisms establish who is acting\n   and what is permitted, but they do not by themselves preserve the\n   organizational meaning of a delegation: the bounded authority,\n   purpose, material context, and the distinction between a\n   recommendation, a decision, an action and its outcome.  This document\n   states requirements, derived from the Enterprise Agent Governance\n   Framework (EAGF), that such semantics be preserved across tool calls,\n   delegations and agent-to-agent handoffs.  It defines no new protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T02:32:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161695, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-luna-eagf-agent-delegation-semantics", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-luna-eagf-agent-delegation-semantics/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171573/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170565/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T02:32:55Z", "title": "Semantic Governance Requirements for AI Agent Delegation and Handoff", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The WIMSE AI Identity Management System (AIMS) requires tamper-\n   evident agent audit logs but does not specify their format or\n   discovery.  This document maps the AIMS minimum fields to the SCITT\n   Agent Execution Evidence profile, defines one claim that binds a\n   workload credential to the persistent agent, governing policy,\n   expected Evidence Service and optional Assurance Statement, and\n   defines an in-toto predicate type for carrying Execution Receipts\n   alongside the in-toto agent audit record format.  It also describes\n   how a service admits an agent's call by checking its assurance at the\n   moment of the request, and defines an HTTP field that links each call\n   to the caller's evidence.  It is a companion to the AIMS work and\n   makes no change to it.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T07:05:32Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161697, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-thaha-wimse-agent-evidence-binding", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-thaha-wimse-agent-evidence-binding/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170573/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171237/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171606/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171752/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T07:05:32Z", "title": "Evidence Binding for AI Agent Credentials", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   In the scenario of AI agent authorization, how to ensure that a\n   user's high-level, natural-language intent is correctly and safely\n   converted into low-level, specific authorization grants.  A\n   misunderstanding or malicious interpretation during this translation\n   can lead to significant security vulnerabilities.  This document\n   introduces the \"Intent Token,\" a new type of security token designed\n   to bridge this semantic gap.  The Intent Token is a signed credential\n   that immutably captures the user's original intent.  It decouples the\n   act of granting an intent from the act of issuing specific access\n   tokens.  A client presents this Intent Token to an Authorization\n   Server, which acts as a trusted intermediary.  The Authorization\n   Server's responsibility is to interpret the user's stated intent and,\n   based on its policies, issue narrowly-scoped, short-lived access\n   tokens for specific resources, thereby ensuring the principle of\n   least privilege is upheld throughout the process.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-22T20:05:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161716, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-oauth-agent-intent-token", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-oauth-agent-intent-token/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170597/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-19T20:05:44Z", "title": "The Intent Token - A Credential for Delegated User Intent", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent protocols often require a participant to establish a decision-\n   time input before it acts: issuer standing, key availability,\n   delegated authority, policy profile availability, consumption state,\n   revocation status, or the outcome of a downstream operation.  A\n   participant that establishes an input and receives a negative answer\n   has learned a different fact from a participant that cannot establish\n   the input at all.  Collapsing those facts into one denial or failure\n   value changes retry behavior, alert routing, audit interpretation,\n   incident ownership, and post-incident reconstruction.\n\n   This document specifies requirements for preserving evaluation state\n   across agent protocol boundaries: the value of an input, whether that\n   value was established at decision time, the freshness of the source\n   used to establish it, and whether the resulting policy decision was\n   actually evaluated.  The requirements are stated independently of\n   encoding, so that policy, authorization, revocation, routing, and\n   audit documents can satisfy them in their own formats.  Test cases\n   for the requirements are included, with a machine-readable form\n   published separately.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-23T02:53:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161386, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-konda-agentproto-evaluation-state", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-konda-agentproto-evaluation-state/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169850/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170099/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170604/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-20T02:53:59Z", "title": "Preserving Evaluation State in Agent Protocol Decisions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents increasingly call agents and services operated by\n   other organizations.  A receiving organization often wants to know\n   two things about any such caller: that it is the agent it claims to\n   be, and that the organization operating it still authorizes it.\n   Existing approaches to the second question require a trust\n   relationship between the two organizations' authorization servers.\n\n   This document defines a DNS record by which the owner of a domain\n   links an agent's DNS name to the OAuth client that the agent uses at\n   the owner's own authorization server.  The sending agent presents a\n   fresh access token for that client; the receiver verifies the token\n   against the record and against the issuer's published keys.  No trust\n   relationship, registration or federation between the two\n   organizations is needed, and disabling the client at the sending\n   organization's authorization server stops the agent being accepted by\n   every receiver.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T10:17:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161730, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-shankarlingam-oauth-agent-dns-link", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-shankarlingam-oauth-agent-dns-link/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170636/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171123/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T10:17:08Z", "title": "Linking an Agent's DNS Name to its OAuth Client", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic systems can carry out tasks using agents, model services,\n   data services, tools, and computing environments distributed across\n   sites and administrative domains.  Dependencies among these\n   components create communication and computing requirements that may\n   evolve during execution.  Whether a proposed collaboration can\n   satisfy those requirements depends on network connectivity and\n   performance, computing capacity and readiness, data and model\n   locality, and the policies governing resource use and data movement.\n\n   This document describes a framework for cross-domain agentic\n   collaboration that accounts for task dependencies and the network and\n   computing conditions affecting communication and execution.  It\n   outlines the roles and interactions of agents and their runtimes,\n   infrastructure information functions, and resource-management\n   functions.  The framework relates task dependencies to communication\n   and computing requirements, with consideration of execution\n   locations, data movement, and constraints spanning dependent\n   activities.  It discusses the use of appropriately scoped domain\n   information to assess alternative combinations of agents, services,\n   and tools, together with the abstraction and updating of that\n   information under partial visibility and independent domain policies.\n   The framework also addresses adaptation to evolving dependencies and\n   infrastructure conditions, including information overhead and the\n   stability of decisions in large agent populations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-24T09:32:38Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161739, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhao-rtgwg-agentic-systems", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhao-rtgwg-agentic-systems/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170682/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-21T09:32:38Z", "title": "A Framework for Network- and Compute-Aware Cross-Domain Agentic Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes an experimental, signed JSON statement and\n   verification model for observing computational-agent continuity\n   across sessions, instances and delegations.  It distinguishes model,\n   runtime, agent, grant and execution identifiers, retains predecessor\n   evidence, and produces three-valued verification outcomes.  Integrity\n   of a record never implies permission to perform an action.  This is\n   not an OAuth token format or a claim of standardized identity\n   ontology.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:38:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161750, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-agent-continuity-evidence", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-agent-continuity-evidence/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171483/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170664/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:38:26Z", "title": "Continuity Evidence Records for Delegated Agent Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines terminology for the security evaluation of AI\n   agents.  It establishes a shared vocabulary for four first-level\n   evaluation dimensions -- Foundation Model Security, Interaction\n   Security, Operational Security, and Basic Security -- for the 55\n   second-level evaluation metrics organized under those dimensions, for\n   the common metric evaluation convention that applies to all of them,\n   and for the three agent security risk levels.  This document defines\n   terminology only.  It does not prescribe evaluation procedures; the\n   procedures are specified in\n   [I-D.han-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-methodology], and the System\n   Under Test boundaries and evaluation scenarios are specified in\n   [I-D.han-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-profiles].\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-26T07:21:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161805, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hcf-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-terminology", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 51, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hcf-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-terminology/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170796/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-23T07:21:49Z", "title": "Benchmarking Terminology for AI Agent Security Evaluation", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines evaluation profiles for the security assessment\n   of AI agents.  A profile fixes the System Under Test boundary and the\n   scenario against which the metrics defined in\n   [I-D.han-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-terminology] are applied, so\n   that results obtained under different boundaries or scenarios are not\n   compared without qualification.  The document defines four\n   infrastructure profiles, which correspond to the four component\n   groups of an agent and to the four evaluation dimensions; six\n   scenario profiles, which correspond to the agent types in scope; the\n   input and output boundary specification that applies to all profiles;\n   profile composition rules; and the minimum evaluation logging fields\n   required by each profile.  The metric definitions are the subject of\n   [I-D.han-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-terminology], and the methods\n   by which the metrics are exercised are the subject of\n   [I-D.han-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-methodology].\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-26T07:45:48Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161806, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hcf-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-profiles", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 46, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hcf-bpm-agent-security-benchmark-profiles/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170800/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-23T07:45:48Z", "title": "Evaluation Profiles for AI Agent Security Assessment", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This informational document describes future-oriented distributed\n   authority and execution-finality profiles for cooperating autonomous\n   agents, delegated operations, external tools, cloud gateways, native\n   resources, and cross-domain workflows.  It examines identity, non-\n   amplifying delegation, provenance and influence lineage, transitive\n   side effects, cross-agent collision management, policy convergence\n   and downstream effectuation-boundary enforcement.  It distinguishes a\n   proposed objective or authorized plan from authority for an actual\n   resource effect.  Source-derived procedures, tests and algorithms are\n   presented as implementation examples, not as evidence that identified\n   vendors implement this proposed architecture.\n\n   Industry Relevance: Recent public developments in autonomous AI-agent\n   security and execution governance include Microsoft Execution\n   Containers (MXC), Microsoft Agent Governance Toolkit ADR-0030\n   (Action-Bound, Fail-Closed Approval Protocol), and Project\n   Perception; Google Agent Gateway and Gemini Agent; Meta Muse/Sentinel\n   AI-Agent Security Architecture; NVIDIA OpenShell Reference\n   Architecture and NVIDIA Sentry; OpenAI Agents API, Codex, Agents SDK,\n   and Responses API; and Anthropic Claude Managed Agents, Claude Code\n   Sandboxing, and Claude Code Auto Mode.  These initiatives illustrate\n   industry attention to agent isolation, independent policy\n   enforcement, action-bound authorization, delegated authority,\n   credential isolation, network-egress security, hardware-assisted\n   containment, runtime supervision, and fail-closed controls.  This\n   draft describes related proposed execution-finality mechanisms\n   without asserting that these initiatives implement them.\n\n   Relevance to the IETF: the mechanisms described here require common,\n   interoperable objects and verification semantics across independently\n   built components, including operation descriptors, act-bound non-\n   bearer capabilities, finality-sink verification results, validation\n   evidence, and authority, revocation, and containment generations.\n   The document relates to existing IETF work on OAuth, GNAP, RATS,\n   WIMSE, and SCITT, and asks the community whether and where this\n   effectuation-boundary problem should be addressed.\n\n   This document is intentionally long and is not repetitive: each\n   profile specifies a distinct mechanism with its own algorithms,\n   failure cases, and enforcement points, and the security properties\n   depend on how these mechanisms combine.  A reduced excerpt would hide\n   the conditions under which they fail closed, so this document and its\n   two companion drafts, among which the profiles are divided, are meant\n   to be evaluated as a whole.\n\n   This draft builds on earlier Internet-Drafts by the same author, in\n   particular draft-das-agent-execution-finality, and adds mechanism\n   families not described there; the Introduction states precisely what\n   was disclosed earlier and what is disclosed here.\n\n   IPR: The technical content of this draft set is based upon the\n   author's international application PCT/IB2026/055615, which forms the\n   foundation of this disclosure and comprises approximately 300\n   distinct, non-repetitive embodiments and enforcement points.  A\n   separate patent application specific to the material in this draft\n   set has also been filed.  Related Indian applications: 202531130665,\n   202631000572, 202631001586, 202631002990, 202631124028, and\n   202631125689.  Related international applications: PCT/IB2025/058316,\n   PCT/IB2026/053385, PCT/IB2026/055615, PCT/IB2026/055760, PCT/\n   IB2026/055870, PCT/IB2026/056058, PCT/IB2026/056809, and PCT/\n   IB2026/056941.  These identify related technical disclosures; not all\n   described mechanisms are necessarily patent-claimed or granted, and\n   patent scope and priority depend on applicable law and examination.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-26T08:29:16Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161809, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-distributed-agent-authority-finality", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 1472, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-distributed-agent-authority-finality/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170803/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T08:37:02Z", "title": "Distributed Authority and Execution-Finality for Multi-Agent Systems, Delegated Operations, and Cross-Domain Gateways", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines how a relying party in one administrative\n   domain validates the mandate under which a software agent, including\n   an AI agent, acts for a principal in another domain.  Its distinctive\n   element is evidence of the delegator's authority: a statement from an\n   authority source about which permissions the person or entity\n   granting the mandate may delegate to agents, kept separate from the\n   statement that the mandate was granted.  The document specifies the\n   mandate format with precisely defined limit semantics, a\n   deterministic validation procedure driven by an explicit validation\n   profile and kept separate from the relying party's authorization\n   decision, the attribution of every validated claim to the issuer and\n   role that asserted it, rules for enforcing the mandate after token\n   exchange, and the boundary between this protocol and a trust\n   framework.  It reuses JSON Web Tokens, OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange, Rich\n   Authorization Requests, WIMSE workload credentials and HTTP Message\n   Signatures, Token Status Lists, and Entity Attestation Tokens.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T08:41:35Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161834, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hulea-wimse-agent-mandate", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 57, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hulea-wimse-agent-mandate/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170847/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171599/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T08:41:35Z", "title": "Verifiable Mandates for Agents Acting Across Administrative Domains", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The context distribution is important in a multi-agent system, which\n   will impact the execution latency, token consumption, and task\n   completion success rate, especially in a complex and multi-round\n   workflow.\n\n   This document specifies the scenarios and procedures of agent context\n   distribution as well as the corresponding optimization during the\n   procedures in order to provide precise control of the context\n   distribution among the multiple agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T01:33:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161858, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chang-agentproto-context-interaction", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chang-agentproto-context-interaction/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170900/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-26T01:33:13Z", "title": "Agent Context Interaction Optimizations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Each AI agent can hold a valid permission and the group can still do\n   something nobody authorized.  When Apple Siri hands a task to an app\n   through App Intents, Google Gemini delegates to Android apps and\n   cloud agents, or an orchestrator dispatches sub-agents across iOS,\n   iPadOS, macOS, Android, Windows, Linux, cloud APIs, and connected\n   devices, authority is copied, narrowed, widened, and combined along\n   the way.  Prompt-injection-driven delegation, confused-deputy\n   escalation, replayed agent approvals, conflicting writes to the same\n   resource, duplicated payments, stale reservations, overspent shared\n   budgets, colluding or non-independent evaluator quorums, and a final\n   tool call detached from its originating authority are typical\n   results.\n\n   This document, Part 5 of a twelve-part series, specifies multi-agent\n   control for execution finality.  It describes protected arbitration\n   of conflicting effects across agents using effect footprints and\n   atomic reservations; cryptographic delegation lineage across\n   providers with transitive scope attenuation, so that a sub-agent can\n   never receive more authority than its parent; version-bound\n   concurrency checks; authenticated quorum membership with operation-\n   bound approval certificates and separation of duties; budgets\n   conserved across branches of a delegation tree; revocation that\n   reaches every descendant; and independent Finality Sink verification\n   of the actual resource effect.  Reference algorithms, ten threat\n   models, and twenty-four reproducible positive and negative tests,\n   including an iOS and Android cooperating-receiver case, are included.\n   Part 1 of the series defines the shared terminology and common\n   workflows.\n\n   The document relates these mechanisms to OAuth 2.0 Rich Authorization\n   Requests, Resource Indicators, Token Exchange, Demonstrating Proof of\n   Possession (DPoP), and current security best practice; the Grant\n   Negotiation and Authorization Protocol (GNAP); Workload Identity in\n   Multi-System Environments (WIMSE) and its agent-delegation\n   discussions; Remote ATtestation procedureS (RATS); CBOR Object\n   Signing and Encryption (COSE); Supply Chain Integrity, Transparency,\n   and Trust (SCITT); Authentication and Authorization for Constrained\n   Environments (ACE); and HTTP conditional requests.  These are treated\n   as qualified inputs rather than final authority, without claiming\n   working-group adoption or IETF consensus.\n\n   Industry alignment covers Apple iOS, iPadOS, and macOS with Siri,\n   Shortcuts, and App Intents; Google Android with Gemini, Binder, and\n   intents; Samsung Bixby and Microsoft Copilot; Microsoft Windows and\n   Azure agent orchestration; agent platforms from OpenAI, Anthropic,\n   Google Cloud, and Meta; NVIDIA, Qualcomm, Arm, and Huawei hardware;\n   banking and payments; enterprise SaaS, procurement, and inventory;\n   robotics; and telecommunications.  Product names are illustrative and\n   imply no endorsement or implementation by any vendor.  For the EU\n   Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689), traceable\n   delegation lineage, bounded authority, and quorum-based approval can\n   help providers and deployers address human-oversight, record-keeping,\n   risk-management, and robustness and cybersecurity requirements where\n   those obligations apply; this is a technical mapping, not a\n   conformity claim or legal advice.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T12:15:39Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161883, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-multi-agent-authority-control", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 530, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-multi-agent-authority-control/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170939/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T05:46:19Z", "title": "Secure AI Execution-Finality for iOS, Android, and Cross-Platform AI Agents, Part 5: Multi-Agent Delegation, Conflict Arbitration, and Quorum Authority", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   An AI agent can call the right tool with valid credentials and still\n   act on the wrong thing.  When a user tells Apple Siri or Google\n   Gemini to \"send this to her\", asks a coding agent to deploy, or lets\n   a browser agent complete a form, the agent must resolve which file,\n   contact, account, tenant, or device is meant, and which tool, tool\n   version, and model will act.  On iOS, iPadOS, macOS, Android,\n   Windows, Linux, enterprise SaaS, cloud platforms, and connected\n   hardware, agents can select the wrong user-referenced object, follow\n   instructions injected into web pages, repositories, or tool outputs,\n   call a tool whose description or capabilities silently changed, run\n   on a swapped model or configuration, or send authorized-looking\n   requests to redirected hosts, rebound aliases, and substituted\n   tenants.  Identity authentication, sandboxing, and transport\n   encryption can all succeed while files, credentials, payments,\n   developer infrastructure, and physical devices are exposed.\n\n   This document, Part 7 of a twelve-part series, specifies execution-\n   time integrity for AI tools.  It describes protected correspondence\n   between what the user saw and referred to and the object actually\n   acted on; canonical tool-effect contracts with semantic-compatibility\n   checks across tool versions; algorithm, model, and configuration\n   baseline continuity; instruction provenance with evaluation of lower-\n   trust influence on downstream actions; authenticated resolution of\n   resource aliases, hosts, and tenants, including redirects and DNS\n   rebinding; fencing of tool installation, activation, runtime upgrade,\n   and rollback; detection of dynamic service and capability\n   substitution; and reconstruction of the actual operation at an\n   independent Finality Sink.  Reference algorithms, twelve threat\n   models, and thirty reproducible tests, including iOS App Intents and\n   Android cooperating-app cases, are included.  Part 1 of the series\n   defines the shared terminology and common workflows.\n\n   The document relates these mechanisms to OAuth 2.0 authorization,\n   Rich Authorization Requests, Resource Indicators, Token Exchange, and\n   Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP); Workload Identity in Multi-\n   System Environments (WIMSE); Remote ATtestation procedureS (RATS);\n   CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE); Supply Chain Integrity,\n   Transparency, and Trust (SCITT); Software Updates for Internet of\n   Things (SUIT) manifests; Trusted Execution Environment Provisioning\n   (TEEP); Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments\n   (ACE); and HTTP redirect and conditional-request semantics.  None of\n   these alone is treated as conferring final authority for a\n   consequential effect, and no working-group adoption or IETF consensus\n   is claimed.\n\n   Industry alignment covers Apple iOS, iPadOS, and macOS with Siri,\n   Shortcuts, App Intents, and Keychain; Google Android with Gemini,\n   intents, Binder, and Keystore; Samsung Bixby, Microsoft Copilot, and\n   Amazon Alexa; Microsoft Windows and Azure; coding and computer-use\n   agents from OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Meta; Model Context\n   Protocol servers, agent-to-agent protocols, and tool and plugin\n   registries; browser agents; NVIDIA, Qualcomm, Arm, and Huawei\n   hardware; banking, payments, and agentic commerce; email and\n   messaging agents; healthcare record agents; document signing; cloud\n   infrastructure-as-code; model hosting and model routing; smart homes\n   using Matter; automotive assistants and over-the-air updates;\n   government digital identity, including the EU Digital Identity\n   Wallet; telecommunications; industrial IoT and robotics; and software\n   supply chains and CI/CD.  For each, the document states the technical\n   effect: the consequential operation reaches the Finality Sink only if\n   the actual target, tool contract, model baseline, and instruction\n   lineage match what was authorized.  Product names are illustrative\n   and imply no endorsement or implementation by any vendor.  For the EU\n   Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689), execution-\n   time integrity of tools, models, and targets can help providers and\n   deployers address accuracy, robustness, and cybersecurity, record-\n   keeping, and risk-management requirements where those obligations\n   apply; this is a technical mapping, not a conformity claim or legal\n   advice.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T12:45:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161885, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-agent-tool-integrity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 627, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-agent-tool-integrity/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170943/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T05:44:27Z", "title": "Secure AI Execution-Finality for iOS, Android, and Cross-Platform AI Agents, Part 7: AI Tool Integrity, Semantic Target Correspondence, and Execution-Time Resource Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Contact centers bring people, AI agents, and business systems into\n   the same voice session.  Consumer-controlled agents are also\n   beginning to call agents operated by businesses.  This document\n   describes five contact center use cases for the agentic dialog\n   management protocol: a consumer agent calling a business agent,\n   escalation from an AI agent to a human representative, a call that\n   resumes on another channel, a voice dialog passing through speech\n   recognition and synthesis, and a dialog crossing an existing SIP or\n   PSTN network.  It derives numbered requirements that can be\n   incorporated into the working group's use cases and requirements\n   document.  It also explains how a dialog identifier relates to the\n   conversation records that contact centers already keep.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T13:03:22Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161888, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-howe-agentproto-contact-center-usecases", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-howe-agentproto-contact-center-usecases/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170946/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-26T13:03:22Z", "title": "Contact Center Use Cases and Requirements for Agentic Dialogs", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Giving an AI agent a credential usually means giving it everything\n   that credential can do.  When Apple Siri, Google Gemini, enterprise\n   copilots, coding agents, or third-party AI tools act with a user's\n   OAuth tokens, API keys, signing keys, or logged-in sessions on iOS,\n   iPadOS, macOS, Android, cloud APIs, developer tools, financial\n   services, and telecom systems, a prompt-injected or confused agent\n   can export data, call APIs, sign transactions, write poisoned entries\n   into its own long-term memory, or leak information piece by piece to\n   different recipients. iOS Keychain and the Secure Enclave, Android\n   Keystore, sandboxing, token authentication, proof of possession, and\n   a successful approval each protect something real, but none of them\n   establishes authority for the exact outgoing data, recipient, or\n   resource effect.\n\n   This document, Part 9 of a twelve-part series, specifies how\n   credentials and data are used only for the specific operation that\n   was authorized.  It describes protected secret custody in which the\n   agent never receives the raw secret; operation-bound credential\n   activation; continuity of credential generation and source authority\n   across key and token rotation; independent Finality Sink inspection\n   of the real outgoing request; recipient-scoped, cumulative,\n   correlation-aware disclosure accounting, including derived summaries;\n   provenance of source-derived information; retrieval-time provenance\n   and write authority for agent memory and retrieval-augmented\n   generation stores; and non-replayable consumption of finality.  It\n   does not assume that a third-party mobile application can mediate\n   other applications without their cooperation.  Reference algorithms,\n   fourteen threat models, and thirty-two reproducible tests, including\n   current Android and iOS authorized-call cases, are included.  Part 1\n   of the series defines the shared terminology and common workflows.\n\n   The document relates these mechanisms to OAuth 2.0 Rich Authorization\n   Requests, Resource Indicators, Token Exchange, Demonstrating Proof of\n   Possession (DPoP), and mutual-TLS sender constraints; the Workload\n   Identity in Multi-System Environments (WIMSE) architecture and the\n   WIMSE working group's AI Identity Management System draft on agent\n   authentication and authorization; Authentication and Authorization\n   for Constrained Environments (ACE); CBOR Object Signing and\n   Encryption (COSE); Remote ATtestation procedureS (RATS); Supply Chain\n   Integrity, Transparency, and Trust (SCITT); and HTTP semantics.\n   These are treated as qualified inputs rather than final authority,\n   without claiming working-group adoption or IETF consensus.\n\n   Industry alignment covers Apple iOS, iPadOS, and macOS with Siri,\n   Keychain, Secure Enclave, and App Intents; Google Android with\n   Gemini, Keystore, app permissions, and inter-process communication;\n   Samsung Bixby, Microsoft Copilot, and Amazon Alexa; Microsoft Windows\n   and Azure; Amazon Web Services and other cloud identity platforms;\n   cloud key-management and hardware security module signing services;\n   agent platforms from OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Meta; Model\n   Context Protocol connectors and their OAuth-based authorization;\n   passkeys and WebAuthn; password managers and autofill; enterprise\n   SaaS connectors; open banking and consent-based data sharing,\n   including India's Account Aggregator framework; digital wallets and\n   payment APIs; healthcare data; human-resources and recruiting agents;\n   source-code secrets and CI/CD; personal assistant memory; advertising\n   and analytics data sharing; the EU Digital Identity Wallet; NVIDIA,\n   Qualcomm, Arm, and Huawei hardware; telecommunications messaging; RAG\n   and vector databases; and public-sector systems.  For each, the\n   document states the technical effect: a secret is exercised, or data\n   is released, only for the specific authorized operation and\n   recipient, and only within the remaining disclosure budget.  Product\n   names are illustrative and imply no endorsement or implementation by\n   any vendor.  For the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation (EU)\n   2024/1689), and alongside data-protection-by-design obligations under\n   the General Data Protection Regulation, operation-bound credential\n   use and recipient-scoped disclosure records can help providers and\n   deployers address cybersecurity, record-keeping, and risk-management\n   requirements where those obligations apply; this is a technical\n   mapping, not a conformity claim or legal advice.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T13:08:20Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161889, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-das-agent-credential-privacy", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 593, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-das-agent-credential-privacy/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170947/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T05:44:10Z", "title": "Secure AI Execution-Finality for iOS, Android, and Cross-Platform AI Agents, Part 9: Credential Custody, Operation-Bound Secret Use, and Protected Data Disclosure", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   BRICK proposes a vendor-neutral JSON construction contract for AI\r\n   agents.  It defines agent identity, goals, capabilities, instruction\r\n   and skill references, explicitly namespaced MCP tool dependencies,\r\n   and an optional projection to A2A Agent Cards.  It references the\r\n   existing MCP, A2A, and Agent Skills specifications rather than\r\n   redefining their protocols or formats.  BRICK does not prescribe a\r\n   language model, reasoning engine, deployment platform, discovery\r\n   service, or authorization protocol.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T15:01:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161896, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharaf-brick-agent-definition", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharaf-brick-agent-definition/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170908/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-26T15:01:03Z", "title": "BRICK: Portable Definition and Composition of AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous software agents, and increasingly physical robots, now act\n   at machine scale and speed across Internet protocols and throughout\n   society, in roles whose decisions and actions can have legal,\n   economic, safety, security, and physical consequences, often with\n   limited or no human supervision.\n\n   Any credential, authorization, delegation, certification, reputation,\n   audit history, insurance policy, regulatory obligation, safety\n   decision, liability, or legal process concerning an autonomous entity\n   depends on being able to identify that same entity reliably over\n   time.\n\n   Conversely, when an autonomous entity or its operator is malicious,\n   compromised, or beyond effective human control, the absence of\n   durable identity lets it shed history, evade consequences, or\n   multiply cheaply into Sybil swarms.\n\n   Many proposed mechanisms bind claims to identifiers, accounts,\n   credentials, keys, or names that can be replaced, reassigned,\n   transferred, revoked, suspended, or lost.  They may authenticate that\n   subject perfectly while still failing to establish a durable identity\n   for the entity it represents.\n\n   This document offers precise terminology that distinguishes identity\n   from the identifiers, attributes, credentials, authorizations, and\n   reputation with which it is commonly conflated, minting the term\n   \"identity0\" so that the strict sense has an unambiguous name.  It\n   presents a layered reference model showing where current \"agent\n   identity\" efforts fit and the durable identity layer they commonly\n   presuppose but do not provide.\n\n   This document is informational and defines no protocol.  It serves as\n   the framing and vocabulary document for a companion series that\n   specifies a federated durable-identity architecture, provisioning,\n   resolution, application, and governance intended to provide a\n   practical path from this problem statement to interoperable\n   deployment.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T02:41:22Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161254, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-drake-agent-identity-problem-statement", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-drake-agent-identity-problem-statement/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169478/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171016/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T02:41:22Z", "title": "Identity for Autonomous Agents and Robots: Problem Statement, Threat Model, and Terminology", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Agent Identity Registry System (AIRS) provides durable identity\n   infrastructure for autonomous entities such as AI agents and robots,\n   with graduated assurance ranging from scarcity-backed physical\n   anchors through protected-key and software-only participation.  Its\n   companion specifications deliberately do not define or empower a\n   governance authority; they describe the functions such an authority\n   must perform and defer its constitution to a separate effort.\n\n   This document defines that body: the Agent Identity Authority (AIA).\n   It specifies the Authority's name, legal form, mission, and\n   relationship to the protocol specifications; its membership\n   categories and Board composition; binding geographic-diversity rules\n   and non-binding advisory recommendations for ideal composition; the\n   accreditation, dispute-resolution, hardware trust store,\n   transparency, and funding frameworks it operates; and the bootstrap\n   process by which the Authority forms and assumes stewardship of the\n   global production namespace.\n\n   The Authority governs infrastructure, not behavior: it stewards the\n   aid namespace, hardware roots of trust, accreditation, and production\n   Registry Operator succession.  It does not regulate what agents do.\n   Its legitimacy derives from being the least-objectionable steward of\n   a shared resource, in the tradition of ICANN, the regional Internet\n   registries, and the W3C, and its charter is designed so that no\n   single nation, region, or company can capture it or holds a formal\n   veto over it, although supermajority rules let a large enough\n   coordinated bloc block consequential decisions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T02:42:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161255, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-drake-agent-identity-governance", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 43, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-drake-agent-identity-governance/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169479/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171018/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T02:42:53Z", "title": "The Agent Identity Authority: A Multi-Stakeholder Governance Framework for the Agent Identity Registry System", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a record format for AI agent authorization\n   decisions and the effects that followed them.  The format is one in-\n   toto predicate type, signed inside a DSSE envelope.  A record carries\n   the decision and the observed effect with a derived agreement between\n   them, whether the decision point could evaluate the request and how\n   fresh each input it relied on was, and a canonicalization contract\n   under which a party that trusts neither the agent nor its operator\n   recomputes the record's consistency from its bytes.  The document\n   also defines what a party that retains a record keeps beside it, so\n   that the record can later be dated from anchors that party does not\n   control.  The format carries the seven minimum audit fields of the\n   WIMSE AI Identity Management System framework, and it is offered as a\n   data model for the audit records the proposed AUDIT working group\n   would specify.  It defines no policy.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-02T22:20:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161671, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gilda-audit-agent-audit-record", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 71, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gilda-audit-agent-audit-record/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170487/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171029/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171062/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171266/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-29T22:20:04Z", "title": "An Audit Record Format for AI Agent Authorization Decisions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes an Agent Authorization Center (AAC) for user-\n   side intelligent agents, enabling them to access resource parties on\n   behalf of users through either autonomous approval or human\n   authorization modes.  The AAC maintains user-agent associations and\n   personalized authorization policies, balancing agent autonomy with\n   user control.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T09:19:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161929, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-du-oauth-agent-authorization-center", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-du-oauth-agent-authorization-center/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171038/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T09:19:49Z", "title": "Agent Authorization Center for User-Side Agent Authentication and Authorization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a Supply Chain Integrity, Transparency, and\n   Trust (SCITT) payload profile for agent session provenance: signed\n   records that bind an autonomous agent session's lifecycle (start,\n   governed tool dispatch, state transitions, termination, and\n   settlement) to offline-verifiable cryptographic evidence, with an\n   explicit head commitment that detects local hash-chain truncation and\n   whole-store rollback.  It reuses the SCITT architecture and COSE\n   receipt envelope: the Causal Provenance Receipt (CPR) bundle is the\n   signed statement payload, and registration with a Transparency\n   Service is optional and explicitly disclosed.  This profile addresses\n   the session-lifecycle and resumption-evidence grain rather than\n   isolated per-action telemetry, while preserving bounded verifier\n   semantics: a verifier checks cryptographic signatures, canonical\n   digests, chain links, and external anchors; it does not prove that\n   unallegeable physical events occurred.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T22:42:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161947, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-bailey-scitt-agent-session-receipt", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-bailey-scitt-agent-session-receipt/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170996/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T22:42:25Z", "title": "Causal Provenance Receipts for Agent Sessions: A SCITT Profile", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   A delegation record retained as evidence must remain verifiable long\n   after the key that signed it is retired.  A signature of any\n   algorithm establishes who signed, not when, so such a record needs\n   anchoring in trusted time, renewed before the algorithms protecting\n   it weaken.  That is long-settled practice for archived signatures.\n   It is rarely required for agent delegation, and where it is, the\n   anchor is not itself required to be post-quantum.  An anchor is only\n   as good as the protection behind it, and an RFC 3161 timestamp is\n   itself a signature: an adversary holding a cryptanalytically relevant\n   quantum computer can mint one bearing any date it chooses.  Because\n   such a machine may be built without announcement, anchoring must\n   become post-quantum anchoring by a stated transition date, and be\n   applied early, so that the only assumption left about when such a\n   machine appeared is that none existed before a record was first\n   anchored that way.  The earlier that date, the weaker the assumption,\n   which makes choosing it a security decision and not only a scheduling\n   one.\n\n   This document states requirements that software and AI agent identity\n   mechanisms should satisfy in order to remain sound across the\n   transition, and identifies four that no such mechanism yet imposes on\n   delegation credentials in general.  The algorithms binding each hop\n   of a delegation chain to its trust anchor must be visible to a\n   verifier and retained with any record kept as evidence; where a\n   signing key is fetched from a key set over TLS, as OAuth deployments\n   commonly do, that binding is rarely recorded and no mechanism for\n   agent delegation requires it to be, so in practice it is absent.\n   Trust anchors must migrate before the credentials issued under them.\n   Every retained record must be anchored, that anchoring must become\n   post-quantum anchoring from a stated transition date and be\n   maintained by renewal, and credentials of any type kept as evidence\n   must be signed post-quantum from that same date.  Delegation chains\n   also propagate the weakest algorithm in the chain, and routinely\n   cross organizational boundaries where no common trust anchor policy\n   can be assumed.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T03:29:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161300, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-uppalapati-wimse-pq-agent-identity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 46, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-uppalapati-wimse-pq-agent-identity/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169642/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171101/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T03:29:42Z", "title": "Post-Quantum Requirements for Software and AI Agent Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents increasingly delegate tasks to other agents.  Each\n   delegation should convey only a subset of the delegating party's\n   authority, that subset should be bounded in scope, magnitude, and\n   time, and any enforcement point should be able to verify -- offline,\n   with no call to an authorization server -- that a token presented at\n   hop N carries authority no greater than the token at hop N-1, back to\n   a trusted root, and that every hop acts under the same accountable\n   principal.  OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693) models two-party\n   delegation and records prior actors in a nested \"act\" claim, but that\n   claim is informational only and cannot enforce attenuation across a\n   chain of depth two or more.  This document defines the Agent\n   Delegation Chain: a profile of OAuth 2.0 JWT access tokens (RFC 9068)\n   that carries authority as Rich Authorization Requests (RFC 9396),\n   binds the chain to one principal and names the agent acting at each\n   hop, links each delegation to its parent by a cryptographic byte-\n   commitment, and specifies a deterministic offline verification\n   algorithm that enforces monotonic attenuation, bounded depth,\n   monotonic expiry, and principal invariance.  It reuses existing JOSE,\n   proof-of-possession (RFC 9449), and status-list machinery (the OAuth\n   Status List draft) and introduces no new cryptography.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T05:32:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160759, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-asor-wimse-agent-delegation-chain", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 38, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-asor-wimse-agent-delegation-chain/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168166/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168533/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170416/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171071/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T05:32:37Z", "title": "Verifiable Attenuated Delegation for AI Agent Chains", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic applications increasingly distribute a single objective\n   across autonomous agents, tools, and services implemented by\n   different vendors, operated by different organizations, and connected\n   through different protocols.\n\n   A single activity may contain multiple pairwise dialogs, multiple\n   tasks within each dialog, tool invocations, derived resources,\n   downstream delegations, and later interactions that reinterpret or\n   reuse artifacts produced earlier in the activity.  Existing protocols\n   provide useful local identifiers for their own interactions, but\n   those identifiers do not by themselves provide a common information\n   model for correlating independently maintained dialogs, tasks, and\n   resources across protocol and administrative boundaries.\n\n   This document defines an information model consisting of Activities,\n   Dialogs, Tasks, Resources, and typed Relationships.  It distinguishes\n   Activity correlation from protocol-local Dialog context, and\n   distinguishes stable resource identity and provenance from locally\n   defined interpretations.\n\n   As such, this document develops a context-and-correlation\n   information-model architecture, not a functional reference\n   architecture or a wire protocol.  It identifies relevant objects and\n   relationships, relevant to maintaining coherent context, and examines\n   the implications for preserving and propagating their correlations.\n   It does not define the procedures by which applications execute work.\n   The model distinguishes the logical extent of an Activity, which may\n   extend beyond protocol-managed correlation; extending that coverage\n   is selective, while maintaining established managed correlations\n   entails responsibilities.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T17:50:52Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161986, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-agentproto-context-management-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 66, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-agentproto-context-management-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171155/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T17:50:52Z", "title": "Context and Correlation Management Architecture for Distributed Agentic Activities", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Discovery mechanisms for AI resources increasingly use DNS and mDNS\n   as interoperable publication and lookup substrates.  These mechanisms\n   work well when the publisher controls a usable DNS discovery anchor.\n   They do not cover every deployment.  An individual may control an\n   email or platform account without controlling the provider's DNS\n   namespace.  A small organization may use managed infrastructure\n   without operating an agent-specific DNS publication point.  A\n   workload may have a stable identity that is not itself DNS anchored.\n\n   This document describes Registry-Assisted Resolution for the narrower\n   case in which a discovering entity already knows the Subject Identity\n   it wants to resolve, but no usable DNS Discovery Anchor is available\n   or selected by local policy.  A configured registry returns the\n   minimum information needed to continue discovery, together with\n   provenance, authority evidence, freshness, and revocation\n   information.\n\n   This mechanism is complementary to DNS-based discovery.  A validated\n   direct DNS-anchored discovery path remains direct and is normally\n   preferred for a domain-controlled subject.  This document does not\n   define open-ended search, semantic matchmaking, ranking, capability\n   search, a global registry, registry discovery, directory-to-directory\n   routing, identity management, or trust evaluation.\n\n   Multiple independently operated registries may implement the same\n   resolution profile.  Registry selection is a client or deployment\n   policy decision; no universal NandaIndex or global root is assumed.\n   NandaIndex is described as one reference implementation of this\n   model.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-02T03:58:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 158811, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-raskar-agentic-web-federated-resolution", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 33, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-raskar-agentic-web-federated-resolution/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166538/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166663/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171181/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-29T03:58:28Z", "title": "Registry-Assisted Resolution for Known Subjects Without DNS Discovery Anchors", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Parties increasingly need to request verifiable evidence from a\n   counterparty they do not trust — an audit trail, an interaction\n   history, an account of actions taken — and to receive an answer they\n   can check rather than believe.  Evidence formats for the answer\n   exist; the ask has no standard shape, and today one system's silence,\n   another's error, and a third's stale cache are indistinguishable to a\n   relying party.  This document defines a transport-agnostic request/\n   response interaction for verifiable evidence: a request that names\n   its subject and a coverage anchor, and a set of possible outcomes\n   that is exactly one of three things — the evidence artifact, a signed\n   refusal carrying a machine-readable reason, or a recorded absence.\n   The interaction makes asking, granting, and refusing each\n   attributable and checkable, and requires that the same subject under\n   the same coverage yield a byte-identical artifact for every\n   requester.  The interaction is symmetric: any party to a recorded\n   exchange may ask the other, anchored on its own record of that\n   exchange.  A responder may additionally commit, in a signed\n   statement, to keep a subject answerable until a stated time, so that\n   a later absence is attributable rather than merely recorded.  The\n   document deliberately defines no evidence format, no identity or\n   principal scheme, no trust policy, no availability guarantee, and no\n   rule about who may ask.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T01:15:22Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161266, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-agent-evidence-request", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 47, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-agent-evidence-request/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169542/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171187/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171461/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T01:15:22Z", "title": "An Interaction Model for Requesting Verifiable Evidence", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document lets a business authorize a software agent to issue,\n   accept, reject, or dispute invoices, and to report payments, through\n   the Invoice Trust Protocol (ITP), within limits that the business\n   sets.  An authorization is a mandate: a Selective Disclosure JSON Web\n   Token (SD-JWT) credential issued by the business to a key held by the\n   agent and enforced by a named ITP node that records each use.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-02T10:01:20Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162002, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-martinez-itp-agent-mandates", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-martinez-itp-agent-mandates/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171203/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-29T10:01:20Z", "title": "Invoice Trust Protocol (ITP): Agent Mandates", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Live AI interactions combine ongoing observations, overlapping\n   responses, and changes to the devices through which a user\n   participates.  This document contributes three use case extensions to\n   the AgentProto use cases and requirements work: live multimodal\n   calls, AI glasses with companion devices, and embodied systems that\n   converse while performing delegated actions.\n\n   For each scenario, the document identifies the existing requirements\n   it builds on, the interoperability questions it raises, and the\n   requirement refinements motivated by its interaction flow.  The focus\n   is on independently changing observation sources, response state,\n   participation, and authority within a continuing dialog.  The\n   scenarios are independent of transport selection.  An appendix uses\n   Media over QUIC Transport (MOQT) to illustrate questions for\n   subsequent binding and application-profile work.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-02T12:02:39Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162004, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-agentproto-live-agent-use-case", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-agentproto-live-agent-use-case/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171214/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-29T12:02:39Z", "title": "Live Agent Use Case Extensions for Agentic Dialogs", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents act through calls to models, tools and other agents.\n   Identity work establishes who an agent is and what it may do, but\n   leaves execution-evidence formats and assurance criteria out of\n   scope.  This document profiles the Supply Chain Integrity,\n   Transparency, and Trust (SCITT) architecture for Signed Statements\n   that bind an agent's registered identity, declared capabilities,\n   risk-based assessment and supervised operational status to a hash-\n   chained record of every call the agent makes.  It also defines\n   admission records produced where a call is received, and the linking\n   of records for requests that pass between agents of different\n   operators.  It specifies Registration Policy, offline verification\n   and Bundle requirements.  It does not define an accreditation scheme,\n   a universal risk classification, an identifier scheme or a policy\n   language.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T10:05:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161698, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-thaha-scitt-agent-execution-evidence", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 73, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-thaha-scitt-agent-execution-evidence/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170572/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171234/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171604/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T10:05:28Z", "title": "Agent Execution Evidence: A SCITT Profile for Verifiable Records of AI Agent Runs", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a deterministic cryptographic verification\n   profile for execution traces, delegation chains, and weight-custody\n   manifests generated by autonomous artificial intelligence (AI) agents\n   operating across Model Context Protocol (MCP), Agent-to-Agent (A2A),\n   and enterprise system-of-record boundaries.  The profile binds JSON\n   Canonicalization Scheme (JCS, RFC 8785) pre-image serialization,\n   Ed25519 (RFC 8032) and ECDSA P-256/P-384 signatures, JSON Web Key\n   (JWK, RFC 7517) thumbprint pinning, and fail-closed revocation and\n   freshness checks so that relying parties can independently verify\n   whether an agent action was authorized prior to commit.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-02T21:25:43Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162021, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-durga-agenttrust-trace-verification", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-durga-agenttrust-trace-verification/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171262/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-29T21:25:43Z", "title": "Cryptographic Execution-Trace Verification, JCS Canonicalization, and Fail-Closed Delegation Attestation for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents act on authority that changes while they run:\n   permissions are granted and revoked, running instances are replaced,\n   and some requests are refused.  Activity logs record what a system\n   reports it did, but they do not by themselves show what an agent was\n   allowed to do when it acted, and the operator can edit them.  This\n   document specifies a model for an authority history: an append-only,\n   signed record of who granted what to whom, which actions were\n   admitted, which requests were refused, and which unfinished duties\n   survive when one agent replaces another.  Given independently\n   authenticated owner keys and history identity, a verifier can replay\n   the history and recompute its recorded authorization decisions while\n   trusting neither the agent nor the operator.  The document states\n   what such a verifier can and cannot establish.  Optional profiles\n   cover external anchoring, federated operation, human-attested keys\n   and zero-knowledge disclosure.  An informative appendix describes one\n   encoding.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T00:43:54Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162029, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-rampertab-audit-agent-authority-history", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 84, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-rampertab-audit-agent-authority-history/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171269/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T00:43:55Z", "title": "Replayable Authority Histories for Auditing Automated Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agent discovery depends on sources such as registries, name\n   services, DID methods, and catalogs.  A valid signature on an\n   artifact does not by itself show that a source has not withheld a\n   record, or served inconsistent records to different observers.  This\n   document specifies a source- and format-agnostic procedure for\n   corroborating independent observations of an agent.  It defines claim\n   classification, canonical views, deterministic disagreement findings,\n   multi-vantage equivocation detection, propagation-delay confirmation,\n   and signed Corroboration Records.  The procedure produces verifiable\n   evidence for every sweep, including agreement and insufficient-\n   observation results.  It composes with registry-assisted resolution\n   of known subjects: a registry's renewable subject-to-target binding\n   carries a sequence, and the procedure attributes a source serving a\n   superseded binding rather than comparing it, so propagation lag is\n   never reported as disagreement.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T08:34:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157753, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chandra-agent-registry-corroboration", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chandra-agent-registry-corroboration/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168244/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165647/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171315/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T08:34:25Z", "title": "Multi-Source Corroboration for AI Agent Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the terminology and architecture for AI agent\n   communications protocols over the Internet.  It provides the\n   foundational architectural elements for the Agent Communications\n   Protocol (ACP) - also known as agentproto - a new protocol to be\n   developed in the IETF.  ACP enables communication between user agent\n   clients and AI agents, between AI Agents and other AI Agents, or\n   between AI Agents and tools.  All three of these interaction patterns\n   - and the many use cases therein - require a session-like protocol\n   which handles lifecycle management, including creation, modification,\n   deletion and so on - along with the exchange of input and output.\n   These session-like associations can be extremely long lived - running\n   for hours or even days - and span multiple IP hosts used by the both\n   the principal and by the agent.  They can also be highly intermittent\n   with asynchronous responses.  All of these mean a new type of\n   protocol is needed.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T11:54:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162055, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-manyfolks-agentproto-arch", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-manyfolks-agentproto-arch/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171337/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T11:54:29Z", "title": "Architecture for AI Agent Communications on the Internet", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous agents increasingly act across administrative and security\n   domains using workload identities, OAuth credentials, delegated\n   authorization, attestations, and policy engines.  Existing mechanisms\n   can establish identity, delegation, or access rights, but deployments\n   still lack a common artifact that records which policy and which\n   evidence were evaluated when an operation moved into an authorized,\n   denied, revoked, or expired authority state.\n\n   This document defines an Agent Authority Transition Receipt (AATR), a\n   signed, non-bearer receipt that cryptographically binds an agent\n   operation to the principal, policy, evidence set, decision, audience,\n   validity interval, and predecessor authority state used for that\n   decision.  AATR intentionally separates evidence from authorization\n   and authorization from execution.  Missing or indeterminate required\n   evidence fails closed.  AATR is designed to compose with OAuth,\n   workload identity, remote attestation, transparency services, and\n   agent-specific delegation protocols rather than replace them.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:39:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161024, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-agent-authority-transition-receipts", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-agent-authority-transition-receipts/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168986/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171481/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:39:00Z", "title": "Agent Authority Transition Receipts for Agentic Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Agent Transfer Protocol (AGTP) base specification verifies agent\n   identity at the application layer: the Agent-ID header resolves to an\n   issuer-signed Agent Identity Document, and a per-request Request-\n   Signature proves possession of the key that document names.  That\n   verification runs per request and needs the document.  This document\n   specifies the AGTP Agent Certificate Extension: an optional mechanism\n   that binds the Canonical Agent-ID, the recorded owner, and the\n   Authority-Scope commitment to an X.509 v3 certificate presented\n   during TLS mutual authentication, so that the same verification is\n   made once at the handshake and infrastructure components including\n   Scope-Enforcement Points (SEPs), load balancers, and governance\n   gateways can enforce authority scope without application-layer\n   access, at O(1) cost per request.  The extension also defines\n   session-level revocation propagation via AGTP NOTIFY broadcast and\n   records certificate issuance and revocation in the AGTP transparency\n   log.\n\n   Note: Certain mechanisms described in this document may be subject to\n   pending patent applications by the author.  The licensor is prepared\n   to grant a royalty-free license to implementers consistent with the\n   IETF's IPR framework.  See Section 9.4.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T19:40:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155645, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hood-agtp-agent-cert", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hood-agtp-agent-cert/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161525/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163558/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163828/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165082/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171417/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T19:40:30Z", "title": "AGTP Agent Certificate Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous software agents call APIs, tools and other agents on\n   behalf of people and organisations.  A party receiving such a call\n   today can usually learn only that some bearer credential was\n   presented.  It cannot learn which organisation is accountable for the\n   agent, which model, configuration and tools the agent is running, on\n   whose behalf it is acting, or whether any of that has changed since\n   it was approved.\n\n   This document defines two signed objects and how they travel on HTTP\n   requests.  An Agent Passport binds an agent's public key to its\n   accountable controller and to a digest of its build.  A Delegation\n   Receipt records one step of authority passing from a principal,\n   through a delegator, to an agent; a chain of receipts can only narrow\n   that authority.  Requests carry both, and are signed with the agent's\n   key using HTTP Message Signatures, so a relying party can check,\n   without contacting the issuer per request, who the agent is, what it\n   is running, on whose behalf it acts and what it may do.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T23:23:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162092, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-le-agent-passport", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-le-agent-passport/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171451/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T23:23:13Z", "title": "Agent Passports and Delegation Receipts for Autonomous Software Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic systems can authenticate successfully, hold a valid\n   authorization object, and still be unsafe to execute because required\n   evidence is missing, the software has not reached a required\n   assurance stage, the authority epoch is stale, a prior revocation is\n   unresolved, or a human-approval requirement is unsatisfied.  This\n   document defines the High-Assurance Agent Control Envelope (HAACE), a\n   protocol-neutral profile that binds an exact agent action to identity\n   status, authorization state, policy, assurance stage, evidence state,\n   authority epoch, revocation state, oversight requirements, and an\n   action-time decision.  HAACE requires fail-closed treatment of\n   missing or indeterminate prerequisites, separates authorization from\n   execution, and produces non-bearer decision and execution receipts.\n   The profile composes with OAuth, workload identity, attestation,\n   transparency, and agent-authorization mechanisms rather than\n   replacing them.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T10:23:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161627, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-watts-high-assurance-agent-control-envelope", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-watts-high-assurance-agent-control-envelope/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170390/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171495/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T10:23:02Z", "title": "High-Assurance Agent Control Envelope", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous AI agent ecosystems rely on multi-hop cryptographic\n   delegation chains and signed execution traces (currently built on\n   Ed25519, ECDSA P-256, and RSA-PSS) to enforce least-privilege tool\n   invocations across Model Context Protocol (MCP) and Agent-to-Agent\n   (A2A) boundaries, and to preserve long-term non-repudiation for\n   regulatory audit logs.  This architecture faces two existential\n   vulnerabilities in the Post-Quantum era: (1) \"Harvest-Now, Forge-and-\n   Repudiate-Later\" (HNFRL) attacks, wherein Cryptographically Relevant\n   Quantum Computers (CRQCs) running Shor's algorithm retroactively\n   forge or repudiate multi-year compliance traces (e.g., 7-to-10-year\n   HIPAA, SOX, FDA 21 CFR Part 11, and defense operational logs) and\n   synthesize retroactive human-in-the-loop approvals; and (2) \"Lattice\n   Signature-Bloat Amplification,\" wherein naively substituting 64-byte\n   Ed25519 signatures with 3,309-byte ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) signatures\n   across a 10-hop sub-agent chain inflates per-call authorization\n   headers from 640 bytes to over 46 KB (including public keys),\n   exceeding HTTP/2 header limits and degrading sub-10ms real-time tool\n   routing.\n\n   This document specifies a wire-compatible Post-Quantum Agentic\n   Delegation and Trace Receipt profile (\"PQ-Agentic\").  It introduces a\n   two-tier cryptographic architecture: (a) a Hot-Path Hybrid Delegation\n   Envelope combining classical Ed25519 with ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) via\n   Pre-Verified Session-Grant Digest Chaining (PVSGDC), replacing\n   redundant per-call inline re-transmission of O(N) prior delegators'\n   5,261-byte ML-DSA-65 public keys and signatures with 32-byte SHAKE256\n   digests of out-of-band PEP-verified delegation grants; and (b) a\n   Cold-Path Long-Horizon Archival Receipt anchored with stateless hash-\n   based SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128f (FIPS 205) over RFC 9943 SCITT transparency\n   Merkle roots, guaranteeing 30-year quantum-resistant non-repudiation\n   even under catastrophic lattice cryptanalysis.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T16:16:52Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162111, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-durga-pquip-pqc-agentic-delegation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-durga-pquip-pqc-agentic-delegation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171549/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T16:16:52Z", "title": "Post-Quantum Hybrid Cryptographic Delegation Chains and Merkle-Aggregated Execution Receipts for Autonomous AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines SD-Card, an SD-JWT encoding of AI agent\n   metadata that lets an agent reveal to each verifier only the\n   capabilities, endpoints, and security requirements it is authorized\n   to see, while the verifier can check that they are authentic and\n   issuer-attested.  SD-Card keeps agent metadata confidential; it does\n   not make agents anonymous.  The document specifies the SD-Card data\n   model, issuance, discovery, and key-bound presentation, and defines\n   bindings for the Model Context Protocol (MCP), the Personal Agent\n   Protocol (PAP), and the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T05:54:11Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162118, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nandakumar-agent-sd-card", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 72, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nandakumar-agent-sd-card/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171585/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T05:54:11Z", "title": "Agent SD-Card: Selective Disclosure of Agent Metadata for Discovery and Authentication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   In the Internet of Agents, registration and capability discovery can\n   identify candidate agents that meet basic functional constraints.\n   However, the response performance of candidate services may vary with\n   the underlying model, concurrent requests, and deployment\n   environment.  Static capability declarations or generic computing-\n   load indicators alone may not adequately represent actual agent\n   service performance.  This document discusses the applicability of\n   dynamic information, including Time to First Token (TTFT), output\n   token generation rate, request success rate, concurrency, and\n   availability, to candidate agent selection.  It focuses on the\n   observed entity, data sources, measurement windows, information\n   freshness, and prerequisites for comparing candidates, and outlines\n   an illustrative selection process.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T10:55:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162125, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhu-agent-dynamic-service-selection", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhu-agent-dynamic-service-selection/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171612/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T10:55:14Z", "title": "Dynamic Service Performance Considerations for AI Agent Selection", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When autonomous AI agents execute write operations against enterprise\n   Systems of Record (ERP, MES, WMS, CRM, ITSM), traditional HTTP\n   idempotency keys (RFC 9110) and post-hoc Saga orchestrators cannot\n   guarantee deterministic recovery if a stochastic multi-step plan\n   halts mid-flight or hallucinates downstream parameters.  This\n   document specifies the Pre-Release Transactional Compensation Lease\n   (PTCL) protocol: a fail-closed wire contract requiring an agent\n   runtime to acquire a cryptographically signed recovery reservation\n   lease, prove a bounded rollback window (T_req <= T_granted), and\n   present a Compare-And-Swap (CAS) epoch fencing token prior to\n   committing any state mutation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T15:00:08Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162132, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-durga-agent-tx-compensation-lease", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 4, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-durga-agent-tx-compensation-lease/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171647/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T15:00:08Z", "title": "Pre-Release Transactional Compensation Leases and CAS Fencing Receipts for Autonomous Agents Over Systems of Record", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents increasingly persist memory across sessions and treat that\n   memory, on the next turn, as if it were their own prior experience.\n   This document defines a benchmarking method that measures whether an\n   agent's memory subsystem detects that its persisted memory has been\n   altered, removed, reordered, replayed, or forged at the storage\n   layer, and refuses to serve that memory or reports it before it is\n   served.  The method defines ten storage-level edits, three verdict\n   classes, a detection-point distinction between read time and audit\n   time, three control cases, and a scoring rule.  It is a laboratory\n   method for controlled, reproducible measurement, in the spirit of RFC\n   2544 and RFC 8239, and it is intended as a test method for the\n   \"Protection of Memory Data Integrity\" metric under discussion in the\n   Benchmarking & Performance Measurements (BPM) Working Group.  This\n   revision, which replaces draft-khandelwal-bmwg-agent-memory-\n   integrity-02, adds a tenth edit, in which the subsystem erases a\n   record through its own interface and the adversary restores a copy of\n   the store taken before that erasure, so that the method measures\n   whether an erasure persists against a restored backup; it is the edit\n   that undoes a right-to-erasure request.  The method's results are\n   reported as thirty-one rows on released memory subsystems, eighteen\n   of which claim tamper evidence, with the new edit measured on seven\n   of them, and the machine-readable record in which a result set is\n   published so that an unrelated party can recompute it is described.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T16:39:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162135, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-khandelwal-bpm-agent-memory-integrity", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-khandelwal-bpm-agent-memory-integrity/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171652/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T16:39:15Z", "title": "A Benchmarking Method for the Integrity of AI Agent Memory at Rest", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the Protocol for Agent Communication Exchange\n   (PACE), an agentic dialog management protocol with a binding to Media\n   over QUIC Transport (MOQT).  A dialog is the correlated sequence of\n   interactions between two or more participants over the course of a\n   task.  PACE defines a transport-independent dialog context and\n   control operations to establish, modify, and terminate dialogs and to\n   revoke propagation relationships.  It specifies how that context\n   stays coherent across intermediaries, trust boundaries, modality\n   changes, and interruptions, and how it is carried over MOQT.  PACE\n   does not interpret or define dialog content.  Over MOQT, a dialog can\n   combine text, audio, video, and request/response exchanges in point-\n   to-point, point-to-multipoint, and multi-party topologies.\n   Application-layer agent protocols such as A2A, MCP, and AutoGen keep\n   their own message formats and carry the dialog context through their\n   own extension points.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T20:40:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162138, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nandakumar-agentproto-pace", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 62, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nandakumar-agentproto-pace/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171677/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T20:40:36Z", "title": "PACE: Protocol for Agent Communication Exchange", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "In some environments, a relay agent resides in a network element\nwhich also has access to one or more virtual private networks (VPNs).\nIf a DHCP server wishes to offer service to DHCP clients on those\ndifferent VPNs the DHCP server needs to know information about the\nVPN on which each client resides. The Virtual Subnet Selection sub-\noption of the relay-agent-information option is used by the relay\nagent to tell the DHCP server important information about the VPN\n(called the Virtual Subnet Selection information, or VSS) for every\nDHCP request it passes on to the DHCP server, and is also used to\nproperly forward any DHCP reply that the DHCP server sends back to\nthe relay agent.", "ad": null, "expires": "2008-06-20T07:00:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 24599, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-agent-vpn-id", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-agent-vpn-id/", "rev": "06", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/3318/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/4026/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-07T20:10:02Z", "title": "Virtual Subnet Selection Sub-Option for the Relay Agent Information Option for DHCPv4", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2916}, {"abstract": "   AI agents invoke operations with side effects: writes to resources,\n   creation of durable records, financial transactions, and delegated\n   actions on remote systems.  Existing requirement documents in the\n   agentproto working group establish that such operations require\n   confirmation by the human user, that this confirmation must occur\n   outside of LLM prompt inference, and that in multi-agent delegation\n   the receiving agent must be able to verify that a presented\n   confirmation actually originated from the user and is not fabricated\n   by an intermediate agent.  No document in the current document set\n   defines a protocol, message format, or evidence format that satisfies\n   these requirements.  This document defines the Agent Action\n   Confirmation Protocol (AACP): an action descriptor format derived\n   from OAuth 2.0 Rich Authorization Requests (RFC 9396), a confirmation\n   request format, a signed confirmation evidence format, and verifier-\n   side processing rules with a distinct-outcome failure model.  The\n   design is transport-neutral and profiled here for JSON over HTTP with\n   JWS signatures; a CBOR/COSE mapping is defined.  A requirement\n   traceability matrix maps each mechanism to its source requirement in\n   the agentproto document set.\n\n   This document is informed by, and positions itself relative to, two\n   prior individual submissions that addressed user confirmation of\n   agent actions and have since expired: CHEQ\n   [I-D.rosenberg-aiproto-cheq] and the Agent Operation Authorization\n   framework [I-D.liu-agent-operation-authorization].  The differences\n   are summarized in Section 1.4.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T06:45:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162164, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-agent-action-confirmation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 35, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-agent-action-confirmation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171749/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T06:45:37Z", "title": "Agent Action Confirmation Protocol (AACP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies an agentic AI framework for the management of\n   networks, security, and AI services.  This framework is an Intent-\n   Based System (IBS) to enforce a human-language intent at a target\n   network with the translated policy for networks, security, or AI\n   services.  It consists of a User System, a Cloud System, an Edge\n   System, and a Device.  Target devices in this framework include\n   Network Forwarding Elements (e.g., router and switch), Network\n   Security Functions (e.g., firewall, web filter, and DDoS-attack\n   mitigator), Internet-of-Things (IoT) Devices, Physicak AI Robots,\n   Software-Defined Vehicles (SDV), Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAV), and\n   Urban Air Mobility (UAM).  This document describes the agentic AI\n   framework with its components and interfaces where the components\n   include AI agents and supporting knowledge repositories (i.e.,\n   knowledge base), and also inter-agent interfaces use the Agent-to-\n   Agent (A2A) protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T13:05:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162203, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jeong-nmrg-agentic-ai-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jeong-nmrg-agentic-ai-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171877/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T13:05:10Z", "title": "An Agentic AI Framework for the Management of Networks, Security, and AI Services", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Autonomous and semi-autonomous software agents increasingly take\n   consequential actions across administrative and trust domains.\n   Holding such an action accountable — to a regulator, auditor, or\n   counterparty who does not trust the operator — requires answering\n   several questions, each answerable by an independently-verifiable\n   profile: whether the agent was permitted to act (CAN), which\n   accountable human authorized the specific action (WHO), what outcome\n   the agent system recorded and what is known to have crossed the\n   effect boundary (WHAT), and whether the runtime enforced correctly\n   (AUDIT).\n\n   This document specifies, in Informational terms, how such profiles\n   compose — by a shared action-digest, each verifying independently —\n   and defines a shared conformance-vector suite against which any\n   profile may be tested.  It complements existing audit-architecture\n   and record-format work rather than replacing it, reusing existing\n   signing, transport, and transparency mechanisms.  Its focus is an\n   assurance tier those documents leave open: most agent records today\n   are self-attested by an interested party; this document makes\n   reachable and testable an anchored, third-party-verifiable tier, in\n   which a record is registered to a transparency service (SCITT) so a\n   party who trusts neither the agent nor the operator can verify it.\n   Self-attestation remains a valid baseline; convergence on the\n   disinterested tier — by any conforming profile — is the goal, not a\n   single mandated format.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T15:29:40Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157779, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mih-sato-agent-accountability-composition", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 55, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mih-sato-agent-accountability-composition/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167789/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165738/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171917/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T15:29:40Z", "title": "Agent Accountability: Composition and Conformance", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When an AI agent finds a counterparty, interacts with it, and pays\n   it, the interaction crosses every layer of the stack: a physical tap\n   between two devices, the hardware that holds their keys, a transport\n   session, peer discovery and gossip, and finally an application record\n   of what was agreed, paid, and delivered.  Each layer produces its own\n   evidence, and today nothing joins them.  This document puts the near-\n   field tap at the center and follows one progression, from tap to\n   rendezvous, authenticated session, interaction, payment, and evidence\n   record.  For each layer it states what evidence the layer can\n   contribute, what a verifier has to trust, and how a single verifiable\n   record can cite all of it by digest.  It treats hardware attestation\n   and a transparency log checked by independent witnesses as\n   complementary roots of evidence that answer different questions,\n   describes how light nodes can check one interaction through evidence\n   requests instead of replicating a swarm, and asks which measurable\n   observations can independently corroborate such a record.  It is a\n   problem statement and research agenda for discussion in MAPRG, not a\n   protocol specification.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T15:59:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162235, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gupta-mih-maprg-nfc-agent-evidence", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gupta-mih-maprg-nfc-agent-evidence/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171963/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T15:59:42Z", "title": "From Tap to Record: Layered Evidence for Agent Discovery and Payment over Near-Field Communication", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   An agentic dialog can run for hours or days, go quiet for long\n   stretches, move between hosts, and survive the loss of whatever\n   connection was carrying it.  The Agent Communication Protocol (ACP)\n   architecture names Agentic Dialog Management as a building block and\n   requires an identifier that correlates every message of a dialog and\n   outlives the transport, but leaves the wire format out of scope.\n   This document specifies one such wire format.\n\n   ACP is an application-level session protocol in the style of SIP:\n   every message is a start line, a block of text header fields, a blank\n   line, and an optional body.  A dialog is named by an Agentic Dialog\n   Identifier (ADID) and its durable state is an append-only log of\n   entries, each digesting the one before it into a hash chain.  The\n   chain lets two endpoints that have been apart establish in one\n   exchange which entries they still share, replay only what is missing,\n   and detect divergence.  Periodic checkpoints bound the log, so\n   aligning a dialog does not cost more as it ages.\n\n   ACP carries control and log state only.  Real-time media flows beside\n   it on its own channels and enters the log as a metadata entry or as a\n   content-addressed reference, never as bytes the chain must digest.\n   The semantics are transport-independent; this version binds them to\n   Media over QUIC Transport (MoQ) and to WebTransport over HTTP/3, both\n   of which multiplex the channels ACP needs and migrate across network\n   paths without disturbing the dialog.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T16:07:39Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162236, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-agentproto-acp", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 52, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-agentproto-acp/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171970/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T16:07:39Z", "title": "The Agent Communication Protocol (ACP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When an orchestrator agent delegates sub-tasks across a multi-hop\n   Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) of specialist agents, OAuth 2.0 Token\n   Revocation (RFC 7009) revokes only individual client tokens and\n   provides no mechanism to atomically halt in-flight child, grandchild,\n   or sibling branches or enforce composite time-to-live windows across\n   the entire execution tree.  Furthermore, no protocol binds IEC 62443\n   / Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture (PERA) zone boundaries\n   into agent capability receipts.  This document specifies the\n   Transitive CLEAR (Cryptographic Lease Early-Abort & Revocation) DAG\n   protocol and hard Operational Technology (OT) Level 0-2 physical-\n   safety firewalling.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T17:54:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162252, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-durga-agent-clear-revocation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-durga-agent-clear-revocation/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172032/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T17:54:44Z", "title": "Transitive CLEAR Revocation and Composite Physical-Safety Windows for Multi-Hop Autonomous Agent DAGs", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Long-running autonomous AI agents periodically summarize, compact,\n   and distill episodic interaction logs into persistent vector or graph\n   memory stores.  Lossy context compaction erases the cryptographic\n   provenance of which source documents contributed to a compacted\n   memory state, stripping enterprise sensitivity labels (DLP\n   classifications) and making regulatory data-erasure mandates (GDPR\n   Article 17 Right to Erasure / CCPA purge) impossible to verify.  This\n   document specifies the Ephemeral Context Compaction Receipt (ECCR)\n   protocol: a Merkle inclusion and label-ceiling receipt that preserves\n   deterministic sensitivity inheritance and cryptographic purge\n   verifiability across lossy memory compaction boundaries.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T17:55:12Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162253, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-durga-agent-ephemeral-lineage", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-durga-agent-ephemeral-lineage/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172033/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T17:55:12Z", "title": "Cryptographic Context Compaction Receipts, Sensitivity Label Inheritance, and Verifiable Right-to-Be-Forgotten Lineage for Agent Memory", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document presents a use case and problem statement for the\n   initial discovery of AI agents supporting end-to-end communication\n   services within an organization.  The use case considers AI agents\n   associated with different functions of the Internet protocol suite,\n   including application, transport, Internet-layer, and related\n   network-operation functions.  These agents may share general\n   capabilities but differ in their communication-related operational\n   scopes and responsibilities.  The document examines the minimum\n   public discovery information needed to distinguish such agents within\n   a known organizational discovery scope.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T19:37:47Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162271, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jadoon-dawn-layered-agent-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jadoon-dawn-layered-agent-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172091/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T19:37:47Z", "title": "Discovery of AI Agents for Communication Services: Use Case and Problem Statement", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines use cases and requirements for interconnection\n   of Agent Gateways (AGWs) across separate administrative domains.  An\n   Agent Gateway acts as a trust and protocol boundary for AI agents,\n   mediating agent discovery, intent routing, policy enforcement,\n   context translation, and secure cross-domain collaboration.  This\n   draft does not specify a wire protocol.  Instead, it collects\n   representative deployment scenarios and derives functional,\n   operational, and interoperability requirements to guide future\n   protocol design for AGW-to-AGW interconnection.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T19:39:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162272, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wang-agent-gateway-interconnect-usecase-req", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wang-agent-gateway-interconnect-usecase-req/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172094/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T19:39:34Z", "title": "Agent Gateway Interconnection: Use Cases and Requirements", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents converse with people, other agents and tools.  There is a\n   growing need to capture these conversations for audit, compliance and\n   review.  This document sorts the requirements for capturing agent\n   conversations into those that fit vCon, those better met by plain\n   logging and those that need other solutions.  It then identifies the\n   gaps in vCon and proposes a strawman set of vCon extensions to fill\n   them.  It is intended as a discussion document.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T20:27:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162279, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-petrie-vcon-agent-audit-roadmap", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-petrie-vcon-agent-audit-roadmap/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172116/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T20:27:25Z", "title": "vCon for AI Agent Conversations: Requirements, Gaps and Extension Roadmap", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies records with which a third party can\n   establish, later and without network access, what an automated agent,\n   such as an artificial intelligence (AI) agent, was permitted to do\n   and what it recorded doing.  The person for whom the agent acts, the\n   principal, signs a permission with a passkey; the permission carries\n   limits that a verifier can check mechanically.  The agent signs a\n   receipt for each action with Ed25519 and the Module-Lattice-Based\n   Digital Signature Algorithm (ML-DSA-87).  Receipts are chained and\n   entered in a log whose signed tree heads are time-stamped.  A\n   verifier reports, separately, whether the records are sound and\n   whether each recorded action stayed within its permission.  The\n   content of each record is specified independently of its encoding;\n   one encoding, JSON Web Signature (JWS) over canonical JavaScript\n   Object Notation (JSON), is defined.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T20:39:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161457, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-izmaylov-agent-permission-receipts", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 49, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-izmaylov-agent-permission-receipts/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169946/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170855/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172123/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T20:39:25Z", "title": "Signed Permissions and Action Receipts for Automated Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Network management agents can use an Agent-to-Agent protocol for task\n   exchange.  The protocol alone does not define the application schemas\n   used by independently developed network management agent skills.\n\n   This document standardizes four components for schema-governed\n   network management agent skills: Schema References, per-skill Skill\n   Interface Documents, advertisement and retrieval through an A2A\n   extension and describe_skill, and the A2A mapping for interface\n   version 1.0.  Together, they enable clients to retrieve and validate\n   the application schemas used for skill inputs, outputs, and\n   supporting records.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T21:21:12Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162286, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-huang-ainetops-agent-schema-interfaces", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 44, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-huang-ainetops-agent-schema-interfaces/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172145/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T21:21:12Z", "title": "Schema Interfaces for Network Management Agent Skills", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When one agent hands a task to another, the receiving agent often\n   needs to know who handed it over, on whose behalf, through which\n   earlier agents, and which prior context the work depends on.  It also\n   needs to know that the handoff is fresh and meant for it, and the\n   sender needs a clear answer back.  This document describes a small,\n   transport-neutral handoff context: a signed offer, a signed status\n   reply, and the chain of earlier offers that led to the current one,\n   defined in CDDL for JSON and CBOR, with a lifecycle that maps to\n   Agent2Agent task states.  Each offer names the one before it by hash,\n   so that a dropped or reordered intermediate hop can be detected when\n   the chain is carried.  Prior context is named by digest only; any log\n   or transparency service behind it is opaque.  Appendices show how a\n   SCITT receipt or a Learnlog signed head can be referenced.  The\n   document is offered as input to the agentproto working group's use\n   cases and dialog-context work.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T21:42:22Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161915, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-srivastava-agentproto-handoff-context", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 42, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-srivastava-agentproto-handoff-context/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170995/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172156/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T21:42:22Z", "title": "Signed Handoff Context for Agentic Dialogs", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Secure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) framework uses\n   credentials to sign calls in order to prevent identity impersonation\n   in the telephone network, especially by robocallers.  Its framework\n   also accounts for certain cases of \"legitimate\" impersonation of\n   identity.  This document expands the scope of STIR to include agents\n   acting on behalf of users and enterprises: how agents might best opt-\n   in to self-identify as agents, and how the intent of the originating\n   user or enterprise in delegating to an agent can best be communicated\n   to relying parties.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T21:49:15Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162288, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-peterson-stir-agentic", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-peterson-stir-agentic/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172160/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T21:49:15Z", "title": "Secure Telephone Identity for Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents call other agents and tools across organizational\n   boundaries, but the records of what they did are kept by the\n   operators whose conduct the records describe, and the parties\n   overseeing them learn of problems only afterwards.  TRAIL makes this\n   communication auditable and revocable by parties other than the\n   operator.  An enforcement point outside the agent and its sandbox,\n   the edge, signs each call as a COSE Action Record, which the receiver\n   keeps, and appends it to a tamper-evident trail that witnesses\n   countersign.  Authorities read the trails and publish rules that the\n   edge enforces on every call: require approval, refuse an action, stop\n   an agent, or cap how many agents run across machines.  The edge lets\n   traffic through only while it holds current state from each authority\n   and a recent witness countersignature, so an authority that goes\n   silent, or a trail that nobody is witnessing, stops the agent.  TRAIL\n   defines bindings to HTTP and to Media over QUIC Transport (MOQT) that\n   produce the same evidence; over MOQT, holds reach edges as soon as\n   they are published.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T22:02:28Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162291, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nassar-agentproto-trail", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 60, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nassar-agentproto-trail/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172171/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T22:02:28Z", "title": "TRAIL: Tamper-evident Records for Agent Interactions and Liveness", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Web Bot Auth uses HTTP Message Signatures to let automated HTTP\r\n   clients authenticate their identity to origins via the Signature-\r\n   Agent header field.  When a multi-tenant agent platform signs\r\n   outbound requests with a shared key, all customer workloads on that\r\n   platform share a single cryptographic identity.  Abusive traffic from\r\n   one tenant or workload can degrade the reputation of the entire\r\n   platform and cause origins to block legitimate tenants.\r\n\r\n   This document defines the Signed-Agent-Metadata HTTP header field, an\r\n   RFC 9651 Dictionary that carries optional, pseudonymous per-request\r\n   metadata (product, project, and session).  By signing Signed-Agent-\r\n   Metadata inside the Web Bot Auth signature, a platform enables\r\n   verifiers to evaluate traffic, apply rate limits, and isolate\r\n   reputation at a narrower scope than the signing origin.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T22:35:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162297, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-vilgalys-webbotauth-signed-agent-metadata", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-vilgalys-webbotauth-signed-agent-metadata/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172184/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T22:35:18Z", "title": "The Signed-Agent-Metadata HTTP Header Field for Web Bot Auth", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agent definitions are commonly coupled to a particular framework,\n   model provider, tool environment, or execution service.  This\n   coupling makes it difficult for an independently operated runtime to\n   determine whether it can interpret, place, authorize, and execute an\n   agent workload.\n\n   This document describes an architecture for portable AI agent\n   workloads across heterogeneous Internet runtimes.  It separates\n   authoring, packaging and distribution, and execution; identifies the\n   information a workload needs to declare at that boundary; and\n   distinguishes representation portability, execution portability, and\n   placement portability.  It also identifies research questions around\n   capability matching, workload placement, dependency distribution,\n   identity, authorization, state, and migration.\n\n   This document is architectural and research-oriented.  It does not\n   define a package wire format, universal agent language, runtime API,\n   discovery service, or new transport protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T23:02:22Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162300, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-raskar-portable-agent-runtime-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-raskar-portable-agent-runtime-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172206/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T23:02:22Z", "title": "Architecture for Portable AI Agent Workloads Across Internet Runtimes", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agent definitions are commonly coupled to a particular framework,\n   model provider, tool environment, or execution service.  This\n   coupling makes it difficult for an independently operated runtime to\n   determine whether it can interpret, place, authorize, and execute an\n   agent workload.\n\n   This document describes an architecture for portable AI agent\n   workloads across heterogeneous Internet runtimes.  It separates\n   authoring, packaging and distribution, and execution; identifies the\n   information a workload needs to declare at that boundary; and\n   distinguishes representation portability, execution portability, and\n   placement portability.  It also identifies research questions around\n   capability matching, workload placement, dependency distribution,\n   identity, authorization, state, and migration.\n\n   This document is architectural and research-oriented.  It does not\n   define a package wire format, universal agent language, runtime API,\n   discovery service, or new transport protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T23:17:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162305, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-raskar-net4ai-agent-portability", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-raskar-net4ai-agent-portability/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172225/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T23:17:10Z", "title": "Architecture for Portable AI Agent Workloads Across Internet Runtimes", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents that use large language models increasingly perform\n   consequential tasks on behalf of humans and other AI agents.\n   Demonstrating that recorded AI agent behavior truthfully represents\n   actual behavior is essential for accountability, compliance, and\n   human oversight.  To achieve that goal, this document defines an\n   extension to the Virtualized Conversation (vCon) object format,\n   allowing a vCon to capture a conversation between a human and an AI\n   agent, or between AI Agents.  The vCon core format can already\n   capture the back and forth messages (including text and voice) sent\n   by AI agents, but additional extensions are required to fully\n   reproduce the AI agent trajectory, such as agent metadata, message\n   exchanges, tool invocations, reasoning traces, system events, file\n   attribution, etc.  This document defines those extensions in both\n   descriptive language and formal CDDL definitions.  This draft is a\n   replacement for the former Verifiable Agent Conversation Records\n   specification, which was a standalone format and not an extension of\n   vCon.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T23:29:38Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162309, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-birkholz-vcon-ai-agent-extension", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 67, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-birkholz-vcon-ai-agent-extension/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172239/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-03T00:02:13Z", "title": "An AI Agent Extension for Virtualized Conversations (vCon)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Agentic Session Initiation Protocol (A-SIP) is used for the\n   establishment and lifecycle maintenance of agentic dialogs.  It also\n   enables and content exchange between a principal and an AI Agent over\n   the Internet.  A-SIP can be used between a user agent and AI Agent,\n   between AI Agents, or between an AI Agent and a tool with agentic-\n   like properties.  A-SIP is built ontop of HTTP/3, web transport and\n   Media Over Quic Transport (MOQT), leveraging a modern, public-cloud\n   friendly protocol stack.  Its usage of MOQT is optional, needed only\n   when real-time media is exchanged.  This allows asynchronous agent\n   interactions to be done entirely using HTTP/3, so simple cases remain\n   simple.  Despite the name, A-SIP has no technical relationship with\n   SIP (RFC3261).  This document is not yet a fully fleshed out\n   protocol, and is meant to solicit feedback on this general direction\n   of work.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T23:38:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162320, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-rosenberg-agentproto-asip", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-rosenberg-agentproto-asip/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172257/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T23:38:42Z", "title": "The Agentic Session Initiation Protocol (A-SIP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Portable AI agent packages can contain executable or interpretable\n   behavior, prompts, workflow graphs, tool schemas, dependencies, and\n   declarations of execution requirements.  Such packages may be\n   authored, transformed, repackaged, scanned, approved, published,\n   superseded, and withdrawn by different parties before they are\n   executed by an independent runtime.\n\n   This document describes a SCITT application profile for transparent\n   provenance of portable AI agent packages.  It maps the package\n   lifecycle to the SCITT architecture, identifies an immutable package\n   digest as the artifact binding point, and defines classes of supply-\n   chain statements that can be made about a package without defining a\n   new statement payload format.  Examples include authorship, build and\n   transformation, execution-profile conformance, security assessment,\n   publication, approval, supersession, and withdrawal statements.\n\n   This document does not define a portable-agent package format, agent\n   execution protocol, runtime authorization model, or per-action\n   execution receipt.  It complements SCITT profiles that record agent\n   actions or sessions by focusing on the provenance of the agent\n   package before and across deployment.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T23:43:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162323, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-raskar-scitt-agent-provenance", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-raskar-scitt-agent-provenance/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172265/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T23:43:37Z", "title": "SCITT Profile for Portable AI Agent Package Provenance", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The DHCP Relay Agent Assignment Notification (RAAN) option is sent\n   from a DHCP server to a DHCP relay agent to inform the relay agent of\n   IPv6 addresses that have been assigned or IPv6 prefixes that have\n   been delegated to DHCP clients.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2017-10-03T21:14:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31987, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-dhc-dhcpv6-agentopt-delegate", "note": "", "notify": "Tomek Mrugalski <tomasz.mrugalski@gmail.com>", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-dhc-dhcpv6-agentopt-delegate/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/tomasz.mrugalski@gmail.com/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/12267/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/16886/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/85873/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-06T20:35:17Z", "title": "DHCPv6 Relay Agent Assignment Notification (RAAN) Option", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2553}]}